window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("node-ts-for-ai", {"topics":[{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/typescript-strict-mode-and-advanced-types","sources":["Official docs: TypeScript Handbook (tsconfig reference, Narrowing, Conditional and Mapped Types)"],"why":"A Node AI service is mostly glue between things that have no static shape: JSON from a model, tool-call\narguments the model invented, server-sent events that come in four or five variants, SDK response\nobjects with deeply optional fields (in OpenAI's Chat Completions shape, `choices[0]?.message?.content`\nis legitimately `null` when the model returned a tool call instead of text). JavaScript lets you read any property of anything, so the\nnatural failure mode is `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined` thrown halfway through a\nrequest, after tokens have been billed and partially streamed to the user.\n\nThe naive answer is \"write tests\". Tests exercise the paths you thought of; a type checker exercises\nevery call site of every function on every change, including the provider SDK upgrade that silently\nmade a field optional. The second naive answer is \"use TypeScript, but leave the defaults\": without\n`strict`, untyped parameters become `any`, `null` is a member of every type, and `JSON.parse` returns\n`any` that flows unchecked into your domain objects. That TypeScript catches almost nothing, and worse,\nit creates a false sense that the shapes are checked. Strict mode plus the advanced type features exist\nto make the compiler say *no* at the exact places where a dynamic language would have said *maybe*:\nnull handling, variant handling, and the boundary where untrusted data becomes typed data.","how":"### What `strict` actually switches on\n`\"strict\": true` is an umbrella for a family of flags, each closing a specific hole:\n\n- **`noImplicitAny`** — an untyped parameter or variable is an error rather than silently `any`.\n  Because `any` is viral: one untyped parameter turns everything derived from it into `any`.\n- **`strictNullChecks`** — `null` and `undefined` are their own types, not members of every type. A\n  `string | null` must be narrowed before `.trim()`. This is the flag that turns \"Cannot read\n  properties of undefined\" from a runtime surprise into a compile error.\n- **`strictFunctionTypes`** — parameters of function-typed values are checked contravariantly. A handler\n  typed to accept `TextEvent` cannot be passed where a handler of `StreamEvent` is expected, because it\n  would receive events it cannot handle. Parameters declared with method syntax (`onEvent(e: T): void`\n  in an interface) stay bivariant for compatibility, so prefer property syntax (`onEvent: (e: T) => void`)\n  for callback contracts.\n- **`strictPropertyInitialization`**, **`strictBindCallApply`**, **`noImplicitThis`**, **`alwaysStrict`**\n  — class fields must be initialised at declaration or in the constructor, `.call/.apply` are type-checked, `this` must have a\n  known type, and `\"use strict\"` is emitted.\n- **`useUnknownInCatchVariables`** — `catch (e)` makes `e: unknown`, so you must narrow before reading\n  `e.message`. Anything can be thrown in JS, including strings and `undefined`.\n\nNew TypeScript releases occasionally add flags to the `strict` family (5.6 added\n`strictBuiltinIteratorReturn`), so upgrading the compiler can surface new errors; that is the point.\n\nThree flags are *not* in `strict` and are worth adding: `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` (`arr[i]` and\n`record[key]` are `T | undefined`, which is the truth — `choices[0]` can be missing),\n`exactOptionalPropertyTypes` (an optional property may be absent but may not be explicitly\n`undefined`; this matters because `{ ...defaults, ...overrides }` with `temperature: undefined`\n*overwrites* the default, and `\"temperature\" in opts` is true for a present-but-undefined key), and\n`noFallthroughCasesInSwitch`.\n\n### How narrowing works\nStrictness is livable only because the checker does *control-flow analysis*: it tracks, per code path,\nwhat a variable can be. After `if (x === null) return;` the type of `x` below is `T`, not `T | null`.\nNarrowing comes from `typeof x === \"string\"`, `x instanceof Error`, `\"tool_calls\" in msg`, equality on\na literal field (`ev.type === \"done\"`), truthiness, and two user-written forms: a type predicate\n(`function isToolCall(x: unknown): x is ToolCall`) narrows in the caller's `if`, and an assertion\nfunction (`function assertDefined<T>(x: T): asserts x is NonNullable<T>`) narrows everything after the\ncall or throws. Both are trusted blindly: if the body of a type guard is wrong, the compiler believes\nthe lie, so keep guards tiny or generate them from a schema.\n\n### `unknown` versus `any`\n`any` switches the checker off for that value. `unknown` is the honest top type: you can hold it, but\nyou cannot call, index or dereference it until you narrow it with `typeof`, `instanceof`, a user-defined\ntype guard (`function isToolCall(x: unknown): x is ToolCall`), or a runtime validator. The rule that\nfollows: wrap `JSON.parse` so it returns `unknown`, and make every boundary (HTTP body, model output,\nenv var, webhook) go through a parse step that produces a typed value or throws. Types inside the\nboundary can then be trusted *because* nothing crosses it unvalidated.\n\n### Discriminated unions and exhaustiveness\nModel a stream as a union of object types sharing a literal `type` field:\n`{ type: \"text_delta\"; text: string } | { type: \"tool_call\"; name: string; args: unknown } |\n{ type: \"done\"; usage: Usage }`. A `switch (ev.type)` narrows `ev` in each branch, and a `default`\nbranch that assigns `ev` to a variable of type `never` fails to compile the day a provider adds a new\nvariant. This is why unions beat class hierarchies for protocol data: the compiler enumerates the\ncall sites you must update.\n\n### Generics with constraints\n`function defineTool<A, R>(spec: { parse: (x: unknown) => A; run: (args: A) => Promise<R> })` ties the\nargument parser to the handler's parameter type, so a handler cannot be registered with a parser that\nproduces a different shape. `extends` constrains what a type parameter may be (`K extends keyof T`),\nand inference from arguments means callers rarely write the type parameters by hand.\n\n### Conditional, mapped and template-literal types\nMapped types transform every property (`Partial<T>`, `Readonly<T>`, `Record<K, V>`, or a custom\n`{ [P in keyof T]: T[P] | null }`). Conditional types branch on a type\n(`T extends Promise<infer U> ? U : T` is a simplified `Awaited`; the built-in one also recurses and\nunwraps any thenable; `ReturnType` and `Parameters` are built the same way with `infer`). Template-literal types build string unions\n(`` `${Provider}:${Model}` ``). They are for library and contract code — typing an SDK wrapper or a\ntool registry — not for everyday application logic, where they cost readability.\n\n### Branded types\nTypeScript is structural: any `string` fits any `string` parameter, so an unvalidated user string and\na sanitized SQL fragment, or a token count and a dollar amount, are interchangeable. A brand adds a\nphantom property that only one function can produce: `type ValidatedPrompt = string & { readonly\n__brand: \"ValidatedPrompt\" }`. Only `validatePrompt(s: string): ValidatedPrompt` returns one (via a\nsingle internal cast), so `callModel(p: ValidatedPrompt)` cannot be handed raw input by mistake. The\nbrand costs nothing at runtime because it is erased; it encodes \"this check already happened\" in the\ntype, which is the same idea as parsing at the boundary.\n\n### `as const` and `satisfies`\n`as const` makes a literal deeply readonly and keeps literal types (`\"gpt-4o\"` instead of `string`).\n`satisfies` checks a value against a type *without* widening it, so a model table can be validated\nas `Record<string, ModelSpec>` while `keyof typeof table` still yields the literal model names. A plain\nannotation would widen the keys to `string` and lose that.\n\n### What the compiler cannot do\n`tsc` emits JavaScript with every type removed; no check runs at runtime. A strict program that casts\n`JSON.parse(body) as Request` is exactly as unsafe as untyped JS. Strictness pays off only when paired\nwith runtime validation at the edges, which is the subject of the schema-validation lesson.","example":"A typed stream handler, an exhaustiveness check, a model table that keeps literal keys, and a branded\ntype that makes \"validated\" visible to the compiler. Compile with\n`\"strict\": true` and `\"noUncheckedIndexedAccess\": true` in `tsconfig.json`.\n\n```ts\ntype Usage = { inputTokens: number; outputTokens: number };\ntype StreamEvent =\n  | { type: \"text_delta\"; text: string }\n  | { type: \"tool_call\"; name: string; args: unknown }\n  | { type: \"done\"; usage: Usage };\n\nconst models = {\n  fast: { id: \"small-model\", maxOutput: 4096 },\n  smart: { id: \"large-model\", maxOutput: 16384 },\n} as const satisfies Record<string, { id: string; maxOutput: number }>;\ntype ModelKey = keyof typeof models;            // \"fast\" | \"smart\", not string\n\nfunction parseJson(text: string): unknown {      // never let `any` escape JSON.parse\n  return JSON.parse(text);\n}\n\nfunction handle(ev: StreamEvent, out: string[], tools: Map<string, (a: unknown) => void>) {\n  switch (ev.type) {\n    case \"text_delta\": out.push(ev.text); break;          // ev narrowed: .text exists\n    case \"tool_call\": {\n      const fn = tools.get(ev.name);                        // Map.get is T | undefined\n      if (!fn) throw new Error(`unknown tool ${ev.name}`);\n      fn(ev.args); break;\n    }\n    case \"done\": console.log(ev.usage.outputTokens); break;\n    default: { const never: never = ev; return never; }   // compile error if a variant is added\n  }\n}\n\nfunction pick(key: ModelKey) { return models[key].id; }    // models[\"typo\"] is a compile error\n\ntype ValidatedPrompt = string & { readonly __brand: \"ValidatedPrompt\" };\nfunction validatePrompt(s: string): ValidatedPrompt {\n  if (!s.trim() || s.length > 32_000) throw new Error(\"bad prompt\");\n  return s as ValidatedPrompt;                              // the one sanctioned cast\n}\ndeclare function callModel(p: ValidatedPrompt): Promise<string>;\n// callModel(rawInput) fails to compile; callModel(validatePrompt(rawInput)) compiles\nconst first = [\"a\", \"b\"][0];                                // string | undefined under noUncheckedIndexedAccess\ntry { parseJson(\"{\"); } catch (e) {                        // e: unknown\n  if (e instanceof SyntaxError) console.error(e.message);\n}\n```","tradeoffs":["Strict mode front-loads work: every `null` must be handled explicitly, which costs time on a fresh codebase but pays back the first time a provider makes a field optional; it flips against you only on throwaway scripts where no second reader exists.","Advanced types (conditional, mapped, template-literal) make contracts precise but raise compile times and the reading cost for teammates; they pay off when many call sites consume one contract (an SDK wrapper, a tool registry) and cost more than they save when a type has one or two users.","`unknown` at every boundary forces a parse step, which is extra code and a small runtime cost, but the alternative is trusting casts; the trade only flips for data your own process produced and never serialized, where the compiler already knows the shape.","Branded types prevent mixing up same-shaped values (raw vs validated input, tokens vs dollars), but every construction site needs the brand's factory; worth it where a mix-up is a security or billing bug, noise where it is merely a naming issue.","Discriminated unions give exhaustiveness checks, while class hierarchies give polymorphic dispatch; unions win for protocol data that crosses the wire, classes win when behaviour outweighs shape.","Gradual adoption (`strict` off, flags turned on one by one) keeps a migration shippable but leaves the codebase in a state where the types lie; set a deadline or the migration never finishes."],"pitfalls":["Casting with `as` to silence an error (`JSON.parse(x) as Response`); the symptom is a runtime `TypeError` on a field the type said was present, usually after an upstream API change.","Leaving `strict: false` and assuming the red squiggles mean the code is checked; the symptom is a codebase full of implicit `any` where renaming a field breaks nothing at compile time and everything at runtime.","Forgetting the `never` default branch in a `switch` over a union; the symptom is a new event type silently dropped on the floor, often noticed as \"the stream ends early for tool calls\".","Annotating instead of using `satisfies`, so a config map loses its literal keys; the symptom is `keyof typeof config` becoming `string` and typos in model names compiling cleanly.","Treating `catch (e: any)` as harmless; the symptom is `e.message` being `undefined` because a library threw a string or a non-Error object, which then hides the real failure in logs.","Writing a type guard or assertion function whose body checks less than its signature claims (`x is ToolCall` after only checking `typeof x === \"object\"`); the symptom is a crash on a field the guard \"proved\" existed, with no cast anywhere to grep for."],"when":"Turn on `strict` on day one of any Node AI service and add `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`; the cost of\nretrofitting grows with every file. Reach for discriminated unions whenever data has variants (stream\nevents, tool results, provider error shapes) and for constrained generics when you build a registry or\nSDK wrapper that must tie an input type to a handler. Use conditional and mapped types only when a plain\ntype would have to be repeated or kept in sync by hand. Measure success by the share of `any` and `as`\nin the codebase (lint for both with `@typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any` and\n`consistent-type-assertions`) and by how often runtime `TypeError`s reach production. In interviews,\nbeing able to explain *why* `strictNullChecks` matters, what `unknown` buys over `any`, and how\nexhaustiveness checking works is the expected depth; writing a mapped type from memory is not.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Models provider stream events and tool contracts as discriminated unions, keeps `JSON.parse` behind an `unknown`-returning wrapper, and fixes compile errors instead of casting past them.","architect":"Sets the compiler baseline (`strict`, index-access checks, lint bans on `any`) as a repository standard and decides which shared packages own the cross-service type contracts.","fde":"Reads a customer's `tsconfig.json` first when debugging \"random undefined\" incidents, and frames turning on `strict` as the cheapest reliability fix available in their stack."},"check":{"q":"A teammate argues that since TypeScript types are erased at runtime, enabling `strict` is\ncosmetic and runtime validation with a schema library is all that matters. How do you respond?","a":"Both are needed because they catch different failures. Runtime validation guards the boundary:\nit turns untrusted JSON into a value you can trust, but it only runs where someone remembered to call\nit. Strict typing guards everything inside the boundary: once a value is typed, every call site is\nchecked on every change, including the SDK upgrade that made a field nullable or the new stream\nvariant nobody handled. Without `strict`, a validated value can still be dereferenced as `any` two\nfunctions later. The right design is `unknown` at the edge, a schema parse that produces a typed value,\nand strict checking everywhere after that, so a mismatch surfaces either at compile time or at the\nboundary, never mid-request."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is `unknown` preferred over `any` for the result of `JSON.parse`?","options":["`unknown` is faster because the engine skips property lookups","`unknown` cannot be used until narrowed, so the compiler forces a check before the value spreads through typed code","`any` cannot hold `null`, so parsed JSON with nulls would fail","`unknown` automatically validates the JSON against an interface"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`any` disables checking and propagates silently through every assignment. `unknown` holds the value but refuses every operation until a type guard or validator narrows it, which is exactly the discipline a boundary needs. Neither type performs any runtime validation."},{"q":"What does a `default: const never: never = ev` branch in a switch over a discriminated union achieve?","options":["It throws at runtime if an unexpected event arrives","It tells the bundler the branch is dead code and removes it","It makes the code fail to compile when a new union member is added but not handled","It converts the union into an enum for faster dispatch"],"answer":2,"explanation":"If every variant is handled, `ev` has type `never` in the default branch and the assignment type-checks. Add a variant without a case and `ev` is no longer `never`, so the compiler reports every switch that needs updating. It provides no runtime behaviour on its own."},{"q":"Why use `satisfies` instead of a type annotation for a constant config table?","options":["`satisfies` performs runtime validation of the object","Annotations are not allowed on `const` declarations","`satisfies` checks the value against the type while keeping its literal keys and values, so `keyof typeof table` stays precise","`satisfies` makes the object deeply immutable"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An annotation widens the value to the declared type, turning literal keys into `string`. `satisfies` only verifies compatibility, so the inferred type keeps `\"fast\" | \"smart\"` as keys and the compiler can reject typos in model names. Immutability comes from `as const`, not `satisfies`."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/event-loop-and-microtask-queue","sources":["Official docs: Node.js — The Node.js Event Loop, Timers, and process.nextTick()","Official docs: Node.js — perf_hooks (monitorEventLoopDelay)"],"why":"A service that calls LLM APIs spends almost all of its wall-clock time waiting: a request to a model\nmight take 2–30 seconds, during which the CPU does nothing useful for that request. The thread-per-\nrequest model (one OS thread blocked per outstanding call) spends a stack and a kernel thread on every\nidle wait; that works at hundreds of concurrent calls, but memory and scheduling overhead grow with the\nnumber of *waiting* requests rather than with the work actually being done. Node took the\nopposite bet: one JavaScript thread, non-blocking I/O everywhere, and a loop that runs the callback for\nwhichever operation just completed. A pending HTTP response costs a few kilobytes of heap, not a thread.\n\nThe price of that bet is that the single thread is shared by *everything*. There is no scheduler to\npreempt a long computation; a callback runs until it returns. If a request handler spends 300 ms\nsynchronously tokenizing a 2 MB document or `JSON.parse`-ing a huge tool result, no other callback\nruns for 300 ms: streamed tokens stop flowing to every other client, health checks time out, timers\nfire late. The naive mental model, \"async code runs in parallel\", fails here; only the *waiting* is\nconcurrent, the JavaScript is strictly sequential. Understanding the loop's phases and the microtask\nqueues is what lets you reason about ordering (why a promise callback runs before a `setTimeout(0)`),\nabout starvation (a recursive `nextTick` that never lets I/O run), and about when to move work off the\nthread entirely.","how":"### One thread, a loop, and libuv\nNode embeds V8 for JavaScript and libuv for the event loop and asynchronous I/O. Network sockets are\nwatched with the OS's readiness mechanism (epoll, kqueue, IOCP), so waiting on 10,000 sockets is one\nsystem call, not 10,000 threads. Operations that the OS cannot do asynchronously (most filesystem calls,\nDNS lookups via `getaddrinfo`, some crypto) are run on libuv's small thread pool (four threads by\ndefault, tunable with `UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE`) and their results are posted back to the loop as events.\nYour JavaScript never runs on those threads.\n\n### The phases\nEach iteration (\"tick\") of the loop visits phases in a fixed order, running the callbacks queued for\neach:\n\n1. **timers** — callbacks of `setTimeout`/`setInterval` whose threshold has elapsed. A timer is a\n   *minimum* delay, not a schedule; it runs when the loop gets here.\n2. **pending callbacks** — deferred I/O callbacks from the previous iteration (some system errors).\n   libuv then runs internal idle/prepare hooks; nothing in user code schedules there.\n3. **poll** — the heart of the loop: run the callbacks for completed I/O (socket data, HTTP responses,\n   file reads), then, if there is nothing else to do, block in the OS waiting for more. The block has a\n   timeout derived from the nearest timer, and it does not block at all if `setImmediate` callbacks are\n   queued. This is where an idle Node process spends its time: asleep in `epoll_wait`, using no CPU.\n4. **check** — `setImmediate` callbacks. It exists so that code can say \"run right after the I/O I just\n   handled\" without competing with timers.\n5. **close callbacks** — `'close'` events for destroyed sockets and handles.\n\nBetween every single callback, Node drains the two microtask queues (next section); since Node 11 this\nhappens after each individual timer or immediate, not once per phase as in older versions. The loop exits when\nthere are no pending timers, handles or requests, which is why a script \"just ends\" when its work is done.\n\n### Microtasks: `process.nextTick` and promise jobs\nNode keeps two queues that are not phases at all: the `nextTick` queue and the promise microtask queue\n(the ECMAScript \"job queue\", where `.then` callbacks, `await` continuations and `queueMicrotask` land).\nAfter each callback returns, Node drains the `nextTick` queue completely, then the promise queue\ncompletely, repeating until both are empty, *then* moves on. Microtasks queued while draining are run in\nthe same drain. Two consequences:\n\n- Ordering is deterministic: synchronous code first, then every `nextTick`, then every promise\n  continuation, then the next macrotask (timer, I/O or immediate). An `await` on an already-resolved\n  value still yields to the microtask queue, which is enough to let other promise continuations run but\n  *not* enough to let I/O run.\n- Starvation is possible: a function that re-queues itself with `nextTick` or with a resolved promise\n  keeps the drain going forever, and the poll phase never gets a turn. The symptom is a process that is\n  busy but handles no requests. `setImmediate` is the safe way to yield to I/O.\n\nOne trap: the top level of an **ES module** is itself evaluated inside a promise job, so code queued\nthere sees the promise queue drained *before* the `nextTick` queue (`.then` prints before `nextTick`).\nThe \"nextTick first\" rule holds in CommonJS modules and inside ordinary callbacks such as a request\nhandler, which is where it usually matters.\n\n`setTimeout(fn, 0)` versus `setImmediate(fn)` from the main module is non-deterministic (it depends on\nwhether 1 ms has elapsed by the time the timers phase is checked); inside an I/O callback, `setImmediate`\nalways runs first because check comes before the next timers phase.\n\n### What \"blocking the loop\" looks like in an AI service\nEverything in a request handler that is not an `await` on I/O runs to completion on the loop thread:\n`JSON.parse` of a multi-megabyte response, a regex over a long transcript, computing cosine similarity\nacross 100k embeddings in plain JS, synchronous `zlib`/`crypto` calls, or a tight loop building a\nprompt from a large document. While that runs, every open SSE stream in the process freezes, because\nthe `write()` calls that would flush their next tokens are callbacks waiting for the loop. Event-loop\nlag is therefore the first metric to watch: `perf_hooks.monitorEventLoopDelay()` samples how late the\nloop is, and p99 lag above tens of milliseconds means some handler is doing CPU work inline. Its\ncompanion, `performance.eventLoopUtilization()`, reports the fraction of time the loop was busy rather\nthan idle in the poll phase. Read them together: utilization near 1 with lag that rises smoothly means\nthe process is saturated by many short callbacks (scale out), while lag spikes at moderate utilization\nmean a few long callbacks (find and offload them). The remedies, in order: make it async in chunks\n(`await setImmediate`-style yielding), move it to a worker thread, or move it out of the process.\n\n### Why chunk size is a latency budget\nYielding does not make work faster; it bounds how long anyone else waits. If one slice of work takes\n*s* ms of synchronous time, every other callback can be delayed by up to *s* ms per slice, and the\nyielding request itself takes longer in total because other callbacks run between its slices. So pick\nthe slice from the latency you can tolerate: if other streams must never stall more than ~10 ms, a\nslice must be measured to run under 10 ms on the production CPU. Note the yield must be a *macrotask*\n(`setImmediate`): `await Promise.resolve()` or `await` on any already-resolved value only re-enters the\nmicrotask queue, which is drained before the poll phase, so it yields to nothing that matters.","example":"The ordering rules and a lag monitor in one script. Run it as CommonJS (for example compile with `tsc`\nto `module: commonjs`, or run with `tsx` in a package without `\"type\": \"module\"`); as an ES module\nlines 3 and 4 swap, for the reason given above.\n\n```ts\nimport { monitorEventLoopDelay } from \"node:perf_hooks\";\nimport { setImmediate as yieldToLoop } from \"node:timers/promises\";\n\nconsole.log(\"1 sync\");\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(\"5 timeout (timers phase)\"), 0);\nsetImmediate(() => console.log(\"6 immediate (check phase)\"));\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"4 promise microtask\"));\nprocess.nextTick(() => console.log(\"3 nextTick (drained before promises)\"));\nconsole.log(\"2 sync\");\n// CommonJS: 1,2,3,4 then 5,6 (5/6 may swap from the main module; never from an I/O callback).\n// ESM top level: 1,2,4,3 then 5,6, because module evaluation itself runs inside a promise job.\n\n// Event-loop lag: sample the histogram and alert when p99 is high.\nconst h = monitorEventLoopDelay({ resolution: 10 });\nh.enable();\nsetInterval(() => {\n  console.log(`loop lag p99 ${(h.percentile(99) / 1e6).toFixed(1)} ms`);\n  h.reset();\n}, 5_000).unref();\n\n// A CPU-heavy scoring loop made cooperative: yield to I/O every 500 vectors so\n// streaming responses to other clients keep flowing while this request computes.\n// At 1536 dims, 500 vectors is ~768k multiply-adds per slice: measure it, it is\n// the worst-case stall every other client sees. 100k vectors = 200 slices.\nasync function topK(query: Float32Array, vectors: Float32Array[], k: number) {\n  const scores: Array<[number, number]> = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < vectors.length; i++) {\n    let dot = 0;\n    for (let d = 0; d < query.length; d++) dot += query[d]! * vectors[i]![d]!;\n    scores.push([dot, i]);\n    if (i % 500 === 499) await yieldToLoop();   // setImmediate: lets the poll phase run\n  }\n  return scores.sort((a, b) => b[0] - a[0]).slice(0, k);\n}\nvoid topK;\n```","tradeoffs":["One thread with non-blocking I/O gives very cheap concurrency for I/O-bound work like proxying model calls, but gives zero parallelism for CPU work; the moment handlers do real computation the model flips and you need worker threads or separate processes.","Deterministic microtask ordering makes promise-based code predictable and easy to test, but the same rule lets a promise loop starve I/O, which thread-based runtimes would have preempted.","Chunking CPU work with `setImmediate` keeps the process responsive without threads, at the cost of longer total time for that request and code that is harder to read; it wins for occasional medium jobs and loses for sustained heavy compute.","The libuv thread pool hides blocking syscalls for you, but with four threads by default a burst of file reads or DNS lookups queues behind each other; raising `UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE` helps until memory and contention bite."],"pitfalls":["Doing CPU-heavy work inline in a request handler (tokenizing, big `JSON.parse`, similarity search); the symptom is every concurrent stream stalling at once and p99 latency spiking while CPU is at 100% on a single core.","Recursively scheduling with `process.nextTick` or resolved promises to \"spread work out\"; the symptom is a process that is alive and busy yet accepts no new connections, because the poll phase never runs.","Assuming `setTimeout(fn, 100)` fires at 100 ms; the symptom is timers drifting by seconds under load, because they only fire when the timers phase is reached.","Blocking the loop with synchronous APIs (`fs.readFileSync`, `child_process.execSync`, sync `zlib`) in a hot path because \"it is just a small file\"; the symptom appears only under concurrency, when the small stall multiplies across every in-flight request.","Not watching event-loop lag at all; the symptom is latency incidents with no CPU, memory or upstream signal to explain them, because the delay is inside the process.","\"Yielding\" with `await Promise.resolve()` or `await null` inside a hot loop; the symptom is that loop lag is unchanged, because microtasks are drained before the loop ever reaches poll."],"when":"You do not opt into the event loop; you design around it. Reach for plain async I/O for everything that\nwaits on a network or a model, and expect it to scale to thousands of concurrent requests per process.\nThe decision point is CPU: if a code path does more than a few milliseconds of synchronous work per\nrequest, chunk it with `setImmediate` yielding when it is occasional, or move it to a worker thread or a\nseparate service when it is sustained. Measure with `monitorEventLoopDelay` in production and put a\np99 lag alert next to your latency SLO; add `--cpu-prof` or `clinic`-style profiling when lag is high.\nIn interviews, expect to be asked to predict the output order of a snippet mixing `setTimeout`,\n`setImmediate`, `nextTick` and promises, and to explain why a single slow handler hurts unrelated users.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Keeps request handlers free of synchronous heavy work, yields or offloads CPU-bound steps, and ships an event-loop-lag gauge with every service so stalls are visible.","architect":"Decides which workloads belong on the loop (model proxying, streaming, orchestration) and which must live in workers or separate services (parsing, embedding math, batch jobs).","fde":"Diagnoses \"the assistant freezes for everyone at once\" at a customer by checking loop lag before blaming the model provider, and explains why a bigger VM will not fix a single-thread stall."},"check":{"q":"A streaming chat gateway shows all active streams pausing for about half a second several times\na minute. CPU is at 100% on one core, the model provider's latency is normal, and memory is flat. What is\nhappening and how would you confirm and fix it?","a":"The pattern points to event-loop blocking: some handler runs roughly 500 ms of synchronous\nJavaScript, and because every stream's next `write()` is a callback queued on the same loop, all streams\nfreeze together. Confirm by sampling `monitorEventLoopDelay` (p99 lag will match the pause) and taking a\nCPU profile to find the hot function; likely candidates are parsing a large tool result, tokenizing\ndocuments for context packing, or a similarity search over embeddings in plain JS. Fix by moving that\nwork to a worker thread (or a separate service) if it is sustained, or by chunking it with `setImmediate`\nyields if it is occasional, and keep a loop-lag alert so regressions are caught before users notice."},"quiz":[{"q":"In what order do these run: a `setTimeout(fn, 0)`, a resolved promise's `.then`, and a `process.nextTick` callback queued from the same synchronous block inside an HTTP request handler?","options":["setTimeout, then nextTick, then the promise callback","promise callback, then nextTick, then setTimeout","nextTick, then the promise callback, then setTimeout","All three run in the order they were queued"],"answer":2,"explanation":"After the synchronous block finishes, Node drains the nextTick queue, then the promise microtask queue, and only then advances the loop to the timers phase where the setTimeout fires. Queue order matters only within a single queue. (At the top level of an ES module the promise callback runs first, because module evaluation is itself a promise job; inside a handler callback the nextTick-first rule holds.)"},{"q":"Why does a loop that re-queues itself with `process.nextTick` prevent the server from accepting connections?","options":["nextTick callbacks run on the libuv thread pool, which fills up","The nextTick queue is drained completely before the loop reaches the poll phase, so I/O callbacks never run","nextTick disables the timers phase until the queue is empty","Each nextTick call opens a file descriptor that is never closed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Microtask queues are drained to empty after every callback, and work queued during the drain is included. A self-re-queuing nextTick is never empty, so the loop never gets to poll for socket events. Using setImmediate instead schedules in the check phase and lets I/O interleave."},{"q":"Which change most directly fixes a handler that stalls all concurrent streams for 300 ms while computing embeddings similarity in JavaScript?","options":["Increase `UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE` so the computation runs on more threads","Wrap the computation in `async` and `await` its result","Move the computation to a worker thread or yield with `setImmediate` between chunks","Replace `setTimeout` calls in the handler with `setImmediate`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Marking a function `async` does not move its synchronous body off the loop, and the libuv pool only serves native operations, not your JavaScript. Only a worker thread gives true parallelism, and chunked yielding at least lets other callbacks run between slices."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/promises-and-async-error-propagation","sources":["Official docs: Node.js — process 'unhandledRejection' event and --unhandled-rejections","Official docs: MDN — Promise, Promise.allSettled, Promise.any, Error cause"],"why":"Callback-style Node passed errors as the first argument of every callback, and the result was error\nhandling that was easy to forget and impossible to compose: a forgotten `if (err)` meant silent\ncorruption, and sequencing five dependent I/O calls meant five levels of nesting with five separate\nerror paths. Promises fixed the composition problem by making the result a value: a chain of `.then`\ncalls has one `.catch` at the end, and a rejection anywhere skips straight to it. `async`/`await`\nthen made the control flow readable again, with `try`/`catch` working across awaits.\n\nWhat promises did not fix, and what still bites AI code specifically, is the *ownership* of a failure.\nAn agent loop makes dozens of concurrent calls: the model, three tool executions, an embedding lookup,\na logging sink. Each returns a promise. If one is started and never awaited, its rejection has no\nhandler; modern Node treats that as fatal and exits the process, taking every other in-flight request\nwith it. If several are awaited through `Promise.all`, the first rejection wins and the others keep\nrunning unobserved. If a low-level `fetch` error is caught and rethrown as `new Error(\"model call\nfailed\")`, the status code and the provider's error body are gone by the time it reaches a log. The\ndiscipline in this lesson exists because, in a system whose failures are dominated by upstream\ntimeouts, rate limits and malformed outputs, the difference between a retryable 429 and a permanent\n400 must survive the trip up the call stack.","how":"### States and chaining\nA promise is pending, then settles exactly once as fulfilled (with a value) or rejected (with a reason,\nconventionally an `Error`). `.then(onFulfilled, onRejected)` returns a *new* promise whose outcome is\nwhatever the handler returns or throws; a returned promise is adopted (flattened). This is why a chain\npropagates: a throw in any step rejects the step's promise, every following `.then` without a rejection\nhandler passes the rejection through unchanged, and the first `.catch` receives it. Handlers always run\nas microtasks, never synchronously, even when the promise is already settled, which keeps ordering\nconsistent (see the event-loop lesson).\n\n### What `async`/`await` desugars to\nAn `async` function always returns a promise. `return x` fulfils it; a thrown exception, including one\nthrown synchronously before the first `await`, rejects it rather than escaping to the caller. `await p`\nsuspends the function, and when `p` rejects, the rejection is re-thrown at the `await` expression, so\nan enclosing `try`/`catch` catches it. This is the single rule that makes propagation work: errors cross\n`await` boundaries as exceptions, so a stack of async functions behaves like a stack of sync ones.\n\nOne subtlety: `return fetchModel()` inside a `try` block does **not** catch a rejection of `fetchModel()`\nbecause the promise is returned before it settles; `return await fetchModel()` does. Outside a `try`,\nthe two are equivalent apart from a stack frame.\n\n### Unhandled rejections\nA rejected promise with no handler attached by the end of the current microtask drain triggers\n`'unhandledRejection'`. Since Node 15 the default mode is `throw`: the rejection is raised as an uncaught\nexception and the process exits (unless an `'unhandledRejection'` listener is installed, which\nsuppresses the throw). The crash output is the original error and its stack when the reason is an\n`Error`; only a non-Error reason (a thrown string, say) is wrapped in an error with code\n`ERR_UNHANDLED_REJECTION`, which then carries no useful stack. The usual cause is a *floating promise*: `logToSink(event)` called\nwithout `await` or `.catch`, or a `.then` with no rejection branch. Fix the cause, not the symptom:\nevery promise must be awaited, returned, passed to a combinator, or given an explicit `.catch` that\ndecides what to do. Lint for it (`@typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises`). Installing a global\n`process.on('unhandledRejection')` handler that only logs turns a crash into silent data loss; it is\nacceptable as a last-resort reporter, not as a strategy.\n\n### The handler-attachment window\n\"Unhandled\" is judged per promise at the moment it rejects, not at the end of the function. That\nmakes a tempting concurrency pattern dangerous:\n\n```\nconst a = callModel(p1);   // both start now: good\nconst b = callTool(t1);\nconst x = await a;         // while suspended here, b rejects -> nobody is listening yet\nconst y = await b;         // too late: the process may already have crashed\n```\n\nIf `b` rejects while the function is still suspended on `a`, `b` has no handler, the\n`'unhandledRejection'` check fires, and in the default mode the process exits; attaching a handler\nlater only triggers the `'rejectionHandled'` event. `await Promise.all([a, b])` (or `allSettled`)\nattaches handlers to both immediately, which is the reason to use a combinator for concurrent work\nrather than staggered awaits.\n\n### Where propagation stops\n`await` carries errors only along promise edges. Four places break the chain:\n\n- **Callbacks scheduled from async code.** A `throw` inside `setTimeout(() => ...)`, an event-emitter\n  listener or a stream `'data'` handler is not inside your `try`; the enclosing async function has\n  already moved on. It becomes an uncaught exception. Wrap the callback-based API in a promise (or use\n  `events.once`, `timers/promises`, `stream/promises`) so the error re-enters the chain.\n- **Emitter `'error'` events.** An `EventEmitter` that emits `'error'` with no listener throws. Streams\n  and SDK clients that expose events need a listener, or use a promise-returning wrapper.\n- **Non-async functions that return promises.** `function call() { validate(x); return fetch(...) }`\n  throws *synchronously* if `validate` fails, so `call().catch(...)` never sees it. Marking the\n  function `async` converts that throw into a rejection, making error handling uniform.\n- **Floating promises**, covered above: no edge means no propagation.\n\n### Combinators and their error semantics\n- `Promise.all(ps)` — fulfils with an array when all fulfil; rejects with the **first** rejection. The\n  remaining promises are not cancelled; they run to completion unobserved. Use it when any failure makes\n  the whole result useless.\n- `Promise.allSettled(ps)` — never rejects; yields `{status, value|reason}` per input. Use it when\n  partial results are useful, e.g. fan-out to several providers or tools where you want every outcome.\n- `Promise.any(ps)` — fulfils with the first fulfilment; rejects only when all reject, with an\n  `AggregateError` whose `.errors` holds each reason. Use it for racing equivalent providers.\n- `Promise.race(ps)` — settles with whichever settles first, success or failure; mostly used to impose a\n  timeout, which `AbortSignal.timeout` does more cleanly because it also stops the work.\n\nBecause combinators do not cancel losers, pair them with an `AbortController` when the losing calls are\nexpensive (every model call is).\n\n### Keeping the cause\nWrapping an error adds context but must not destroy the original. `new Error(\"tool failed\", { cause:\nerr })` (ES2022) keeps the chain; a custom `class ProviderError extends Error` can carry `status`,\n`retryable` and the provider's error body. `catch (e: unknown)` forces narrowing with `instanceof`\nbefore reading fields. Node links async stack frames across `await` (async stack traces), but only when\nthe promise is awaited directly; a `.then` chain hides the originating frame, which is one more reason\nto prefer `await`.","example":"Fan-out to three providers with `allSettled` so every outcome is known, classify each failure with a\nstatus and a retryable flag, preserve the original cause when wrapping, abort the losers once a winner\narrives, and give the fire-and-forget audit call an explicit `.catch` so it can never become an\nunhandled rejection.\n\n```ts\nclass ProviderError extends Error {\n  constructor(msg: string, readonly status: number, readonly retryable: boolean, opts?: { cause?: unknown }) {\n    super(msg, opts); this.name = \"ProviderError\";\n  }\n}\n\nasync function callProvider(name: string, prompt: string, signal: AbortSignal): Promise<string> {\n  try {\n    const res = await fetch(`https://${name}.example/v1/complete`, {\n      method: \"POST\", body: JSON.stringify({ prompt }), signal,\n    });\n    if (!res.ok) {\n      const body = await res.text();\n      throw new ProviderError(`${name} returned ${res.status}: ${body.slice(0, 200)}`,\n        res.status, res.status === 429 || res.status >= 500);\n    }\n    return await res.text();                 // `return await` so the try block sees a rejection\n  } catch (e: unknown) {\n    if (e instanceof ProviderError || signal.aborted) throw e; // classified, or a cancellation: not retryable\n    throw new ProviderError(`${name} transport failure`, 0, true, { cause: e });\n  }\n}\n\nasync function bestEffort(prompt: string) {\n  const ac = new AbortController();\n  const names = [\"alpha\", \"beta\", \"gamma\"];\n  let winner: string | undefined;\n  const calls = names.map((n) => callProvider(n, prompt, ac.signal).then((text) => {\n    winner ??= text; ac.abort(); return text;   // first success aborts the losers mid-flight\n  }));\n  const results = await Promise.allSettled(calls); // losers now settle quickly with AbortError\n  if (winner !== undefined) return winner;\n  const reasons = results.map((r) => (r as PromiseRejectedResult).reason);\n  const retryable = reasons.every((r) => r instanceof ProviderError && r.retryable);\n  throw new AggregateError(reasons, retryable ? \"all providers failed (retryable)\" : \"all providers failed\");\n}\n\n// Fire-and-forget logging is still a promise: attach a handler or the rejection becomes fatal.\nvoid (async () => { /* audit sink */ })().catch((e) => console.error(\"audit sink failed\", e));\n```","tradeoffs":["`Promise.all` is simplest and fastest to fail, but it discards the other outcomes and leaves them running; it is the right choice only when a single failure invalidates the whole batch.","`allSettled` gives complete information at the cost of handling every outcome yourself, which is more code but is what you want for provider fan-out, tool batches and anything partially useful.","Wrapping errors with `cause` keeps diagnostics but makes error objects larger and may carry sensitive provider bodies into logs; redact at the logging boundary rather than by dropping the cause.","Crashing on unhandled rejections loses in-flight requests but surfaces bugs immediately; swallowing them keeps the process up while silently losing work, which is worse in anything that bills tokens.","Prefer `await` over `.then` chains for readable stack traces, accepting that sequential awaits can serialize work you meant to run concurrently; for concurrency, start the promises and await them through one combinator, not one by one, so every rejection has a handler from the start."],"pitfalls":["Forgetting `await` on a call whose result you do not use (logging, metrics, cache writes); the symptom is a process crash whose stack points into a logging or cache helper rather than the request that started it (or `ERR_UNHANDLED_REJECTION` with no stack when a non-Error was thrown), or data that never arrives.","`return promise` inside `try` instead of `return await promise`; the symptom is a `catch` block that \"never runs\" while the error surfaces in the caller.","Awaiting sequentially what could run concurrently (`const a = await x(); const b = await y();`); the symptom is latency equal to the sum of calls instead of the max.","\"Fixing\" that by starting both and awaiting them one after another; the symptom is an intermittent crash on unhandled rejection that only happens when the second call fails while the first is slow.","Throwing inside a `setTimeout` or event-listener callback and expecting the surrounding `try` to catch it; the symptom is an `uncaughtException` crash whose stack has no request-handler frames.","Rethrowing as a fresh `new Error(msg)` without `cause`; the symptom is logs saying \"model call failed\" with no status code, no request id and no stack from the real failure.","Treating `Promise.all` rejection as \"the batch failed\" and retrying everything; the symptom is duplicated tool side effects because the calls that had succeeded are run again."],"when":"Use `async`/`await` with `try`/`catch` as the default for all I/O, and reach for combinators only at\nexplicit fan-out points: `all` for all-or-nothing, `allSettled` for partial results, `any` for racing\nequivalent providers, always paired with an `AbortController` so losers stop consuming resources\n(whether a provider stops generating, and billing, when the client disconnects is vendor-dependent). Define\none error class per boundary (provider, tool, validation) carrying status and retryability, and wrap\nwith `cause`. Enforce `no-floating-promises` in lint and keep the default crash-on-unhandled-rejection\nbehaviour so bugs surface in staging. Measure the rate of unhandled rejections and of errors without a\ncause chain in logs. In interviews, explaining why `return await` differs inside `try`, what `all`\ndoes with the losers, and how an unhandled rejection becomes a crash shows the expected depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes provider and tool clients that classify errors, preserve causes, and never leave a promise floating, and runs with lint rules that catch missing awaits.","architect":"Defines the error taxonomy that crosses service boundaries (retryable vs permanent, provider vs validation) and the fan-out policy for multi-provider calls.","fde":"Traces a customer's \"random crash every few hours\" to an unawaited promise in a webhook handler, and shows how `cause` chains would have pointed at the upstream 429 immediately."},"check":{"q":"An agent service calls three tools concurrently with `Promise.all`. One tool rejects, the handler\ncatches it and retries the whole batch. Users report duplicated emails. Explain the bug and the fix.","a":"`Promise.all` rejects as soon as one promise rejects, but the other two keep running and in this\ncase one of them already sent an email. Retrying the batch re-runs that tool and sends a second email.\nThe fix has three parts: use `Promise.allSettled` so each outcome is known and only the failed tool is\nretried; give side-effecting tools an idempotency key so a retry cannot duplicate the effect; and pass an\n`AbortSignal` so that when the batch is genuinely abandoned, still-running tools are cancelled instead of\ncompleting unobserved. The error should also carry the original cause and a retryable flag so the loop\ncan decide whether a retry is even appropriate."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does `return fetchModel()` inside a `try` block fail to catch a rejection from `fetchModel()`, while `return await fetchModel()` catches it?","options":["`return` exits the try block synchronously before any promise is created","Without `await` the promise is returned still pending, so its rejection happens after the function has already left the try block","`await` converts the rejection into a return value of `undefined`","The two forms are identical; the difference is only in stack trace depth"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`try`/`catch` only sees exceptions thrown while control is inside the block. A returned pending promise settles later, outside the block, so the rejection propagates to the caller. `return await` suspends inside the block and re-throws there. Outside a try, the forms behave the same."},{"q":"What happens to the other promises when `Promise.all` rejects because one input rejected?","options":["They are cancelled and their rejections are suppressed","They are cancelled and their pending work is rolled back","They keep running to completion and their results are ignored","They are converted into unhandled rejections and crash the process"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Promises cannot be cancelled by the combinator; it only stops listening. The remaining work, including paid model calls and side effects, continues. Cancellation must be done cooperatively through an `AbortSignal` passed into each call."},{"q":"A service crashes on an unhandled promise rejection even though every `await` is inside a `try`. What is the most likely cause?","options":["A `catch` block rethrew the error","A promise was created without being awaited, returned, or given a `.catch`, and it rejected","`Promise.allSettled` rejected because all inputs rejected","The error object was not an instance of `Error`"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A rejection with no handler attached by the end of the microtask drain is unhandled, and Node's default is to raise it as a fatal exception. Floating promises from fire-and-forget calls are the usual source. `allSettled` never rejects, and a non-Error reason only changes how the crash is reported (`ERR_UNHANDLED_REJECTION` without a stack), not whether it happens."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure","sources":["Official docs: Node.js — Stream (Backpressuring in Streams, stream.pipeline, Web Streams API)","Official docs: WHATWG Streams Standard"],"why":"An LLM answer of 800 tokens takes on the order of ten seconds to generate at typical decode rates of\ntens of tokens per second. Waiting for the whole thing and then\nsending it makes the user stare at a blank screen for the full duration; streaming each token as it is\nproduced turns the same latency into a visibly \"typing\" response with time-to-first-token measured in\nhundreds of milliseconds. The same shape appears everywhere in AI systems: a 300 MB PDF corpus to chunk\nand embed, a JSONL export of a million rows to score, audio frames to transcribe. Loading any of these\ninto memory first means memory proportional to input size, and a single large input takes the process\ndown.\n\nStreaming alone is not enough, though, because the two ends rarely run at the same speed. A model API\npushes tokens faster than a phone on a poor connection can acknowledge them; a file reader can produce\n64 KiB chunks far faster than an embedding call can consume them. Without a rule for the mismatch, the\nfast side's output piles up in buffers in your process. Size it honestly: a chat answer is small (800\ntokens is a few KB of text, perhaps tens of KB once each token is wrapped in an SSE JSON event), so\nfor text chat the unbounded buffer is a latent bug more than an outage. Bulk payloads are different.\nRaw 24 kHz 16-bit mono speech is 48,000 bytes per second; a 60-second synthesized answer is about\n2.9 MB, and a thousand stalled mobile clients is about 2.9 GB of heap, and the process dies of memory\npressure while every request looks healthy. The same arithmetic applies to file downloads, exports and\nproxied uploads. The naive fix, \"make the consumer faster\", does not exist\nfor a network you do not control. Backpressure is the actual fix: the consumer's capacity propagates\nbackwards to the producer, which stops reading from its own source until there is room. Node's stream\nabstractions exist to make that propagation automatic, and to make errors and early termination\npropagate along the same path.","how":"### The classic `node:stream` types\n- **Readable** — a source you pull from (`fs.createReadStream`, an HTTP request body, a socket).\n- **Writable** — a sink you push into (an HTTP response, a file, a socket).\n- **Duplex** — both, independently (a TCP socket).\n- **Transform** — a Duplex where output is computed from input (gzip, a line splitter, an SSE parser).\n\nEach has an internal buffer bounded by `highWaterMark` (bytes for byte streams, item count in\n`objectMode`, where the default is 16 objects; the byte default is tens of KiB and was raised in recent\nNode versions, so set it explicitly when it matters). It is a *threshold*, not a hard cap: the stream\nwill accept a write beyond it, but reports that it would rather you stopped. Worst-case memory per\nstream is therefore roughly the sum of the `highWaterMark`s along the chain plus one chunk per stage,\nwhich is what makes per-process memory budgetable: that figure times maximum concurrency.\n\n### The backpressure protocol\n`writable.write(chunk)` returns `false` when the internal buffer has reached `highWaterMark`. The\ncontract is that the producer stops writing and waits for the `'drain'` event, then resumes. On the\nreadable side, when nobody is consuming (the destination said stop), the readable stops calling its\nunderlying `_read`, so the OS socket buffer fills, and TCP's own flow control eventually tells the\nremote server to slow down. That chain, from a slow browser through your process back to the model\nprovider's socket, is backpressure end to end. It only works if every link honours the signal, which is\nwhy hand-written `on('data', chunk => res.write(chunk))` loops are the classic bug: they ignore the\n`false` return and buffer without bound.\n\n### `pipe` versus `pipeline`\n`readable.pipe(writable)` implements the pause/drain dance for you, but it does not forward errors or\nclean up: if the destination errors or closes, the source is merely unpiped and left paused, never\ndestroyed, so its file descriptor or upstream HTTP connection stays open; if the source errors, the\ndestination is not closed. Both leak. `stream.pipeline(src, ...transforms, dest, cb)` (or its promise form in `node:stream/promises`)\nwires backpressure *and* error propagation *and* cleanup: an error or early close anywhere destroys\nevery stream in the chain and the callback receives the error. There is almost no reason to use `pipe`\nin a server.\n\n### Async iteration\nReadables are async iterables: `for await (const chunk of readable)` pulls one chunk at a time and\nnaturally applies backpressure because the loop body must finish before the next chunk is requested.\n`Readable.from(asyncGenerator)` goes the other way. For token streams this is often the clearest code:\nan async generator that yields parsed events, consumed by a loop that writes to the response and\nawaits `drain` when needed.\n\n### Web Streams\n`fetch` in Node returns `response.body` as a WHATWG `ReadableStream`, the same API as browsers and the\none many AI SDKs expose. It has the same ideas with different names: a queuing strategy with a\n`highWaterMark`, `pipeThrough(transformStream)`, `pipeTo(writable)` with error and abort propagation,\nand `reader.read()` for manual pulling. Backpressure is pull-based: a `ReadableStream`'s source\n`pull()` is called only while the queue's `desiredSize` (high-water mark minus queued size) is\npositive, and a writer exposes `writer.ready`, a promise that resolves when there is room, as the\nequivalent of waiting for `'drain'`. `Readable.fromWeb()` and `Readable.toWeb()` convert between the\ntwo worlds, which you need when a provider SDK hands you a Web stream and your HTTP framework wants a\nNode stream (or the reverse). Both kinds are async-iterable, so `for await` works on either.\n\n### Framing on the wire: SSE and chunked bodies\nA token stream has no natural chunk boundaries at the TCP level; a chunk may contain half a JSON\nevent. Providers therefore frame events with Server-Sent Events (`data: {...}\\n\\n`), and a Transform\non your side must buffer partial lines until a blank line arrives, and must decode bytes with a\n`StringDecoder` (or `TextDecoderStream`) because a chunk boundary can fall inside a multi-byte UTF-8\ncharacter. A `Transform` enforces backpressure at chunk granularity for you: it does not call\n`_transform` with the next chunk until its readable side has been drained below `highWaterMark`. One\nchunk that expands into fifty events overshoots the threshold by that one chunk's output, which is\nbounded. Backpressure truly breaks in a hand-written `Readable` or event-driven producer that keeps\ncalling `push()` after it has returned `false`; that buffer has no bound.","example":"A Transform that reframes a provider's SSE stream into newline-delimited JSON for the browser, wired\nwith `pipeline` so a client disconnect or upstream error tears down the whole chain. On client\ndisconnect the `AbortSignal` cancels the upstream `fetch` and closes that connection; most providers\nstop generating when the connection drops, but whether partial output is still billed is\nvendor-specific.\n\n```ts\nimport { Transform, Readable, type TransformCallback } from \"node:stream\";\nimport { pipeline } from \"node:stream/promises\";\nimport { StringDecoder } from \"node:string_decoder\";\nimport type { ReadableStream as WebStream } from \"node:stream/web\";\nimport type { IncomingMessage, ServerResponse } from \"node:http\";\n\nclass SseToNdjson extends Transform {\n  private buf = \"\";\n  private dec = new StringDecoder(\"utf8\");   // never splits a multi-byte character across chunks\n  override _transform(chunk: Buffer, _enc: BufferEncoding, cb: TransformCallback) {\n    this.buf += this.dec.write(chunk).replace(/\\r\\n/g, \"\\n\");   // SSE also allows CRLF\n    let idx: number;\n    try {\n      while ((idx = this.buf.indexOf(\"\\n\\n\")) !== -1) {           // one complete SSE event\n        const event = this.buf.slice(0, idx); this.buf = this.buf.slice(idx + 2);\n        for (const line of event.split(\"\\n\")) {\n          if (!line.startsWith(\"data:\")) continue;\n          const data = line.slice(5).trim();\n          if (data === \"[DONE]\") continue;                         // stream ends when upstream ends\n          this.push(JSON.stringify({ t: JSON.parse(data).delta ?? \"\" }) + \"\\n\");\n        }\n      }\n    } catch (e) { return cb(e as Error); }  // report parse errors to pipeline instead of throwing\n    cb();                                    // Transform waits for the reader before the next chunk\n  }\n  override _flush(cb: TransformCallback) {  // upstream ended: anything left is an incomplete event\n    this.buf += this.dec.end();\n    cb(this.buf.trim() ? new Error(\"stream ended mid-event\") : null);\n  }\n}\n\nexport async function proxy(req: IncomingMessage, res: ServerResponse) {\n  const ac = new AbortController();\n  res.on(\"close\", () => ac.abort());          // client went away -> stop upstream (no-op if finished)\n  const upstream = await fetch(\"https://provider.example/v1/stream\", {\n    method: \"POST\", body: JSON.stringify({ prompt: \"hi\", stream: true }), signal: ac.signal,\n  });\n  if (!upstream.ok || !upstream.body) { res.statusCode = 502; return res.end(); }\n  res.writeHead(200, { \"content-type\": \"application/x-ndjson\", \"x-accel-buffering\": \"no\" });\n  try {\n    const body = upstream.body as unknown as WebStream<Uint8Array>; // DOM vs node:stream/web typing only\n    await pipeline(Readable.fromWeb(body), new SseToNdjson(), res, { signal: ac.signal });\n  } catch (e) {\n    if (!ac.signal.aborted) console.error(\"stream failed\", e);   // disconnect is normal\n  }\n}\n```","tradeoffs":["Streaming gives low time-to-first-token and bounded memory, but you lose the ability to validate or moderate the complete output before the user sees it; add a trailing check or stream through a guard stage when the use case demands it.","Backpressure protects memory, but it also means a slow client slows the upstream read, holding a provider connection open longer; when the whole response is small (a text chat answer of tens of KB), draining upstream promptly into a bounded per-stream buffer and disconnecting clients that exceed it is often better than perfect flow control. For audio and files, strict backpressure wins.","`objectMode` streams of parsed events are convenient and let the `highWaterMark` count events, but each object is a heap allocation; for bulk byte processing stay in `Buffer` mode.","Web Streams are portable across runtimes (Node, browsers, edge) and match the AI SDK surfaces, while Node streams have richer tooling and wider framework support; converting between them is cheap but easy to get wrong around error propagation.","A larger `highWaterMark` smooths bursty producers and reduces pause/resume churn, at the cost of more memory per stream, which multiplies by concurrency."],"pitfalls":["Ignoring the `false` return of `write()` in a manual `'data'` handler; the symptom is heap growth proportional to the number of slow clients and eventual out-of-memory under load.","Using `pipe` instead of `pipeline`, so an error on the response does not destroy the upstream body; the symptom is leaked file descriptors and upstream connections held open after users disconnect.","Writing a custom `Readable` (for example, one fed by an SDK's event callbacks) that keeps calling `push()` after it returns `false`; the symptom is backpressure that \"works in tests\" but buffers unboundedly when real clients are slow.","Decoding each chunk with `chunk.toString()` in a streaming parser; the symptom is occasional `\\uFFFD` replacement characters in non-English or emoji output where a character straddled two chunks.","Buffering a whole stream (`await res.text()`) to parse it, then streaming it to the client; the symptom is time-to-first-token equal to total generation time.","Reverse proxies or compression middleware buffering the response; the symptom is tokens arriving in one burst at the end even though the server wrote them incrementally (hence nginx's `X-Accel-Buffering: no` header, or the equivalent setting in your proxy, and disabling compression for streamed routes).","Not listening for client disconnect and aborting upstream; the symptom is token spend on responses nobody receives."],"when":"Stream whenever the output is produced incrementally and a human is waiting (chat completions, voice,\nprogress of long agent runs), and whenever input size is unbounded (files, exports, logs). Use\n`pipeline` or `pipeTo` rather than manual event wiring, keep one `AbortController` per request tied\nto client disconnect, and build Transforms that respect chunk boundaries. Do not stream when the\nconsumer needs the complete result to act (a JSON tool call must be parsed whole) or when the\nresponse is small and fast. Measure time-to-first-token, per-stream heap, and the count of upstream\nstreams still open after client disconnect. In interviews, explain what `write()` returning `false`\nmeans, why `pipeline` beats `pipe`, and how a slow browser ends up throttling a model provider.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the SSE-to-client proxy with `pipeline`, respects `drain`, converts between Web and Node streams at SDK boundaries, and aborts upstream on disconnect.","architect":"Decides where streaming is exposed in the API, sets buffer and concurrency budgets per process, and ensures proxies and gateways in the path do not buffer streamed routes.","fde":"Fixes \"the answer appears all at once\" at a customer by finding the buffering load balancer or compression layer, and explains why their memory alerts correlate with slow mobile clients."},"check":{"q":"A voice assistant gateway streams synthesized speech (raw 24 kHz 16-bit PCM) from a TTS provider\nto mobile clients. Memory climbs steadily during the evening commute and the process is eventually\nkilled. Provider latency is normal and request counts are flat. Walk through how streams could cause\nthis and how you would prove and fix it.","a":"The likely cause is missing backpressure between the provider stream and slow clients: the\nhandler reads audio as fast as the provider sends it and writes it to each response, ignoring\n`write()` returning `false`, so bytes for slow connections accumulate in the response's internal buffer.\nAt 48 KB per second of audio, a minute-long answer to a client on a stalled cellular link buffers about\n2.9 MB, so memory scales with the number of slow clients (commuters in tunnels), not with request\ncount. The same bug in a text chat gateway would barely show, because the whole answer is tens of KB;\nthat is why it survived testing. Prove it with a heap\nsnapshot showing large `Writable` buffers, or by correlating memory with the count of long-lived\nresponses. Fix by replacing manual wiring with `pipeline` (or `pipeTo`), which pauses the upstream read\nwhen the response buffer is full, and by aborting the upstream request on client `'close'` so abandoned\nstreams stop consuming memory and tokens. Add a per-stream buffered-bytes metric (`res.writableLength`\nsampled across open responses) so a regression is visible."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does `writable.write(chunk)` returning `false` mean?","options":["The chunk was rejected and must be written again","The stream has ended and further writes will throw","The chunk was buffered but the buffer is at or above `highWaterMark`, so the producer should pause until `'drain'`","The underlying socket is closed and the data was lost"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The write still succeeds; `false` is a request to stop producing. Ignoring it does not lose data, it grows the buffer without bound. Waiting for `'drain'` before writing again is the backpressure contract that `pipeline` implements for you."},{"q":"Why is `stream.pipeline` preferred over `readable.pipe(writable)` in a server?","options":["`pipeline` is faster because it skips the internal buffer","`pipeline` propagates errors and early closes to every stream in the chain and destroys them, while `pipe` leaves the source open","`pipe` does not implement backpressure","`pipeline` works with Web Streams and `pipe` does not"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`pipe` handles pause and drain correctly but does not forward errors or destroy the source when the destination fails, which leaves the source paused but open and leaks upstream connections. `pipeline` ties the lifetimes together and reports a single error."},{"q":"A provider SDK returns a WHATWG `ReadableStream`, and the HTTP framework expects a Node `Readable`. What is the right bridge?","options":["Read the whole stream with `await response.text()` and write the string","`Readable.fromWeb(stream)` and then `pipeline` into the response","Wrap each `reader.read()` result in `process.nextTick`","Use `JSON.parse` on the stream object directly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`Readable.fromWeb` adapts the Web stream to a Node `Readable` while keeping backpressure and cancellation semantics, so `pipeline` can manage the chain. Buffering with `text()` destroys the streaming benefit, and the other options do not produce a stream at all."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/worker-threads-for-cpu-bound-tasks","sources":["Official docs: Node.js — worker_threads","Official docs: MDN — Structured clone algorithm, Transferable objects, SharedArrayBuffer and Atomics"],"why":"Most of an AI service is waiting on the network, which the event loop handles beautifully. But some\nsteps are genuinely compute: counting tokens with a BPE tokenizer over a 200-page document before\ndeciding how to chunk it, parsing a 50 MB PDF, computing cosine similarity between a query and a few\nhundred thousand embeddings held in memory, hashing files for deduplication, decompressing a large\nexport. Each of these can take hundreds of milliseconds to seconds of pure JavaScript, and during that\ntime the single thread serves nobody: streamed tokens for every other user stop, and health checks fail.\n\nThe naive fixes do not work. Marking the function `async` does nothing; `async` only yields at `await`\nand there is nothing to await in a tight loop. Chunking with `setImmediate` keeps the loop responsive\nbut makes the heavy job slower and still spends the one core on it; two heavy requests at once halve\neach other's throughput. Spawning a child process per job works but costs a whole Node runtime (tens of\nmegabytes and tens of milliseconds, illustrative) and serializes everything through pipes. Before\n`worker_threads` existed, the standard advice was \"Node is not for CPU work; call out to another\nservice\". Worker threads changed the trade: a worker is a second V8 isolate inside the same process,\nstarts far cheaper than a process, can share memory with the main thread, and lets the main loop keep\nstreaming while real parallel work happens on the other cores.","how":"### Isolates, not shared heaps\n`new Worker(file, { workerData })` starts an OS thread running its own V8 isolate and its own event\nloop. The worker has a separate heap and global scope: no variables, no module instances, no open\nsockets are shared with the parent. This is *because* V8's heap is not thread-safe; isolating heaps is\nwhat makes it possible to run JavaScript in parallel at all, and it is also why you cannot \"just call\na function on another thread\". Each worker therefore costs memory (its own heap, its own copies of\nloaded modules) and startup time (loading and compiling the worker script), which is why workers are\npooled and reused rather than created per request.\n\n### Message passing and structured clone\nParent and worker talk through `MessagePort`s: `worker.postMessage(value)` on one side,\n`parentPort.on('message', ...)` on the other. Values are copied with the structured clone algorithm,\nwhich handles plain objects, arrays, `Map`, `Set`, `Date`, typed arrays and `Error`s. Functions and\nsymbols throw a `DataCloneError`, and class instances arrive as plain objects with their prototype and\nmethods gone, so a `Tokenizer` object cannot be sent, only the data needed to rebuild one. Cloning is O(size): sending a 100 MB string to a\nworker copies 100 MB, and the copy itself happens on the sending thread, blocking it. The design\nconsequence is that the message should be a job description (a file path, an id, a small payload), and\nlarge data should be transferred or shared rather than cloned.\n\n### Transfer and shared memory\n`postMessage(value, [arrayBuffer])` lists `ArrayBuffer`s to *transfer*: ownership moves to the\nreceiver in O(1) and the sender's view becomes detached (length 0). This is the right way to hand a\n`Float32Array` of embeddings or a `Buffer` of file bytes to a worker and get a result buffer back. For\ndata that both sides must read concurrently, `SharedArrayBuffer` gives genuinely shared memory, with\n`Atomics` for synchronization (`Atomics.add`, `Atomics.wait`/`notify`). Shared memory is fast and\ndangerous in the usual ways: no structural locking, so keep it to numeric arrays and a clear protocol.\n\n### Pooling\nA pool keeps N workers (typically the CPU count, minus one for the main thread) alive, queues jobs, and\ndispatches each to an idle worker, resolving a promise with the result. Writing one is about forty\nlines; `piscina` (created by Node.js core contributors) is the common library and adds idle timeouts,\ncancellation and `AbortSignal` support. The pool is also your concurrency limit: with a queue in front,\na burst of 1,000 tokenization requests becomes N parallel jobs and a bounded wait, instead of 1,000\nthreads. Check the CPU count the runtime reports against the container's CPU quota; on older Node\nversions and some orchestrators `availableParallelism()` reflects the host, and a pool sized to 64 host\ncores inside a 2-CPU container just thrashes.\n\n### Parallelism across jobs versus within one job\nA pool parallelises *across* jobs: eight queries on eight workers can finish in about the time of\none, if there are eight free cores and the work is compute-bound. A brute-force scan of a 1.2 GB matrix\nis often limited by memory bandwidth instead, so eight concurrent scans share that bandwidth and the\nspeed-up is smaller; measure it rather than assuming linear scaling.\nIt does nothing for the latency of a single query, which still scans every vector on one thread. To\ncut one job's latency, split the data: give each of N workers a range of rows in the shared matrix,\nhave each return its local top-k, and merge the N small lists on the main thread. That divides the\nscan by up to N (less when memory bandwidth is the limit), at the cost of N messages and a merge per query, and it occupies the whole pool\nfor one request, so it fits low-concurrency, latency-sensitive paths rather than high-throughput ones.\n\n### Cancelling work inside a worker\nA worker running a tight synchronous loop is in the same position as the main thread was: its own\nevent loop is blocked, so it cannot receive a `'message'` saying \"stop\". Passing an `AbortSignal`\ndoes not help either, since the abort is delivered as a callback the busy worker never runs. Two real\noptions exist. `worker.terminate()` stops the thread at the next safe point regardless of what it is\ndoing, which is reliable but throws away the worker (the pool must spawn a replacement, paying\nstartup again). Or the job checks a cancellation flag in a `SharedArrayBuffer` with `Atomics.load`\nevery few thousand iterations, which the main thread sets with `Atomics.store`; that keeps the worker\nalive but requires the job to cooperate. Libraries such as `piscina` map an `AbortSignal` onto these\nmechanisms for you.\n\n### Memory limits per worker\nEach worker has its own V8 heap limits. `new Worker(file, { resourceLimits: { maxOldGenerationSizeMb } })`\ncaps a worker's heap so one runaway document parse kills that worker (an `'error'` the pool can\nhandle) instead of pushing the whole process into the container's out-of-memory killer. Budget\ncontainer memory as main heap + N × worker heap + shared buffers.\n\n### Workers versus `cluster` and child processes\n`cluster` (or several replicas behind a load balancer) runs N copies of the whole server so I/O-bound\ntraffic uses N cores; each copy still blocks on its own CPU work. Worker threads keep one server and\nmove the CPU work aside. Child processes give stronger isolation (a crash or native-module segfault\ndoes not take down the server) at higher memory and IPC cost. Many production services use replicas\nfor I/O scale and a worker pool inside each replica for CPU steps.\n\n### What workers are not for\nI/O-bound work gains nothing: a worker waiting on an HTTP call is a wasted thread. Work that native\ncode already runs off the loop (the async `crypto` and `zlib` APIs use libuv's pool) does not need a\nworker.\nAnd work that needs more than one machine's cores, or must survive a process crash, belongs in a job\nqueue (see the BullMQ lesson), not in threads. Workers also complicate observability: logs, traces and\nmetrics from inside a worker need to be forwarded or tagged, because the worker has its own globals.","example":"A minimal pool that dispatches top-k cosine-similarity jobs to workers, transferring the query vector\ninstead of cloning it. The embedding matrix lives in a `SharedArrayBuffer`: passing an ordinary\n`Float32Array` as `workerData` would structured-clone the whole ~1.2 GB matrix into every worker. The worker file and the pool live in separate modules; this listing shows both\nwith the worker guarded by `isMainThread`.\n\n```ts\nimport { Worker, isMainThread, parentPort, workerData } from \"node:worker_threads\";\nimport { availableParallelism } from \"node:os\";\n\ntype Job = { query: Float32Array; k: number };\ntype Hit = { index: number; score: number };\n\nif (!isMainThread) {\n  const vectors = workerData.vectors as Float32Array;      // flattened [n, dim], L2-normalised, shared\n  const dim = workerData.dim as number;\n  parentPort!.on(\"message\", ({ query, k }: Job) => {\n    const hits: Hit[] = [];\n    for (let i = 0, n = vectors.length / dim; i < n; i++) {\n      let dot = 0;                                          // dot of unit vectors = cosine\n      for (let d = 0; d < dim; d++) dot += query[d]! * vectors[i * dim + d]!;\n      hits.push({ index: i, score: dot });\n    }\n    hits.sort((a, b) => b.score - a.score);\n    parentPort!.postMessage(hits.slice(0, k));             // small result: cloning is fine\n  });\n} else {\n  class Pool {                                              // minimal: no crash replacement\n    private idle: Worker[] = []; private queue: Array<() => void> = [];\n    constructor(file: URL, data: unknown, size = Math.max(1, availableParallelism() - 1)) {\n      for (let i = 0; i < size; i++) this.idle.push(new Worker(file, { workerData: data }));\n    }\n    run(job: Job): Promise<Hit[]> {\n      return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n        const start = () => {\n          const w = this.idle.pop()!;\n          const onError = (e: Error) => { w.off(\"message\", onMsg); reject(e); };\n          const onMsg = (hits: Hit[]) => {\n            w.off(\"error\", onError); this.idle.push(w); this.queue.shift()?.(); resolve(hits);\n          };\n          w.once(\"message\", onMsg).once(\"error\", onError);\n          w.postMessage(job, [job.query.buffer as ArrayBuffer]); // transfer: O(1), query detached here\n        };\n        this.idle.length ? start() : this.queue.push(start);\n      });\n    }\n  }\n  const dim = 1536, n = 200_000;                            // 200k x 1536 x 4 B = ~1.2 GB\n  const vectors = new Float32Array(new SharedArrayBuffer(n * dim * 4)); // shared, not cloned per worker\n  const pool = new Pool(new URL(import.meta.url), { vectors, dim });\n  const hits = await pool.run({ query: new Float32Array(dim).fill(0.01), k: 5 });\n  console.log(hits[0]);                                    // main loop stayed free to serve streams\n}\n```","tradeoffs":["Workers give true parallelism for JavaScript on multi-core machines, but every worker is a separate heap, so memory scales with pool size; on a small container two workers may be all you can afford.","Structured clone is convenient and safe, while transfer is O(1) but detaches the sender's buffer; choose transfer for large payloads and accept the discipline of not touching them afterwards.","`SharedArrayBuffer` avoids copying entirely and suits read-mostly data like an embedding matrix, but it reintroduces data races that JavaScript otherwise never has, so keep it to numeric arrays with `Atomics` guarding writes.","A pool bounds concurrency and amortizes startup, but a job that blocks a worker for seconds blocks that slot; give long jobs their own pool or move them to a queue.","`terminate()` cancels anything instantly but costs a worker restart, while a shared-memory cancel flag is cheap but needs the job to poll it; terminate wins for rare cancellations of untrusted or third-party code, the flag wins when cancellation is routine (users abandoning requests).","Compared with a separate service, workers are simpler to deploy and share the process's memory, yet they die with the process and cannot scale beyond one machine; durable or horizontally scaled work belongs in a job queue."],"pitfalls":["Creating a new `Worker` per request; the symptom is latency dominated by worker startup and memory spikes under load, often worse than running the job inline.","Sending large buffers without the transfer list; the symptom is the main thread stalling on the copy, which is the very problem the worker was meant to solve.","Reading from a `TypedArray` after transferring its buffer; the symptom is zero-length arrays or `TypeError`s about detached buffers far from the `postMessage` call.","Using workers for I/O-bound steps like calling the model API; the symptom is no throughput gain plus extra memory and harder debugging.","Forgetting that workers have their own globals; the symptom is missing logs and traces, or a database client initialized twice because the module was loaded again in the worker.","Not handling `'error'` and `'exit'` events; the symptom is a pool that silently shrinks after a worker crashes and eventually hangs with all jobs queued.","Expecting a timeout or abort to stop a worker mid-computation; the symptom is the request returning an error while the worker's CPU stays at 100% and its pool slot stays busy until the job finishes."],"when":"Reach for worker threads when a code path performs sustained synchronous computation (more than a few\ntens of milliseconds, repeatedly) that must not stall the event loop and that fits in one machine's\nmemory: tokenization at scale, document parsing, embedding math in JS, compression and hashing of large\npayloads. Prefer `setImmediate` chunking for rare, medium-sized jobs, native or WASM libraries that\nrelease the thread when they exist, and a job queue for anything that must be durable or spread across\nmachines. Measure event-loop lag before and after, per-job time inside the worker, pool queue depth, and\nprocess RSS. Interviewers typically ask why `async` does not help, how data crosses to a worker, and\nwhen a worker is the wrong tool; be ready to compare with Python's multiprocessing and the GIL.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Moves tokenization and parsing into a pooled worker, transfers buffers instead of cloning them, and forwards worker logs and errors so failures are visible.","architect":"Sets the rule for what runs on the loop, in workers, or in a separate job service, and sizes pools against container CPU and memory limits.","fde":"Spots a customer's chunking pipeline parsing PDFs on the request thread, demonstrates the loop-lag fix with a pool, and explains why their 2-core container caps the gain."},"check":{"q":"A document-ingestion endpoint parses uploaded PDFs and tokenizes them in the request handler.\nDuring bulk uploads the chat endpoint in the same process becomes unresponsive. A colleague proposes\nwrapping the parsing in an `async` function and calling it without `await`. Evaluate the proposal and\ngive a better design.","a":"The proposal does nothing: `async` only yields at `await` points, and parsing is a synchronous\nloop, so it still runs to completion on the event-loop thread and the chat streams still freeze. The\nreal fix is to move parsing and tokenization into a pool of worker threads sized to the container's\nCPUs, send each job the file bytes as a transferred `ArrayBuffer` (not a clone), and return the chunked\ntext as the result. The pool also bounds concurrency so a bulk upload becomes a queue rather than a\nthundering herd. If uploads can exceed the memory of one process or must survive restarts, push jobs\ninto a durable queue with separate worker processes instead, and keep the API process for I/O only.\nMeasure event-loop lag to confirm the chat path no longer stalls."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does moving a CPU-heavy function into an `async` function not stop it from blocking the event loop?","options":["`async` functions run on the libuv thread pool but return to the main thread for results","`async` only yields control at `await`, and a synchronous loop has no await, so it still runs to completion on the main thread","`async` functions are compiled to generators that are slower, making the block worse","The event loop pauses microtasks while an async function runs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`async` changes the return type to a promise and allows `await` inside; it does not move code to another thread. Only an actual `await` on a pending promise suspends the function. True parallelism for JavaScript requires a worker thread."},{"q":"What happens to a `Float32Array` on the main thread after its buffer is listed in the transfer list of `postMessage`?","options":["It is copied, and both sides hold independent data","It becomes read-only on the main thread","Its buffer is detached, so the main-thread view has length 0 and the worker owns the memory","It is shared, so writes on either side are visible to both"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Transfer moves ownership without copying, which is why it is O(1), and the sender's view is detached to prevent two threads touching the same memory. Shared visibility requires a `SharedArrayBuffer`; structured clone without a transfer list copies the data."},{"q":"Which workload benefits least from a worker-thread pool?","options":["Tokenizing thousands of documents for chunking","Making concurrent HTTP requests to a model provider","Computing cosine similarity across an in-memory embedding matrix","Parsing large PDF uploads into text"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HTTP requests are I/O-bound and already non-blocking on the main thread; a worker waiting on a socket adds memory and complexity with no throughput gain. Workers pay off for sustained synchronous computation that would otherwise stall the loop."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","sources":["Official docs: Node.js — AbortController, AbortSignal, events.once, timers/promises","Official docs: WHATWG DOM Standard — AbortController and AbortSignal"],"why":"A model call is the most expensive thing a typical Node service does: seconds of latency, tokens\nbilled as they are produced, and a connection held open for the duration. Users abandon requests\nconstantly (closing a tab, hitting stop, re-sending a prompt), agent loops decide half-way that a\ntool result is no longer needed, and timeouts must give up on a slow provider and fall back to another.\nIn every case the question is the same: how do you make work *stop*, not just stop waiting for it?\n\nPromises offer no answer. A promise is a handle to a result, not to the work; `Promise.race` against a\ntimer settles your await but the underlying HTTP request keeps streaming, the provider keeps\ngenerating, and the tokens keep costing money. Node's older approach was ad hoc: every API had its\nown `req.destroy()`, `stream.destroy()`, `clearTimeout()`, and composing them across a call tree meant\nthreading callbacks by hand. `AbortController` and `AbortSignal`, borrowed from the browser, give one\nobject that every cancellable API understands. The design is cooperative *because* JavaScript cannot\npreempt a running function: the runtime cannot safely unwind a function that holds a database\ntransaction or a half-written file. Instead, a signal is a broadcast that each layer checks or\nsubscribes to, so cancellation flows down the same path as the request, and cleanup happens where the\nresources were acquired.","how":"### The two objects\n`const ac = new AbortController()` creates a controller with one `ac.signal`. The signal has\n`aborted` (boolean), `reason` (whatever `abort(reason)` was given, defaulting to a `DOMException`\nnamed `AbortError`), `throwIfAborted()` (throws the reason if already aborted), and an `'abort'` event.\nThe split is deliberate: the controller stays with the owner who may cancel, while the signal, which\ncan only be observed, is handed to everyone else. A callee cannot abort its caller's work by accident.\n\n### How built-in APIs consume it\n- `fetch(url, { signal })` rejects with `signal.reason` and closes the connection; with a streaming\n  body, reading from `response.body` also rejects. The closed connection is how the provider learns to\n  stop; most stop generating on disconnect, but confirm for yours.\n- `setTimeout` from `node:timers/promises` takes `{ signal }` and rejects early when aborted.\n- `events.once(emitter, 'name', { signal })` stops waiting.\n- Streams: `stream.addAbortSignal(signal, stream)` and `pipeline(..., { signal })` destroy the chain.\n- Child processes, `fs` promises, `readline` and most newer APIs accept a signal.\n- Provider SDKs accept a signal and pass it to the request: the OpenAI and Anthropic Node SDKs take a\n  `signal` request option, the Vercel AI SDK calls it `abortSignal`.\n\n### Composing signals\n`AbortSignal.timeout(ms)` returns a signal that aborts with a `TimeoutError` after the delay.\n`AbortSignal.any([a, b])` (Node 20.3 and later) returns a signal that aborts when any\ninput aborts, carrying that input's reason. Together they express the common case in one line: \"stop\nwhen the client disconnects *or* after 30 s *or* when the parent agent step is cancelled\". Before\n`any` existed the same was done by hand with listeners that forward one abort to another controller.\n\n### Making your own code cancellable\nThree idioms cover nearly everything:\n\n1. **Check at loop boundaries**: `signal.throwIfAborted()` at the top of each iteration of an agent\n   loop or chunk-processing loop, so a long computation stops at the next safe point. This only works\n   if the loop *yields* between iterations (an `await` on real I/O, or `setImmediate`). `abort()` is\n   itself called from a callback — the socket `'close'` handler, the timeout's timer — and no callback\n   can run while a synchronous loop holds the thread, so a loop that never awaits sees\n   `signal.aborted === false` until it finishes. CPU-bound work that must be cancellable either yields\n   in slices or runs in a worker that can be terminated.\n2. **Subscribe for cleanup**: `signal.addEventListener('abort', () => conn.release(), { once: true })`\n   when you hold a resource with no signal-aware API. Remove the listener when the work finishes\n   normally, otherwise a long-lived signal accumulates listeners for every call made with it and leaks.\n3. **Pass it down**: every async function that does I/O takes `signal` as a parameter and forwards it.\n   The call tree becomes a cancellation tree, with one controller at the root per request.\n\n### Distinguishing abort from failure\nAn abort surfaces as a rejection whose `name` is `'AbortError'` (or `'TimeoutError'` for the timeout\nsignal), and `signal.reason` tells you *why*. That holds only if you abort with the default reason or\nan `AbortError`-named `DOMException`: `fetch` and streams reject with whatever reason you pass, so\n`abort(new Error(\"bye\"))` produces a rejection named plain `Error`. This matters for the retry layer: a provider 503 should\nbe retried, a client disconnect must not be, and a timeout may be retried against a different provider.\nCode that treats every rejection as a failure will retry cancelled calls and bill tokens for a user\nwho already left. Check `signal.aborted` (or `error.name`) before classifying.\n\n### Deadlines across hops\nA timeout is really a *deadline*: the request must finish by time T. When a handler calls a tool\nservice that calls a model, each hop should get the time remaining, not its own fresh 30 s, or the\ninner call keeps working (and billing) after the outer caller has already given up. Compute\n`remaining = deadline - Date.now()` at each hop, create `AbortSignal.timeout(remaining - margin)`\ncombined with the parent signal, and pass the deadline to downstream services explicitly (a header\nor a field such as gRPC's deadline), because a signal does not cross a process boundary — only the\nclosed connection does.\n\n### Where the request-level controller comes from\nIn an HTTP server, the root signal comes from the connection: `res.on('close')` (or the framework's\nrequest abort event; in `fetch`-style handlers it is `request.signal`) fires when the client goes away.\nCreate one controller per request, abort it on close, combine with a timeout, and pass the resulting\nsignal into the model call, the tool calls and any streams. Cancelling the parent cancels everything\nunderneath because they all share one signal.","example":"A request-scoped signal combining client disconnect, a hard timeout, and an agent-step cancellation,\npassed through a streaming model call, with the abort reason used to decide whether the error is\nretryable. The listener cleanup in `withResource` shows the pattern for resources that have no\nsignal-aware API.\n\n```ts\nimport { setTimeout as sleep } from \"node:timers/promises\";\nimport type { IncomingMessage, ServerResponse } from \"node:http\";\n\nfunction requestSignal(req: IncomingMessage, res: ServerResponse, timeoutMs: number) {\n  const client = new AbortController();\n  // fetch rejects with this exact reason, so keep its name \"AbortError\" (not a plain Error)\n  res.once(\"close\", () => client.abort(new DOMException(\"client disconnected\", \"AbortError\")));\n  return AbortSignal.any([client.signal, AbortSignal.timeout(timeoutMs)]);\n}\n\nasync function streamModel(prompt: string, signal: AbortSignal, onToken: (t: string) => void) {\n  const res = await fetch(\"https://provider.example/v1/stream\", {\n    method: \"POST\", body: JSON.stringify({ prompt, stream: true }), signal,\n  });\n  const dec = new TextDecoder();                  // stream: true keeps split UTF-8 characters intact\n  for await (const chunk of res.body!) {          // rejects with signal.reason when the signal fires\n    signal.throwIfAborted();                       // also stop between chunks if parsing is slow\n    onToken(dec.decode(chunk, { stream: true }));\n  }\n}\n\nasync function withResource<T>(signal: AbortSignal, fn: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T> {\n  const conn = { release() { console.log(\"released\"); } };\n  let released = false;                          // abort and finally both fire on cancellation\n  const release = () => { if (!released) { released = true; conn.release(); } };\n  signal.addEventListener(\"abort\", release, { once: true });\n  try { return await fn(); }\n  finally { signal.removeEventListener(\"abort\", release); release(); }\n}\n\nexport async function handler(req: IncomingMessage, res: ServerResponse) {\n  const signal = requestSignal(req, res, 30_000);\n  try {\n    await withResource(signal, () => streamModel(\"Explain backpressure\", signal, (t) => res.write(t)));\n    res.end();\n  } catch (e: unknown) {\n    const name = (e as { name?: string }).name;\n    if (name === \"AbortError\") return;                       // user left: no retry, no error log\n    if (name === \"TimeoutError\") {                          // headers may be sent once tokens flowed\n      if (!res.headersSent) res.statusCode = 504;\n      return res.end(\"\\n[provider timeout]\");\n    }\n    throw e;                                                 // genuine failure: let the error layer classify it\n  }\n}\nvoid sleep;                                                  // sleep(ms, undefined, { signal }) for cancellable waits\n```","tradeoffs":["Cooperative cancellation is safe (cleanup runs where resources live) but only as good as the weakest link: one function that ignores the signal keeps the work alive, so coverage is a discipline, not a feature.","Aborting a model stream stops further output tokens on providers that cancel on disconnect, but the full prompt and every token already generated are still billed; cancelling very early saves the most output spend and very late saves almost nothing.","Aborting mid-tool-call can leave external state half-done (an email sent, a row inserted); for side-effecting tools prefer to cancel *before* the call or make the tool idempotent rather than cutting it off.","A single request-level signal is simple, but an agent sometimes wants to cancel one branch and keep the rest; child controllers whose signals combine the parent via `AbortSignal.any` give that granularity at the cost of more plumbing.","Timeouts as signals stop the work, unlike `Promise.race`, but a too-short timeout on a slow-but-fine provider turns success into waste; set them from measured p99 latency, not round numbers."],"pitfalls":["Using `Promise.race` with a timer as a \"timeout\"; the symptom is the request returning 504 while the provider keeps streaming and the token bill keeps growing.","Not wiring `res.on('close')` to a controller; the symptom is upstream connections and token spend continuing for minutes after users closed their tabs, visible as open sockets outliving requests.","Adding `'abort'` listeners to a long-lived signal without removing them; the symptom is `MaxListenersExceededWarning` and memory growth proportional to calls made under that signal.","Retrying on every rejection, including `AbortError`; the symptom is duplicate model calls after cancellation and bills that do not match request counts.","Calling `abort()` and then continuing to use the response stream; the symptom is confusing `ERR_STREAM_PREMATURE_CLOSE` errors reported as failures rather than expected cancellations.","Forgetting that `AbortSignal.timeout` starts counting at creation; the symptom is a timeout that fires early because the signal was created long before the request it guards.","Checking `throwIfAborted()` inside a synchronous loop that never awaits; the symptom is cancellation that \"works\" in tests with I/O but does nothing for a CPU-heavy step, which runs to completion after the client has left."],"when":"Create one controller per inbound request and pass its signal into every model call, tool call,\nstream and timer that belongs to that request; combine it with a timeout and, in agent loops, with a\nper-step controller so a planner can abandon a branch. Check `throwIfAborted` at the top of loops that\ndo real work. Treat `AbortError` as a normal outcome in logs and metrics, separate from failures, and\nnever retry it. Skip the machinery only for fire-and-forget work that must complete regardless, such\nas audit logging. Measure the count of aborts by reason (client, timeout, parent), the token spend on\naborted requests, and sockets open after request end. Interview questions here usually probe why\npromises cannot be cancelled and how a signal propagates through a call tree.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Threads a `signal` parameter through every I/O function, wires client disconnect and timeouts into it, and classifies `AbortError` separately from provider failures in the retry layer.","architect":"Sets the timeout budget per hop and the policy for cancelling side-effecting tools, and makes \"accepts an AbortSignal\" a requirement for internal client libraries.","fde":"Finds the customer's gateway that keeps streaming after users hit stop, quantifies the wasted token spend, and adds disconnect-to-abort wiring as the first-day fix."},"check":{"q":"A team wraps every model call in `Promise.race([call(), timeoutAfter(20_000)])` and reports that\ntimeouts \"work\" because requests return in 20 seconds. Their token bill is higher than their request\nvolume predicts. Explain what is happening and how to fix it properly.","a":"`Promise.race` settles the await when the timer fires, but it does nothing to the underlying HTTP\nrequest: the socket stays open, the provider keeps generating, and every token is billed until the\nresponse finishes on its own. The same applies to retries: the retry starts while the original call is\nstill running, so two calls are billed. The fix is to create an `AbortSignal.timeout(20_000)`, combine\nit with the client-disconnect signal using `AbortSignal.any`, and pass the result to `fetch` or the SDK's\n`signal` option, so the socket is destroyed when the deadline passes and generation stops. The error\nsurfaces as a `TimeoutError`, which the retry layer can handle deliberately. Measuring sockets open\nafter the deadline and token usage per request before and after will show the difference."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does JavaScript use cooperative cancellation through `AbortSignal` instead of letting a promise be cancelled directly?","options":["Promises are immutable, so adding a cancel method would break the specification","The runtime cannot safely preempt running code that holds resources, so each layer must observe the signal and clean up where the resource was acquired","Cancellation is handled by the operating system, not the language","Direct cancellation would require every promise to run on a separate thread"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A promise is a handle to a result, not to the work. Forcibly unwinding a function mid-transaction or mid-write would corrupt state, so the signal is a broadcast that cooperating code checks or subscribes to. The cost is that an ignoring layer keeps work alive."},{"q":"What is the difference between `Promise.race` with a timer and `AbortSignal.timeout` passed to `fetch`?","options":["There is none; both stop the request after the delay","`Promise.race` is faster because it avoids creating a signal","`Promise.race` only stops waiting while the request continues; the signal destroys the request so upstream generation stops","`AbortSignal.timeout` resolves with partial data instead of rejecting"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The race settles your promise but leaves the socket open and the provider generating billed tokens. A signal passed to `fetch` aborts the request, which closes the connection and makes the promise reject with a `TimeoutError` that can be handled explicitly."},{"q":"An `AbortError` reaches a retry wrapper that retries any rejection. What is the correct behaviour?","options":["Retry once, since the abort may have been spurious","Retry against a different provider, because the first was cancelled","Do not retry; the abort means the caller no longer wants the result","Convert it to a 500 error so the client can decide"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An abort is a decision by the owner of the request (client disconnect, parent cancellation) that the work is no longer wanted. Retrying it spends tokens for nobody. Only a timeout signal, identified by its `TimeoutError` reason, may justify a fallback attempt."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation","sources":["Official docs: Zod","Official docs: JSON Schema specification"],"why":"Every interesting input to an AI service is untrusted. A request body can be anything the client sent.\nA model's \"JSON\" output can be a markdown-fenced blob with a trailing comment, a number where a string\nwas asked for, or an argument object with a field the tool never defined. An environment variable is\na string even when the code needs a port number. A compile-time type on any of these is a comment,\nnot a check: `JSON.parse(body) as ChatRequest` tells the compiler to trust you, and the first missing\nfield becomes `undefined` deep inside business logic, usually as a `TypeError` after the model call\nwas already billed.\n\nThe naive alternatives fail in predictable ways. Hand-written `if (typeof x.messages !== \"object\")`\nchecks drift from the type the rest of the code uses and are never complete. Writing a TypeScript\ninterface *and* a separate JSON Schema for the model doubles the surface and lets them disagree, so the\nmodel is instructed to produce one shape and the code checks another. Validating only at the HTTP\nlayer misses the inputs that bypass it: queue messages, model outputs, tool results. The schema-as-value\napproach exists to make one definition serve three purposes: the static type (`z.infer`), the runtime\nparser (`schema.parse`), and the machine-readable contract handed to the model (JSON Schema). The\nprinciple is \"parse, don't validate\": the output of a parse is a *new* value of a narrower type, so\ncode after it cannot forget that the check happened.","how":"### Schema as a value\nZod schemas are built by composition: `z.object({ model: z.string(), temperature:\nz.number().min(0).max(2).default(1), messages: z.array(Message).min(1) })`. Each combinator returns a\nschema object carrying both the runtime checks and the phantom static type, which `z.infer<typeof\nSchema>` extracts. The type is derived from the schema rather than the reverse *because* a type\ncannot be inspected at runtime, while a value can be both executed and reflected on.\n\nA schema has two types, not one. `z.input<typeof S>` is what the producer may send; `z.output<typeof\nS>` (which is what `z.infer` returns) is what `parse` gives back. They differ whenever a field has a\n`.default()` (optional on input, required on output) or a `.transform()` (a string in, a `Date` out).\nUse the input type for whoever constructs the payload, including the model, and the output type for\nthe code that consumes it.\n\n### `parse` versus `safeParse`\n`Schema.parse(input)` returns the typed value or throws a `ZodError`; `Schema.safeParse(input)` returns\n`{ success: true, data }` or `{ success: false, error }` without throwing. Use `parse` at boundaries\nwhere failure should become an HTTP 400 through a central error handler, and `safeParse` where failure\nis an expected branch, such as a model output you intend to repair. `error.issues` is an array of\n`{ path, code, message }`, which is both what you return to a client and what you feed back to a model.\n\n### Transform, refine, coerce\nValidation often needs to change the value: `z.string().transform((s) => s.trim())`, `z.coerce.number()`\nfor a port read from `process.env`, `.refine((v) => v.end > v.start, { message })` for cross-field\nrules, `.pipe()` to chain a transform into a further schema. A transform makes the output type differ\nfrom the input type, which is exactly what `parse` is for: the result is a domain value, not the wire\nshape. Refinements that need I/O (does this `customerId` exist?) can be async, but then only\n`parseAsync`/`safeParseAsync` will run them; keep such checks few, because they turn a cheap pure\nparse into a database round trip at every boundary. `.brand<\"CustomerId\">()` makes the output a\nbranded type, so code that requires a `CustomerId` cannot be handed an unparsed string.\n\n### Objects: strict, strip, passthrough\nBy default Zod *strips* unknown keys from objects. `.strict()` rejects them, which is what you want for\ntool arguments (an unexpected key usually means the model misread the schema) and for request bodies\nwhere silently dropping a typo'd field hides a client bug. `.passthrough()` keeps them, useful for\nforwarding provider payloads you do not fully model. (Zod 4 keeps these methods but prefers the\nequivalent `z.strictObject()` and `z.looseObject()` constructors.) Discriminated unions (`z.discriminatedUnion(\"type\",\n[...])`) validate tagged variants like stream events and give the compiler the same narrowing that a\nhand-written union would.\n\n### From schema to the model, and back\nTool calling and structured outputs require a JSON Schema describing the arguments or the response.\nGenerating it from the Zod schema (Zod 4 ships `z.toJSONSchema`; earlier versions use the\n`zod-to-json-schema` package; the OpenAI Node SDK has Zod helpers and the Vercel AI SDK accepts Zod\nschemas directly)\nkeeps the model's contract and the code's in sync, with `.describe()` strings becoming the field\ndescriptions the model reads. Two details matter: a field with `.default()` is optional on input but\npresent on output, so generate the *input* schema for the model (`{ io: \"input\" }` in Zod 4); and\nproviders' strict structured-output modes accept only a subset of JSON Schema (some reject or ignore\nkeywords such as `format` or length bounds, and some require every property to be listed as\nrequired), so check what your provider supports rather than assuming the whole schema is enforced. The round trip is: schema → JSON Schema → model → JSON text →\n`safeParse` → typed value. Even with provider-side constrained decoding, keep the parse: it is the only\ncheck for `refine` rules, it guards against provider bugs, and it is where a failed parse becomes a\nrepair prompt (\"the field `due_date` must be ISO 8601; you returned `tomorrow`\").\n\n### Alternatives in the ecosystem\nValibot offers the same model with smaller bundles; TypeBox and Ajv work JSON-Schema-first, which suits\nteams whose source of truth is an OpenAPI document; ArkType trades Zod's chaining for a type-syntax\nstring. The choice rarely matters as much as the discipline: one schema per boundary, the type derived\nfrom it, and no `as` casts on untrusted data.","example":"A tool definition whose Zod schema produces both the TypeScript argument type and the JSON Schema sent\nto the model, with a repair loop that feeds validation issues back when the model's arguments do not\nparse. Unknown keys are rejected because an extra key from a model usually means it misread the schema.\n\n```ts\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst ScheduleArgs = z.object({\n  title: z.string().min(1).describe(\"Short title for the calendar event\"),\n  start: z.iso.datetime({ offset: true })                 // without offset: only a trailing \"Z\"\n    .describe(\"ISO 8601 start time with timezone offset\"),\n  durationMinutes: z.number().int().min(5).max(480).default(30),\n  attendees: z.array(z.email()).max(20).default([]),\n}).strict();\ntype ScheduleArgs = z.infer<typeof ScheduleArgs>;       // output: durationMinutes: number (required)\ntype ScheduleInput = z.input<typeof ScheduleArgs>;      // input: durationMinutes?: number\n\nexport const scheduleTool = {\n  name: \"schedule_event\",\n  description: \"Create a calendar event\",\n  // Zod 4 APIs throughout (on Zod 3: z.string().datetime(), z.string().email(), zod-to-json-schema).\n  // io \"input\" describes ScheduleInput: defaulted fields are optional for the model.\n  parameters: z.toJSONSchema(ScheduleArgs, { io: \"input\" }),\n  run: async (args: ScheduleArgs) => ({ id: \"evt_123\", ...args }),\n};\n\ntype ModelCall = (messages: Array<{ role: string; content: string }>) => Promise<string>;\n\nexport async function callToolWithRepair(rawArgs: string, model: ModelCall, maxRepairs = 2) {\n  let text = rawArgs;\n  for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= maxRepairs; attempt++) {\n    let json: unknown = undefined;\n    try { json = JSON.parse(text); } catch { /* reported as a syntax problem below */ }\n    const result = ScheduleArgs.safeParse(json);\n    if (result.success) return scheduleTool.run(result.data);   // result.data is typed and trusted\n    const issues = json === undefined ? \"(root): not valid JSON\" : result.error.issues\n      .map((i) => `${i.path.join(\".\") || \"(root)\"}: ${i.message}`).join(\"\\n\");\n    if (attempt === maxRepairs) throw new Error(`tool args invalid after repairs:\\n${issues}`);\n    text = await model([\n      { role: \"system\", content: \"Return only JSON matching the schema; fix the listed problems.\" },\n      { role: \"user\", content: `Previous arguments:\\n${text}\\n\\nProblems:\\n${issues}` },\n    ]);\n  }\n  throw new Error(\"unreachable\");\n}\n\nconst port = z.coerce.number().int().min(1).max(65535).parse(process.env.PORT ?? \"3000\");\nvoid port;\n```","tradeoffs":["One schema gives type, parser and model contract together, but it couples the wire format to the domain type; when they must diverge, add a `transform` or a second schema rather than casting.","Validating every boundary costs CPU proportional to payload size, negligible against a model call but noticeable on hot internal paths with large arrays; validate at the edge, trust inside.","`.strict()` catches model and client mistakes early, yet it breaks when a provider adds a harmless field; use `strip` for payloads you forward and `strict` for arguments you act on.","Repairing invalid model output with a retry prompt improves success rates at the cost of another model call and latency; bound the attempts and prefer provider structured-output modes that reduce the failure rate in the first place.","JSON-Schema-first tools (TypeBox, Ajv) align with OpenAPI-driven teams, while Zod's TypeScript-first style is more ergonomic for application code; mixing both in one service is where contracts drift.","Tightening a schema (a new required field, a narrower enum) protects consumers but breaks producers still sending the old shape, such as queued messages written before a deploy or a prompt not yet updated; add fields as optional with a default first, and tighten only once every producer has moved."],"pitfalls":["Casting untrusted JSON with `as` instead of parsing; the symptom is a `TypeError` on an `undefined` field deep in business logic, after the model call was billed.","Maintaining a TypeScript interface and a hand-written JSON Schema separately; the symptom is the model returning a field the code rejects, or the code expecting a field the model was never told about.","Using default `strip` behaviour on tool arguments; the symptom is a model hallucinating an extra parameter that is silently dropped, so the tool runs with half of what the model intended.","Returning raw `ZodError` messages to end users or logging full payloads in issues; the symptom is leaked internal field names or PII in client responses and logs.","Skipping the parse because the provider \"guarantees\" the schema; the symptom is `refine` rules never running and a provider-side change or bug reaching production unchecked.","Reading `process.env` without coercion; the symptom is `\"3000\" + 1` style bugs and a port compared as a string.","Using `z.coerce.boolean()` for env flags; it applies JavaScript truthiness, so the symptom is `FEATURE_X=false` turning the feature *on* because `\"false\"` is a non-empty string. Parse with an enum (`z.enum([\"true\", \"false\"]).transform((v) => v === \"true\")`) or Zod 4's `z.stringbool()` instead."],"when":"Parse at every boundary where data enters the process: HTTP bodies and query strings, webhook payloads,\nqueue messages, environment configuration, model outputs and tool-call arguments, and responses from\nthird-party APIs you depend on. Derive types from schemas rather than the reverse, generate the model's\nJSON Schema from the same source, and use `safeParse` with a bounded repair loop where the model is the\nproducer. Do not validate internal function calls between already-typed modules; that is what the\ncompiler is for. Measure the validation failure rate per boundary (a rising rate on model outputs\nusually means a prompt or model change) and the repair success rate. Interviewers ask why types alone\nare insufficient, how a Zod schema becomes a tool definition, and how to handle invalid model output.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Defines one Zod schema per tool and per endpoint, derives types with `z.infer`, generates the tool's JSON Schema from it, and routes `ZodError` issues into 400 responses or repair prompts.","architect":"Decides where the schemas live (a shared contracts package), which boundaries are strict versus permissive, and how schema versions evolve alongside prompts and tool definitions.","fde":"Audits a customer's agent for `as` casts on model output, replaces them with parsed schemas, and uses the validation failure rate to show which tools the model misuses most."},"check":{"q":"A team argues that since their provider supports structured outputs with constrained decoding,\nvalidating the model's JSON with Zod on their side is redundant work. What do you tell them?","a":"Constrained decoding makes the output syntactically match the JSON Schema, which removes most\nfailures, but it does not remove the need to parse. First, the JSON Schema cannot express everything the\nZod schema checks: cross-field `refine` rules, date validity, semantic ranges; and providers' strict\nmodes support only a subset of JSON Schema, so some keywords you did write may not be enforced. Output\ncut off by the max-token limit, or a refusal, can still arrive as invalid JSON. Second, the parse is what\nproduces a typed value; without it the code is still operating on `unknown` or on an `as` cast, so the\ncompiler cannot help. Third, the provider is an external system that can change or have bugs, and the\nparse is the only place that failure is caught before a tool runs with bad arguments. Finally, the\nschema is already written, so the validation is a single `safeParse` call whose cost is negligible next\nto the model call. The constrained mode reduces repairs; it does not replace the boundary check."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why derive the TypeScript type from the Zod schema with `z.infer` rather than writing the interface first and the schema to match?","options":["`z.infer` produces faster code because it skips type checking","A schema is a runtime value that can both validate and be reflected into JSON Schema, while a type is erased, so the schema must be the single source of truth","Interfaces cannot describe optional fields, but schemas can","Zod refuses to compile when an interface exists for the same shape"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Types exist only at compile time; nothing can execute or inspect them. Making the schema primary means the type, the parser and the model-facing JSON Schema cannot drift apart. Interfaces describe optional fields fine; they simply cannot check anything at runtime."},{"q":"When should a tool-argument schema use `.strict()` instead of Zod's default of stripping unknown keys?","options":["Never; stripping is always safer because it avoids rejecting valid calls","Only when the tool has no optional parameters","When an unexpected key likely means the model misread the contract, so rejecting and repairing is better than silently dropping it","When the JSON Schema is generated by the provider instead of the code"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A model that adds an undefined parameter has misunderstood the tool, and stripping it would run the tool with a different intent than the model had. Strict mode surfaces the mismatch so the issue can be fed back for repair. Stripping remains appropriate for payloads you merely forward."},{"q":"A model's tool arguments fail `safeParse`. What is the most robust next step?","options":["Run the tool with whatever fields parsed correctly","Cast the raw JSON to the argument type and continue","Send the parse issues back to the model in a bounded repair loop, and fail the call if it still does not parse","Retry the identical prompt until the output parses"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The issues carry the path and reason for each failure, which is exactly the feedback a model needs to correct its output. Bounding the attempts prevents runaway cost, and failing explicitly beats running a side-effecting tool with partial or unchecked arguments."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/dependency-injection","sources":["Fowler, 2004 — Inversion of Control Containers and the Dependency Injection pattern","Official docs: NestJS","Official docs: Awilix"],"why":"A typical AI backend handler does five things: load a tenant's config, fetch context from Postgres and a\nvector store, call an LLM through an SDK client, write the result plus some usage numbers, and emit a\ntrace. The obvious way to write it is `import { openai } from './clients'` where `clients.ts` does\n`export const openai = new OpenAI()`. This works on day one and hurts on day ten.\n\nThe problems appear in a predictable order. Tests: every unit test of the handler now either calls the\nreal API (slow, paid, flaky, needs secrets in CI) or reaches for module mocking (`vi.mock('./clients')`),\nwhich couples tests to file paths and silently diverges from the real wiring. Per-tenant variation: an\nenterprise customer wants their traffic routed to an Azure endpoint with their own key; a module-level\nsingleton has exactly one key. Provider fallback and A/B tests: \"use Anthropic when OpenAI keeps returning 429 or 5xx\"\ncannot be expressed when the dependency is a fixed import. Lifecycle: the singleton is created at import\ntime, before config is validated and before the process knows whether it is a web server, a worker or a\none-off script, so startup order becomes accidental.\n\nThe naive fix, \"export a `setClient()` setter\", is global mutable state: concurrent requests from\ndifferent tenants race on it. Dependency injection instead makes the dependency an explicit parameter,\ndecided once at the edge of the program, so the core logic is pure with respect to its collaborators.\nThe important word is *explicit*: you can read a function's signature and know everything it touches.","how":"### The shape of the pattern\n1. **Define the dependency by the capability you need, not the library you use.** An interface\n   `LLM { complete(req): Promise<Completion> }` is far narrower than the OpenAI SDK's surface; the handler\n   depends on that interface *because* then a fake, a caching wrapper or a different vendor can satisfy\n   it. TypeScript's structural typing means no `implements` keyword is required: any object with the\n   right shape is accepted.\n2. **Inject through the constructor or a factory argument.** `createChatService({ llm, db, clock })` or\n   `class ChatService { constructor(private deps: Deps) {} }`. Setter and field injection are avoided\n   *because* an object that can exist half-wired is a bug waiting for a code path.\n3. **Compose at one root.** A `main.ts` (or `createApp()`) builds the concrete graph: reads env,\n   constructs the pool, the Redis client and the SDK client, then the services, then the HTTP routes.\n   All `new` calls for infrastructure live here, so the rest of the codebase has none. The root is also\n   where lifecycle lives: it starts things in dependency order and shuts them down in reverse.\n4. **Tests build a different graph.** `createChatService({ llm: fakeLLM, db: testDb, clock: fixed })`.\n   No module mocks, no monkey-patching, no environment variables.\n\n### Composing behaviour at the root\nBecause every adapter satisfies the same `LLM` interface, cross-cutting behaviour is added by wrapping\none implementation in another (the decorator pattern), and the wrapping happens only at the root:\n\n```\nllm = withCache(redis,                       // outermost: a hit skips everything below\n        withFallback(                        // on failure or open breaker, try the next provider\n          withBreaker(openaiAdapter),        // per provider, so one outage does not trip the other\n          withBreaker(anthropicAdapter)))\n```\n\nThe order is a design decision, not decoration. The cache sits outside *because* a hit should cost\nneither a retry nor a breaker slot; each breaker wraps exactly one provider *because* its state\ndescribes that provider's health; the vendor SDK's own retries stay innermost, so a fallback is taken\nonly after a provider has genuinely failed. The handler sees none of this: it still calls\n`llm.complete()`, and a test can wire the same stack around a fake that fails on demand to prove the\nfallback path. Without injection each of these behaviours ends up as an `if` inside business logic.\n\n### Scopes\nInfrastructure clients (pool, Redis, SDK) are singletons: constructing them is expensive and they own\nsockets. Request-scoped objects carry tenant id, trace context, the user's remaining budget and\nper-tenant model routing; they are built per request from the singletons plus the request. A clean way\nin Node is to create a `RequestContext` in middleware and pass it down, or to use `AsyncLocalStorage`\nfor ambient values such as the trace id. `AsyncLocalStorage` is complementary, not a replacement: it is\ngood for cross-cutting context you would otherwise thread through every signature, and bad for real\ndependencies, *because* hidden ambient dependencies recreate the testability problem DI solves.\nContainers differ on scope mismatches: NestJS \"bubbles\" request scope up the chain, so a provider that\ninjects a request-scoped one silently becomes request-scoped itself (correct, but now rebuilt on every\nrequest), whereas a hand-wired root or a container without that rule lets a singleton capture the\nfirst request's object and keep it.\n\n### Containers and decorators\nFrameworks automate the composition root. NestJS uses class decorators (`@Injectable()`) and constructor\nparameter types to resolve dependencies through `reflect-metadata`; this requires the TypeScript\n`experimentalDecorators` and `emitDecoratorMetadata` compiler options, and because interfaces are erased\nat compile time, anything that is not a class must be registered under an explicit token (a string or a\n`Symbol`). tsyringe and InversifyJS work the same way; Awilix resolves by parameter name or by an\nexplicit registration map and needs no decorators. A container buys automatic graph construction,\nscoped lifetimes (request scope in NestJS, `createScope()` in Awilix) and lazy construction; it costs a\nruntime resolution step, errors that surface at boot instead of at compile time, and a learning curve.\nFor a service with a dozen dependencies a hand-written root is usually clearer; past a few dozen with\nrequest scopes, a container pays for itself.\n\n### What it is not\nDI is not \"make everything an interface\". Pure functions and value types are not injected. Inject the\nthings that are slow, stateful, non-deterministic or credentialed: network clients, databases, caches,\nclocks, randomness, the file system.","example":"Three files compressed into one listing: a capability interface, a service that depends only on it, the\ncomposition root that knows about vendors, and a test that swaps in a fake. The service never imports a\nvendor SDK, so the test runs with no network and no API key.\n\n```ts\n// llm.ts — the capability, not the vendor\nexport interface LLM { complete(p: { system: string; user: string; maxTokens: number }): Promise<{ text: string; inputTokens: number; outputTokens: number }> }\n\n// chat-service.ts — depends only on interfaces\nexport interface Deps { llm: LLM; db: { saveUsage(tenant: string, tokens: number): Promise<void> }; now: () => Date }\nexport function createChatService({ llm, db, now }: Deps) {\n  return {\n    async answer(tenant: string, question: string) {\n      const r = await llm.complete({ system: `Today is ${now().toISOString()}`, user: question, maxTokens: 512 });\n      await db.saveUsage(tenant, r.inputTokens + r.outputTokens);\n      return r.text;\n    },\n  };\n}\n\n// main.ts — the composition root: the only place that knows about vendors\nimport OpenAI from \"openai\";\nimport { Pool } from \"pg\";\nconst pool = new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL });\nconst openai = new OpenAI();                                  // reads OPENAI_API_KEY here, nowhere else\nconst llm: LLM = {\n  async complete({ system, user, maxTokens }) {\n    const res = await openai.chat.completions.create({ model: process.env.MODEL ?? \"gpt-4o-mini\", max_completion_tokens: maxTokens,\n      messages: [{ role: \"system\", content: system }, { role: \"user\", content: user }] });\n    return { text: res.choices[0].message.content ?? \"\", inputTokens: res.usage?.prompt_tokens ?? 0, outputTokens: res.usage?.completion_tokens ?? 0 };\n  },\n};\nconst db = { saveUsage: async (t: string, n: number) => { await pool.query(\"insert into usage(tenant, tokens) values ($1, $2)\", [t, n]); } };\nexport const chat = createChatService({ llm, db, now: () => new Date() });\n\n// chat-service.test.ts — no network, no mocking of module paths\nconst calls: string[] = [];\nconst svc = createChatService({\n  llm: { complete: async (p) => { calls.push(p.user); return { text: \"42\", inputTokens: 10, outputTokens: 1 }; } },\n  db: { saveUsage: async () => {} },\n  now: () => new Date(\"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z\"),\n});\n// expect(await svc.answer(\"acme\", \"meaning?\")).toBe(\"42\"); expect(calls).toEqual([\"meaning?\"]);\n```","tradeoffs":["Explicit wiring makes every dependency visible and testable, but signatures and the composition root grow; past a few dozen services with request scopes, a container (NestJS, Awilix) becomes cheaper than hand-wiring.","Decorator-based containers give automatic resolution and scoped lifetimes, but they rely on `emitDecoratorMetadata`, need tokens for anything that is not a class, and move \"missing dependency\" errors from the compiler to boot time; in small services and libraries the trade flips back to manual wiring.","Narrow capability interfaces let you swap vendors and wrap caching or retry decorators around them, but every vendor feature you need (tool calls, streaming, prompt caching) must be surfaced through the interface, so over-abstracting early costs real functionality.","Fakes in unit tests are fast and deterministic, but they only prove the service calls the interface correctly; a thin layer of integration tests against the real SDK (recorded or sandboxed) is still needed or the fake drifts from reality."],"pitfalls":["Module-level singletons created at import time (`export const openai = new OpenAI()`): the symptom is tests that fail with \"missing API key\" or make real calls, and startup crashes whose stack trace points at an import line.","Service locator in disguise: passing one giant `container` object and calling `container.get('llm')` inside business logic; the symptom is that a signature no longer tells you what a function touches, and every test must build the whole container.","Mixing scopes: injecting a request-scoped object (tenant config, trace span) into a singleton; the symptom is one tenant's model or API key leaking into another tenant's request under concurrency.","Mocking by file path (`vi.mock('../clients')`) instead of injecting: the symptom is tests that break when files move, and mocks that stay green after the real wiring changed.","Leaky capability interfaces that return vendor types (`OpenAI.ChatCompletion`, `Anthropic.Message`): the symptom is that fakes must construct vendor objects and adding a second provider still forces edits in business code; return your own small result type instead.","Circular dependencies between services, which some containers can paper over with lazy proxies (NestJS `forwardRef`) and hand-wiring cannot construct at all; the symptom is `undefined` at runtime or a NestJS circular-dependency boot error, and the fix is usually splitting an interface."],"when":"Reach for explicit injection in any service that talks to an LLM, a database or a queue, which is nearly\nall AI backend code. Decide the boundary by asking \"would I want to replace this in a test or per\ntenant?\" If yes, inject it; if it is a pure function, do not. Start with a hand-written composition root\nand a `Deps` type; move to a container when request-scoped lifetimes and the number of services make the\nroot hard to read, or when the team has standardised on NestJS. Measure success by whether unit tests\nrun with no network and no env vars, and whether adding a second LLM provider touches only the root. In\ninterviews, the expected depth is explaining why module singletons break tests and multi-tenancy, how\ninterface erasure affects containers, and where request scope lives.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes services against `LLM`, `Store` and `Clock` interfaces, wires concrete clients in one `createApp()`, and keeps unit tests free of network calls by passing fakes.","architect":"Owns the capability interfaces and the composition root as the seam where provider fallback, per-tenant routing, caching and credential isolation are decided without touching business logic.","fde":"Swaps the customer's private endpoint, proxy or on-prem model in at the root, and demonstrates the service against a fake LLM in the customer's CI before credentials are issued."},"check":{"q":"Your service imports a single OpenAI client at module scope. A customer now requires their\nrequests to go to an Azure OpenAI deployment with their own key, and the team wants automatic fallback\nto Anthropic when OpenAI is rate-limiting or failing (429s and 5xx after retries). How would you restructure, and where does each decision live?","a":"Introduce a narrow `LLM` interface covering only the operations the service uses, and make the\nservice take it as a constructor or factory argument. At the composition root build concrete adapters:\none wrapping the OpenAI SDK, one for Azure with the customer's endpoint and key, one for Anthropic.\nCompose them: a `FallbackLLM` that tries the primary and, on a 429 or 5xx that survives the SDK's own\nretries, calls the secondary, and a\nrouter that picks a per-tenant adapter from config at request time. The business logic never changes;\ntests inject a fake `LLM` and a fixed clock. Credentials stay in the root, tenant routing is\nrequest-scoped, and the fallback policy is a decorator around the interface, which keeps retry and\ncircuit-breaker logic out of the handler."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does TypeScript's type erasure matter for decorator-based DI containers?","options":["Because interfaces cannot be exported from modules that use decorators","Because an interface does not exist at runtime, so the container needs an explicit token or a class to know what to inject","Because erased types make constructor parameters optional","Because `emitDecoratorMetadata` only works with JavaScript files"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Containers resolve dependencies at runtime, and `emitDecoratorMetadata` can only record types that exist as values; an interface compiles to nothing, so a string or `Symbol` token (or a class) must stand in for it."},{"q":"Which dependency is usually NOT worth injecting?","options":["A function returning the current time","A Postgres connection pool","A pure function that formats a prompt template from its arguments","A client for a vector database"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Injection pays off for collaborators that are slow, stateful, non-deterministic or credentialed. A pure function is deterministic and free to call, so injecting it only adds indirection; clocks look harmless but are non-deterministic and matter in tests."},{"q":"A request-scoped tenant config object gets injected into a singleton LLM service. What is the likely symptom?","options":["Compile errors because scopes are checked by the TypeScript compiler","Slower startup because the singleton is now rebuilt per request","Under concurrent traffic, one tenant's model routing or API key is used for another tenant's request","The container refuses to start and logs a circular-dependency error"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A singleton is constructed once and shared, so it captures whichever request-scoped object it was first given; later requests from other tenants reuse that state, a correctness and security failure that only appears under concurrency. (NestJS avoids this by making the consumer request-scoped too, which trades the leak for per-request construction.)"}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/postgresql-transactions-and-migrations","sources":["Official docs: PostgreSQL","Official docs: node-postgres (pg)","Official docs: Drizzle ORM","Official docs: Prisma"],"why":"Consider finishing one step of an agent run: insert the tool result, append the assistant message,\ndecrement the tenant's monthly token budget, and enqueue the next step. If the process dies between the\nsecond and third statement the budget is never charged. If two parallel runs for the same tenant both\ncheck \"1,200 tokens left\", both pass, and both run `remaining = remaining - 1000`, the tenant ends at\nminus 800; if instead each writes back the value it computed in JavaScript (`remaining = 200`), one\ncharge is silently lost. Without transactions you write compensating code for every interleaving and\nstill miss some.\n\nThe Node-specific failure is subtler. `pg`'s `Pool.query()` borrows a connection per call.\n`await pool.query('BEGIN'); await pool.query('INSERT ...'); await pool.query('COMMIT')` is three\nstatements on potentially three different connections. Under concurrency the INSERT can autocommit on\nconnection B while connection A goes back to the pool still inside an open transaction, where it either\nsits idle in transaction or silently absorbs some other request's statements until a stray `COMMIT`\nlands on it. Nothing errors. In a single-user test the pool often hands back the same connection each\ntime, so the bug passes locally; in production the data is simply not atomic, and an idle-in-transaction\nconnection holds its locks until it blocks a migration.\n\nSchema change has the same \"it works locally\" trap. Running `ALTER TABLE` by hand on production means\nstaging and local disagree, nobody can reproduce last month's schema for a bug report, and a replica\nstarting mid-deploy sees columns its code does not know. Migrations exist to make schema a versioned\nartifact that moves through environments with the code, and to make the operational properties of each\nchange (locks, rewrites, index builds on million-row embedding tables) explicit before they hit\nproduction.","how":"### Transactions on one client\nPostgres transactions are per connection. In Node you check a client out of the pool, run `BEGIN`,\nexecute every statement on that same client, then `COMMIT` or `ROLLBACK`, and release the client in\n`finally` *because* a leaked client is a leaked connection: the pool silently shrinks until requests\nhang on `connect()`. Every library wraps this: `pg` leaves it to you, Drizzle exposes\n`db.transaction(async (tx) => { ... })`, Prisma has interactive `$transaction(async (tx) => { ... })`,\nKysely and Knex have equivalents. The callback form exists *because* it makes rollback-on-throw and\nrelease impossible to forget. Inside the callback use `tx`, never the outer `db`: the outer handle goes\nback to the pool and escapes the transaction. Postgres has no true nested transactions; a nested\n`tx.transaction(...)` in Drizzle or Knex becomes a `SAVEPOINT`, so an inner failure can roll back to the\nsavepoint while the outer transaction continues. If the client errors at the socket level, release it\nwith `client.release(err)` so the pool destroys it instead of handing a broken connection to the next\ncaller.\n\n### Isolation and contention\nPostgres defaults to READ COMMITTED: each statement sees data committed before it started. That is\nenough for most inserts but not for read-modify-write on a shared row such as a budget counter. Three\ntools:\n- `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` locks the row so concurrent transactions queue behind you. Cheap, explicit,\n  and the usual answer for counters and \"claim this job\" patterns.\n- Atomic updates with a predicate: `UPDATE budgets SET remaining = remaining - $1 WHERE tenant = $2 AND\n  remaining >= $1`, then check the row count. There is no lock to reason about *because* the condition\n  and the write are one statement. The reason it is safe even at READ COMMITTED: when two such updates\n  hit the same row, the second blocks on the row lock until the first commits, then re-evaluates its\n  `WHERE` against the newly committed row version. With 1,200 remaining and two charges of 1,000, the\n  first leaves 200, the second re-checks `200 >= 1000`, matches zero rows, and the caller sees\n  \"exhausted\". A `SELECT` followed by a JavaScript check and an `UPDATE ... SET remaining = $computed`\n  has no such re-check, which is exactly the lost-update bug.\n- REPEATABLE READ gives the whole transaction one snapshot; if it tries to update a row that a\n  concurrent transaction changed and committed after the snapshot, it aborts with `40001` rather than\n  overwrite. It prevents lost updates but not every multi-row anomaly (write skew).\n- SERIALIZABLE isolation makes the database detect anomalies and abort one transaction with SQLSTATE\n  `40001`. Your code must retry the whole transaction on `40001` (and on `40P01`, deadlock) in a small\n  bounded loop, *because* the abort is the mechanism, not a bug.\n\nKeep transactions short and never hold one open across an LLM call: a 20-second completion inside a\ntransaction pins a connection, holds locks and blocks the next migration. Do the model call, then open a\ntransaction for the writes. Back the rule with server-side safety nets: `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout`\nkills a session that sits inside an open transaction doing nothing (the leaked-client case), and\n`statement_timeout` bounds any single query. Both can be set per role or per connection, so the\napplication role can be strict while the migration role is not.\n\n### Idempotency and the outbox\nRetries from queues and clients mean the same logical write arrives twice. A unique constraint plus\n`INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` (or `DO UPDATE`) turns the second arrival into a no-op inside the\nsame transaction. When a write must also trigger a job or a webhook, insert an outbox row in the same\ntransaction and let a relay worker publish it, *because* \"commit, then enqueue\" can lose the enqueue and\n\"enqueue, then commit\" can run the job before the data exists.\n\n### Migrations\nA migration tool (Drizzle Kit, Prisma Migrate, Knex, node-pg-migrate, or SQL-first tools such as dbmate\nand Flyway) keeps ordered files plus a table recording which have been applied, and `migrate` applies\nthe missing ones in order, usually inside a transaction (tools differ: some wrap each file, some the\nwhole batch, and some need an explicit opt-out per file). The practices that matter in production:\n- **Forward-only, expand/contract.** Renaming a column is: add the new column; deploy code that writes\n  both and reads new-or-old; backfill in batches; deploy code that uses only the new one; drop the old.\n  Each step is compatible with the version before and after *because* during a rolling deploy both\n  versions run at once. Down migrations exist in most tools but are rarely the production rollback:\n  once new code has written data in the new shape, reversing the schema destroys it, so you roll back\n  the *code* (which the expand step keeps compatible) and fix forward.\n- **Backfill in batches, outside the DDL.** `UPDATE ... WHERE id BETWEEN $1 AND $2` in chunks of a few\n  thousand rows, each its own short transaction, *because* one giant `UPDATE` holds row locks on\n  every touched row until it commits, generates a burst of WAL that replicas must replay, and leaves\n  dead tuples that vacuum cannot reclaim while the transaction is open.\n- **Know which statements lock.** `ADD COLUMN` with a constant (non-volatile) default is a\n  metadata-only change since Postgres 11; `ALTER COLUMN ... SET NOT NULL` on an existing column, or\n  adding a CHECK or foreign key, scans the table under a lock that blocks writes. Use\n  `ADD CONSTRAINT ... NOT VALID` and then `VALIDATE CONSTRAINT` in a later step so the scan runs under a\n  weaker lock. For NOT NULL specifically: add `CHECK (col IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID`, validate it, then\n  `SET NOT NULL`, which since Postgres 12 uses the validated check instead of rescanning.\n- **Indexes concurrently.** `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` does not block writes but cannot run inside a\n  transaction block, so the migration file must opt out of the tool's wrapping transaction. This matters\n  for pgvector HNSW or IVFFlat indexes on large embedding tables, which can take minutes to hours to\n  build depending on row count, dimensions and `maintenance_work_mem`.\n- **`lock_timeout`.** Set `SET lock_timeout = '5s'` at the top of a migration: a DDL statement waiting\n  for a lock queues *every* later query behind it, so failing fast and retrying off-peak is safer than\n  waiting.\n- **Run migrations as a separate step** (CI job or init container), not inside every replica's startup,\n  and make sure a `pg_advisory_lock` guards the run so two runners cannot apply the same file\n  concurrently (many tools take one automatically; check yours rather than assuming). Point the\n  migration runner at Postgres directly, not through a transaction-mode pooler such as PgBouncer:\n  session-level advisory locks and `SET lock_timeout` belong to a server connection that the pooler may\n  swap between statements.","example":"A `withTransaction` helper on raw `pg` that pins one client, releases it on every path and retries\ntransient aborts, plus a step recorder that is idempotent (unique step id) and charges the budget with\nan atomic predicate update so no explicit row lock is needed. The SQL file shows an expand step with a\nconcurrent index, which must run outside the tool's transaction.\n\n```ts\nimport { Pool, PoolClient } from \"pg\";\nconst pool = new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL, max: 10 });\n\nexport async function withTransaction<T>(fn: (tx: PoolClient) => Promise<T>, retries = 3): Promise<T> {\n  for (let attempt = 0; ; attempt++) {\n    const client = await pool.connect();                // one connection for the whole transaction\n    let broken = false;\n    try {\n      await client.query(\"BEGIN\");                      // add \"ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE\" if you rely on it\n      const result = await fn(client);\n      await client.query(\"COMMIT\");\n      return result;\n    } catch (err: any) {\n      await client.query(\"ROLLBACK\").catch(() => { broken = true; });   // socket gone: do not reuse\n      const transient = err.code === \"40001\" || err.code === \"40P01\";   // serialization failure / deadlock\n      if (!transient || attempt >= retries) throw err;\n    } finally {\n      client.release(broken);                           // always release; `true` destroys a broken client\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nexport async function recordStep(tenant: string, runId: string, stepId: string, tokens: number, content: string) {\n  return withTransaction(async (tx) => {\n    const inserted = await tx.query(                    // idempotent: a redelivered job hits the unique key\n      \"INSERT INTO run_steps (run_id, step_id, content) VALUES ($1, $2, $3) ON CONFLICT (step_id) DO NOTHING\",\n      [runId, stepId, content]);\n    if (inserted.rowCount === 0) return \"duplicate\";\n    const charged = await tx.query(                     // atomic read-modify-write, no FOR UPDATE needed\n      \"UPDATE budgets SET remaining = remaining - $1 WHERE tenant = $2 AND remaining >= $1\", [tokens, tenant]);\n    if (charged.rowCount === 0) throw new Error(\"budget exhausted\");    // rolls the insert back too\n    await tx.query(\"INSERT INTO outbox (topic, payload) VALUES ('step.completed', $1)\", [{ runId, stepId }]);\n    return \"recorded\";\n  });\n}\n```\n\n```sql\n-- 0007_add_embedding.sql  (expand step; tool configured NOT to wrap this file in a transaction)\nSET lock_timeout = '5s';\nALTER TABLE documents ADD COLUMN embedding vector(1536);        -- nullable: metadata-only change\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY documents_embedding_hnsw ON documents USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops);\n```","tradeoffs":["Pinning a client per transaction gives atomicity but holds a pooled connection for the transaction's lifetime; with `max: 10` and 20-second LLM calls inside transactions the pool becomes the bottleneck, so keep model calls outside.","SERIALIZABLE removes whole classes of anomalies without hand-placed locks, but it aborts under contention and forces retry loops; at low contention it is nearly free, while on hot rows `FOR UPDATE` or atomic predicate updates perform better.","ORMs and query builders (Prisma, Drizzle, Kysely) make transaction scoping hard to get wrong and generate migrations from a schema file, but generated migrations know nothing about locks, concurrency or backfills; large tables need hand-edited SQL.","Expand/contract migrations are safe under rolling deploys but take several deploys instead of one; for a table of a few thousand rows on a single replica a direct change is fine, and the discipline pays off once the table is large or replicas overlap.","Running migrations as a separate pipeline step avoids races between replicas, but code and schema can then be deployed out of order, so every migration must be compatible with the previous code version."],"pitfalls":["Using `pool.query('BEGIN')`: statements land on different connections; the symptom is non-atomic data with no error, plus connections stuck `idle in transaction` in `pg_stat_activity`.","Using the outer `db` instead of `tx` inside a Drizzle or Prisma transaction callback; the symptom is a write that persists although the transaction rolled back, or a deadlock when the outer query waits on a lock the transaction holds.","Forgetting `client.release()` on an error path; the symptom is the service hanging on `pool.connect()` after exactly `max` failures.","`CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` inside the migration tool's transaction; the symptom is `cannot run inside a transaction block`, and a failed concurrent build leaves an INVALID index that must be dropped by hand.","Adding a NOT NULL column with an inline backfill in one statement on a large table; the symptom is a deploy that holds an exclusive lock for minutes while every API request queues behind it.","Running migrations through PgBouncer in transaction mode; the symptom is `lock_timeout` apparently ignored or an advisory lock that never seems to be held, because consecutive statements ran on different server connections.","Running migrations on container start with several replicas and no advisory lock; the symptom is duplicate-apply errors or two runners racing on the same `ALTER`."],"when":"Open a transaction whenever two or more writes must succeed together or a read must be consistent with\na following write: run bookkeeping, budget charges, message plus embedding inserts, outbox rows. Do not\nwrap LLM or HTTP calls in one. Prefer atomic predicate updates and `FOR UPDATE` for hot counters;\nreserve SERIALIZABLE for invariants that span several rows. For schema, every change goes through a\nmigration file reviewed for its lock footprint; anything touching a table beyond a few hundred thousand\nrows gets the expand/contract treatment and concurrent index builds. Watch `pg_stat_activity` for\nidle-in-transaction sessions, transaction duration and lock waits. Interviewers probe exactly the\npool-per-statement trap, the retry on `40001`, and how to add a NOT NULL column to a live table.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the `withTransaction` helper, keeps model calls outside it, handles `40001` retries, and reviews each generated migration for lock and backfill behaviour before merging.","architect":"Sets the policy: which invariants are transactional, where idempotency keys and the outbox live, the default isolation level, and that migrations run as a gated pipeline step with expand/contract.","fde":"Applies migrations inside a customer's VPC where a DBA controls maintenance windows, and explains why an embedding index must be built concurrently rather than during a deploy."},"check":{"q":"A worker charges a tenant's token budget and records an agent step. Under load, budgets\noccasionally go negative and some steps appear twice. Diagnose the two bugs and fix them without\nchanging the isolation level.","a":"Negative budgets mean a check-then-act race: the code reads `remaining`, checks it in JavaScript,\nthen decrements, so two transactions both pass the check against the same value and both subtract.\n(Writing back a value computed in JavaScript would instead lose a charge, which is the same race with a\ndifferent symptom.) Replace it with a single predicate\nupdate, `UPDATE budgets SET remaining = remaining - $1 WHERE tenant = $2 AND remaining >= $1`, and treat\na zero row count as \"exhausted\"; or lock with `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` first. Duplicate steps mean\nat-least-once delivery from the queue with no idempotency: add a unique constraint on the step id, use\n`INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`, and only charge when the insert actually inserted a row. Both must\nrun on one pinned client inside one transaction so a failure after the charge rolls the charge back.\nFinally confirm the code is not calling `pool.query('BEGIN')`, which would scatter the statements across\nconnections."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is `await pool.query('BEGIN'); await pool.query('INSERT ...'); await pool.query('COMMIT')` wrong with node-postgres?","options":["`BEGIN` must be uppercase and parameterised to be recognised","Each `pool.query` may use a different connection, so the INSERT can run outside the transaction","The pool rejects `COMMIT` unless autocommit is disabled","Transactions require the `pg-transaction` extension"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Transactions are a property of a single connection. The pool borrows a connection per call, so the INSERT can autocommit on another connection while the first sits idle in transaction, and no error is raised."},{"q":"You need an HNSW index on a 5-million-row embeddings table in production. Which approach avoids blocking writes?","options":["`CREATE INDEX` inside the migration's transaction so it can roll back","Drop and recreate the table with the index defined inline","`CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` in a migration configured to run outside a transaction block","Add the index at application startup with a retry loop"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Concurrent index builds allow reads and writes during the build but cannot run inside a transaction block, so the migration tool's wrapping transaction must be disabled for that file; a failed build leaves an invalid index to drop."},{"q":"Your transaction retry helper catches SQLSTATE 40001. When does that error actually occur?","options":["Whenever a unique constraint is violated","When the pool has no free connections","When Postgres aborts a transaction under REPEATABLE READ or SERIALIZABLE because a concurrent commit made it unserializable","When a statement exceeds `statement_timeout`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`40001` is `serialization_failure`: the database detects that the transaction's reads are inconsistent with a concurrent commit and aborts it, expecting the application to retry; unique violations are `23505` and timeouts are `57014`."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/redis","sources":["Official docs: Redis","Official docs: ioredis","Official docs: node-redis"],"why":"Run two replicas of an agent API behind a load balancer and every in-process `Map` becomes a bug: the\nrate limiter counts half the traffic, the response cache misses whenever the balancer picks the other\nreplica, the \"is this run already in progress\" flag is wrong half the time, and a streaming client that\nreconnects lands on a replica that has no idea about its stream. Postgres could hold all of this, but a\ntoken-bucket check on every request, with disk-backed transactions and MVCC overhead, costs\nmilliseconds and connections you want for real queries, and TTL-based expiry needs a cron job.\n\nRedis sits in the gap: a network-attached data-structure library where `INCR`, `SET ... NX PX`, `ZADD`\nand `XADD` are single atomic operations measured in microseconds server-side, keys expire on their own,\nand memory is the only limit. The design choice that makes it work is single-threaded command\nexecution: no locks, no partial updates, and a Lua script or `MULTI` block runs as one unit *because*\nnothing else can interleave. One thread is enough *because* the data is in RAM, almost every command is\nO(1) or O(log n), and an event loop multiplexes thousands of client sockets, so the server spends its\ntime executing commands rather than waiting; Redis 6 added optional I/O threads for reading and writing\nsockets, but command execution itself stays serial. The cost of that choice is equally clear: one slow command (a `KEYS *`, a\nhuge `SMEMBERS`, a long script) stalls every client, and data that must survive a crash needs\npersistence configured deliberately rather than assumed.","how":"### Data structures, picked by access pattern\n- **Strings** with `SET key value EX ttl`: cached completions, feature flags, idempotency markers\n  (`SET ... NX`).\n- **Hashes**: one conversation's metadata or a session, updated field by field with `HSET`, expired as\n  a whole (Redis 7.4 added per-field TTLs with `HEXPIRE`, but older servers and many managed offerings\n  only expire whole keys).\n- **Lists**: simple queues and recent-messages windows (`LPUSH` plus `LTRIM`).\n- **Sets and sorted sets**: membership and ranking; sorted sets with timestamps as scores implement\n  sliding-window rate limits (`ZADD`, `ZREMRANGEBYSCORE`, `ZCARD`).\n- **Streams**: an append-only log with consumer groups; the basis for relaying token streams between\n  replicas and for BullMQ's event log.\n- **Pub/Sub**: fire-and-forget broadcast, used to relay streamed tokens from the replica running the\n  LLM call to the replica holding the client's SSE connection. Messages are not stored, so a subscriber\n  that is offline misses them; use Streams when that matters.\n\n### Atomicity: single thread, MULTI, Lua\nEach command is atomic. Compound logic (\"increment, and if this is the first hit set the expiry\")\nneeds either `MULTI`/`EXEC` (queued commands executed without interleaving, but no server-side\nbranching) or a Lua script via `EVAL`/`EVALSHA`, which runs atomically and can branch. Rate limiters,\ntoken buckets and lock release (\"delete only if the value is still my token\") are Lua scripts *because*\na get-then-set from the client is a race between two round trips.\n\n### Persistence and eviction\nRedis persists by RDB snapshots (periodic, cheap, can lose the last minutes) or AOF (append-only log;\n`appendfsync everysec` loses at most about a second). A cache can run with no persistence and\n`maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru`; a queue or lock store must run `noeviction` with AOF, *because* evicting\na job key under memory pressure silently loses work. Decide per instance, and do not share one instance\nbetween the two roles.\n\n### From Node\nTwo mainstream clients: `ioredis` (long the de-facto standard and what BullMQ uses) and `redis`\n(node-redis, the official client). Both are promise-based, support pipelining (node-redis batches\nun-awaited commands automatically, ioredis with `enableAutoPipelining` or an explicit `pipeline()`),\nand support Lua scripts, Cluster and Sentinel. Node-specific rules:\n- A connection in subscriber mode or running a blocking command (`BLPOP`, `XREAD BLOCK`) cannot issue\n  other commands, so pub/sub and blocking consumers get their own connection; BullMQ workers open a\n  separate blocking connection for the same reason and require `maxRetriesPerRequest: null` on it.\n- Configure reconnect and failure behaviour explicitly: ioredis's `maxRetriesPerRequest` and\n  `enableOfflineQueue` decide whether a command waits for reconnection or fails fast. On a request path\n  you usually want fail-fast *because* a hung Redis should degrade the cache, not take down the API.\n- Namespace keys per tenant and per purpose (`t:{tenantId}:cache:{hash}`); hash tags in braces keep\n  related keys on one Cluster slot. That matters for atomicity, not just locality: in Redis Cluster a\n  `MULTI` block or Lua script may only touch keys in a single slot and fails with `CROSSSLOT`\n  otherwise, which is also why BullMQ asks you to wrap a queue prefix in braces on Cluster.\n- Never block the event loop building huge payloads; values beyond a few hundred kilobytes belong in\n  object storage with a Redis pointer.\n\n### Typical AI-service uses\n- **Completion cache**: key = SHA-256 of (model, system prompt, messages, parameters), serialised with\n  stable key order; cache only requests where one canonical answer is acceptable (temperature 0 or\n  explicitly marked cacheable; note that temperature 0 is not bit-for-bit deterministic on hosted APIs,\n  so the cache *chooses* an answer rather than reproducing one) and store usage alongside so cost\n  dashboards stay honest.\n- **Stampede protection**: when a hot cache entry expires, or after a deploy with a cold cache, many\n  identical requests miss at once and every one calls the model, so a single expiry can cost N paid\n  completions. The fix is single-flight: the first miss takes `SET llm:fill:{hash} 1 NX PX 30000` and\n  calls the model, the others poll the cache key briefly (or wait on a pub/sub message) before falling\n  back to their own call. Add random jitter to TTLs so entries written together do not expire\n  together; within one replica, a `Map` of in-flight promises keyed by hash deduplicates for free.\n- **Provider rate limit / semaphore**: a token bucket in Lua keyed by provider and model so N replicas\n  together stay under the vendor's requests-per-minute and tokens-per-minute limits.\n- **Run state and locks**: `SET run:{id}:lock token NX PX 30000` to ensure one worker per run, released\n  by a check-and-delete script; refresh the TTL while working. A TTL lock can still expire under a\n  paused process, so writes that must never happen twice also need a fencing token or a unique\n  constraint downstream.\n- **Short-term memory**: last-N turns for a conversation with a TTL, while long-term memory lives in\n  Postgres or a vector store.","example":"A completion cache and a fixed-window rate limiter using ioredis. The limiter is a Lua script so the\nincrement and the expiry are set atomically; the cache key hashes the fields that affect the output in a\nfixed order, and every Redis failure on the hot path degrades rather than failing the request (the\nlimiter fails open, the cache falls through to the provider). Fixed windows allow up to twice the limit\nacross a window boundary; use a sliding window or token bucket when that matters.\n\n```ts\nimport Redis from \"ioredis\";\nimport { createHash } from \"node:crypto\";\n\nconst redis = new Redis(process.env.REDIS_URL!, { maxRetriesPerRequest: 1, enableOfflineQueue: false });\nredis.on(\"error\", (e) => console.error(\"redis\", e.message));   // an unhandled 'error' event would crash the process\n\n// Atomic fixed-window limiter: the first hit in a window sets the TTL.\nredis.defineCommand(\"hitWindow\", {\n  numberOfKeys: 1,\n  lua: `local n = redis.call('INCR', KEYS[1])\n        if n == 1 then redis.call('PEXPIRE', KEYS[1], ARGV[1]) end\n        return n`,\n});\nexport async function allow(tenant: string, limit = 60, windowMs = 60_000): Promise<boolean> {\n  const bucket = Math.floor(Date.now() / windowMs);\n  try {\n    const n: number = await (redis as any).hitWindow(`rl:${tenant}:${bucket}`, windowMs);\n    return n <= limit;\n  } catch { return true; }                                          // Redis down: fail open (a policy choice)\n}\n\n// Cache-aside for deterministic completions.\nexport async function cachedComplete(req: { model: string; system: string; messages: unknown[]; temperature: number; maxTokens: number },\n                                     call: () => Promise<string>, ttlSec = 3600): Promise<string> {\n  if (req.temperature !== 0) return call();                      // sampled output: do not cache\n  const material = JSON.stringify([req.model, req.system, req.messages, req.temperature, req.maxTokens]);  // fixed field order\n  const key = \"llm:\" + createHash(\"sha256\").update(material).digest(\"hex\");\n  try {\n    const hit = await redis.get(key);\n    if (hit !== null) return hit;\n  } catch { /* Redis down: fall through to the provider */ }\n  const out = await call();\n  redis.set(key, out, \"EX\", ttlSec).catch(() => {});             // best effort, never awaited on the hot path\n  return out;\n}\n```","tradeoffs":["Microsecond atomic operations and built-in expiry, but everything lives in RAM; a completion cache holding 20 KB responses for a million prompts is 20 GB, so entry size and TTL must be budgeted, not assumed.","Single-threaded execution gives atomicity for free, but one slow command stalls all clients; the moment anyone needs scans over large keyspaces or long Lua loops, the instance must be split by role.","Pub/Sub is the simplest way to relay streamed tokens across replicas, but it drops messages for absent subscribers; when a reconnecting client must resume, Streams with consumer groups cost more code and keep the data.","Running Redis as a cache (`allkeys-lru`, no persistence) is cheap and safe to lose; the same configuration under a queue or lock store silently discards work, so durability costs money (AOF, replicas) and a separate instance.","Managed Redis (or Valkey, the Linux Foundation fork) removes operations work but adds network hops; placing it in another availability zone turns sub-millisecond calls into milliseconds on every request."],"pitfalls":["Sharing one connection between pub/sub or blocking commands and normal commands; the symptom is an error that only subscriber commands may be used, or requests that hang behind a `BLPOP`.","No `error` listener on the client; the symptom is the whole Node process exiting on the first network blip with an unhandled `'error'` event.","Caching non-deterministic completions or hashing a request object with unstable key order; the symptom is stale or wrong answers served as hits, or a hit rate far below what the traffic suggests.","No stampede protection on an expensive cache: the symptom is a burst of identical provider calls (and a token-cost spike) at the moment a popular entry expires or right after a cache flush, even though the steady-state hit rate looks healthy.","Using `KEYS pattern` in production to find a tenant's keys; the symptom is latency spikes on every client while the scan runs. Use `SCAN` with a cursor or maintain an index set.","Lock release with a plain `DEL`; the symptom is worker A, whose lock expired during a long LLM call, deleting the lock worker B now holds, so two workers process one run.","BullMQ or locks on an instance configured with `allkeys-lru`; the symptom is jobs that vanish under memory pressure with no error anywhere."],"when":"Reach for Redis when state must be shared across replicas, must be fast, and is either rebuildable\n(cache) or short-lived (rate windows, locks, run state, recent turns). Keep it out of the path for\nanything you cannot afford to lose unless you have configured AOF, replicas and `noeviction` and\nunderstand what each costs. Do not use it as the primary store for conversations or evaluation results;\nthat is Postgres. Measure hit rate, p99 command latency, memory against `maxmemory`, evicted keys and\nconnected clients per replica. In interviews, be ready to explain why rate limiting needs a Lua script,\nwhy pub/sub loses messages, and why a cache and a queue should not share an instance.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the cache-aside and token-bucket helpers, keeps separate connections for subscribers, and makes every Redis call on the request path fail open or fail fast.","architect":"Decides which state is allowed in Redis, the persistence and eviction policy per instance, key namespacing for tenants, and whether Streams or a queue carry cross-replica events.","fde":"Works with whatever managed Redis or Valkey the customer runs, checks eviction policy and version before wiring BullMQ to it, and sizes memory from entry size and TTL."},"check":{"q":"Your agent API runs four replicas. A streaming response is produced by the replica that called\nthe model, but the client's SSE connection may be on another replica, and clients sometimes reconnect\nmid-stream. Design the Redis part and explain the trade-offs.","a":"The producing replica appends each token chunk to a Redis Stream keyed by run id with a TTL, and\nthe replica holding the SSE connection reads it with `XREAD BLOCK` from the last id the client\nacknowledged, which makes reconnects resumable because the chunks are stored, not merely broadcast.\nPub/Sub would be simpler and lower latency, but a client reconnecting would miss everything published\nwhile it was away. Each blocking reader needs its own connection, and the stream must be trimmed or\nexpired so memory does not grow with every run. The instance should run `noeviction` so chunks are not\ndropped under pressure, and the final entry should be a sentinel so the consumer knows the stream is\ncomplete rather than stalled."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is a rate limiter implemented as a Lua script instead of `GET` then `SET` from Node?","options":["Lua runs faster than JavaScript on the Redis server","The read and the write from Node are separate round trips, so two replicas can both read the same count and both allow a request past the limit","Redis rejects `SET` from clients that have recently called `GET`","Lua scripts are the only way to set a TTL on a key"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Redis executes each command atomically, but a client-side get-then-set is two commands with a gap; a script (or `MULTI`) runs as one unit on the single-threaded server, which closes the race."},{"q":"BullMQ jobs are disappearing from a shared Redis instance under load, with no errors logged. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The jobs exceeded Redis's maximum value size","Workers are running with too much concurrency","The instance uses an `allkeys-lru` eviction policy and job keys are evicted when memory fills","Pub/Sub messages were dropped because no subscriber was connected"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A cache-style eviction policy treats job state like any other key and silently removes it at `maxmemory`; queue and lock workloads need `noeviction` and ideally their own instance."},{"q":"Which client setup is correct for a Node service that both caches completions and subscribes to a channel?","options":["One connection for everything, because ioredis multiplexes commands","Two connections: one for normal commands and a dedicated one in subscriber mode","A new connection per request to avoid head-of-line blocking","A connection pool of at least one per CPU core"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A connection that has issued `SUBSCRIBE` can only run subscriber commands, so `GET`/`SET` on it fail; a separate subscriber connection is required, while normal commands pipeline fine on one shared connection."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/bullmq-and-durable-jobs","sources":["Official docs: BullMQ","Official docs: Redis"],"why":"An agent run takes two minutes, calls the model eight times and calls three tools. Doing that inside an\nHTTP handler means the client holds a connection for two minutes, a load balancer idle timeout (60 s is a\ncommon default) can kill it first, a deploy that restarts the process loses the run with no record, and a burst of 200\nruns starts 200 concurrent model calls that hit the provider's rate limit together. The same shape\nappears for batch embedding, nightly evals, document ingestion, webhook fan-out and scheduled\nre-indexing.\n\nThe in-process alternative, `setImmediate(() => runAgent(...))` with a `Map` of in-flight runs, fails\non every axis: it dies with the process, cannot be shared across replicas, has no retry policy and no\nvisibility. Postgres-as-queue (`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`) is a legitimate design and keeps\neverything in one transactional store, but you then write the polling, locking, retry, backoff, delay\nand dead-letter machinery yourself.\n\nBullMQ packages that machinery on top of Redis. Redis fits *because* the queue's hot path (claim a job,\nrenew its lock, move it between states) is a handful of atomic operations on lists, sorted sets and\nhashes, which Redis executes in microseconds and which BullMQ implements as Lua scripts so a claim can\nnever be half-done. The trade is that the queue inherits Redis's durability settings: snapshot-only\npersistence or an LRU eviction policy means lost jobs.","how":"### Objects\n- **Queue**: the producer handle. `queue.add(name, data, opts)` writes a job hash and pushes its id onto\n  the waiting list (or a delayed or prioritised sorted set).\n- **Worker**: the consumer. It takes a processor `(job) => Promise<result>` and a `concurrency`; each\n  slot atomically moves a job from waiting to active and runs the processor. Many Worker processes on\n  many replicas share one queue, which is how you scale.\n- **Job**: `id`, `name`, `data` (JSON), `opts`, `attemptsMade`, `progress`, `returnvalue` and\n  timestamps. A custom `jobId` deduplicates: adding a job whose id already exists in the queue is a\n  no-op while the earlier job is still stored, which is the simplest exactly-once-enqueue tool you have.\n  Custom ids must not be plain integers or contain `:` (BullMQ uses it as its key separator and throws\n  on `add`), so use `embed-<docId>`, not `embed:<docId>`. The dedup window is exactly the job's\n  lifetime in Redis: once `removeOnComplete` deletes the job, the same id can be added again, and while\n  a completed job is still retained, a deliberate re-run with that id is silently ignored. BullMQ 5\n  also offers a `deduplication` option with its own id and TTL when you want that window explicit.\n- **QueueEvents**: a Redis-streams-based listener for `completed`, `failed` and `progress`, so an API\n  replica can stream progress to a client without being the worker.\n- **FlowProducer**: adds a parent job with children; the parent runs only after all children complete,\n  which models \"embed 500 chunks, then write the document summary\".\n\n### Job lifecycle\nA job is always in exactly one state, and each state is a Redis structure: `waiting` (a list),\n`prioritized` and `delayed` (sorted sets ordered by priority or due time), `active` (a list plus a\nper-job lock key), `waiting-children` (a parent blocked on its flow), and the terminal `completed` and\n`failed` sets. Every transition (claim, complete, fail and reschedule, promote a delayed job) is one Lua\nscript *because* it touches several keys at once, and a crash between \"remove from waiting\" and \"add to\nactive\" would otherwise lose or duplicate the job. Reading the queue's health is therefore reading the\nsizes of these sets: a growing `waiting` means too few workers or a limiter that is too tight; a growing\n`failed` means a bug or a dependency that is down; a non-empty `active` with nobody working means stalls.\n\n### Retries, backoff, delays\n`opts.attempts` with `opts.backoff: { type: 'exponential', delay: 1000 }` re-schedules a failed job\nafter 1 s, 2 s, 4 s and so on until `attempts` is exhausted, then leaves it in the failed set for\ninspection or manual retry. Delayed jobs sit in a sorted set keyed by timestamp and are promoted when\ndue *because* polling a list for \"is it time yet\" would waste Redis round trips. `priority` orders\nwaiting jobs; `removeOnComplete` and `removeOnFail` with a count or an age keep Redis from filling with\nhistory. Recurring work (nightly evals, hourly re-index) uses a job scheduler\n(`queue.upsertJobScheduler(id, { pattern: cron } or { every: ms }, template)` in recent BullMQ 5\nreleases, which supersedes the older repeatable-job API): the scheduler keeps one upcoming delayed job\nper schedule, so running several producer replicas does not multiply the runs.\n\n### Locks and stalled jobs\nWhen a worker claims a job it sets a lock with `lockDuration` and renews it on an interval while the\nprocessor runs. If the process dies, or the event loop is blocked long enough that renewal stops, the\nlock expires, a stall checker notices an active job with no lock and moves it back to waiting, up to\n`maxStalledCount` times before marking it failed. This is the mechanism that gives durability across\ncrashes, and it is also why a CPU-bound processor that blocks the event loop can get a job processed\ntwice: the renewal timer never fires. BullMQ's sandboxed processors (pass a file path instead of a\nfunction; the job runs in a child process or a worker thread) exist *because* of that failure mode.\n\n### Rate limiting for LLM providers\n`Worker` takes `limiter: { max, duration }` to cap jobs started per window across all workers of a\nqueue. For provider 429s, a processor can call `await queue.rateLimit(ms)` (in BullMQ 5 this replaces\nthe deprecated `worker.rateLimit`) and throw `Worker.RateLimitError()` so the job returns to the queue\nwithout consuming an attempt while the queue pauses for that window. Per-group limits (one quota per tenant) are a BullMQ Pro feature; the\nopen-source approach is a queue per tenant tier, or a Redis token bucket checked inside the processor.\n\n### Delivery semantics, and what BullMQ is not\nEvery path above is at-least-once: a job can run twice after a stall, after a crash between finishing\nthe work and moving the job to completed, or after a manual retry. Processors must therefore be\nidempotent, usually by keying side effects on `job.id` (a Postgres unique constraint, an idempotency key\non a downstream API). BullMQ is also not a workflow engine: it does not checkpoint the middle of a\nprocessor. A ten-step agent run should either be split into one job per step with state in Postgres, or\nrun under a durable-execution engine (Temporal, Inngest, Restate and similar) when you need replayable\nhistory, long timers and human-in-the-loop waits.","example":"An embedding pipeline: the API enqueues one job per document with a deterministic id (a double-submit is\nharmless), a worker with bounded concurrency and a provider rate limit processes it, and the processor\nis idempotent because the vector write keys on document id and chunk index. Each BullMQ object gets its\nown ioredis connection from a factory here; BullMQ also duplicates a Worker's connection internally for\nits blocking commands, and refuses a Worker connection whose `maxRetriesPerRequest` is not `null`.\n\n```ts\nimport IORedis from \"ioredis\";\nimport { Queue, Worker, QueueEvents } from \"bullmq\";\nconst conn = () => new IORedis(process.env.REDIS_URL!, { maxRetriesPerRequest: null });  // required for Workers\n\nexport const embedQueue = new Queue(\"embed\", { connection: conn(),\n  defaultJobOptions: { attempts: 5, backoff: { type: \"exponential\", delay: 2_000 },\n                       removeOnComplete: { count: 1_000 }, removeOnFail: { age: 7 * 24 * 3600 } } });\n\nexport async function enqueueEmbedding(docId: string, tenant: string) {\n  await embedQueue.add(\"embed-doc\", { docId, tenant }, { jobId: `embed-${docId}` });   // dedup by id (no ':' allowed)\n}\n\n// worker.ts — its own process; several replicas share the queue\nconst worker = new Worker(\"embed\", async (job) => {\n  const { docId } = job.data as { docId: string; tenant: string };\n  const chunks = await loadChunks(docId);\n  for (let i = 0; i < chunks.length; i++) {\n    if (await embeddingExists(docId, i)) continue;                   // resume, do not redo, after a retry\n    try {\n      const vec = await embed(chunks[i].text);                       // provider call\n      await upsertEmbedding(docId, i, vec);                           // idempotent: (docId, i) is unique\n    } catch (e: any) {\n      if (e.status === 429) { await embedQueue.rateLimit(30_000); throw Worker.RateLimitError(); }\n      throw e;                                                       // anything else: retry with backoff\n    }\n    await job.updateProgress(Math.round(((i + 1) / chunks.length) * 100));\n  }\n  return { chunks: chunks.length };\n}, { connection: conn(), concurrency: 4, limiter: { max: 50, duration: 60_000 } });\n\nworker.on(\"failed\", (job, err) => console.error(\"embed failed\", job?.id, job?.attemptsMade, err.message));\n\n// api.ts — stream progress to a client without being the worker\nconst events = new QueueEvents(\"embed\", { connection: conn() });\nevents.on(\"progress\", ({ jobId, data }) => notifyClient(jobId, data));\n```","tradeoffs":["Redis makes claim, renew and move operations microsecond-fast and lets any number of replicas share a queue, but durability equals Redis's: AOF and `noeviction` are mandatory for work you cannot re-enqueue, while a cache-style instance is fine for best-effort jobs.","Retries with exponential backoff absorb transient provider failures without code, but each retry re-runs the whole processor; a 500-chunk job failing at chunk 499 restarts at chunk 0 unless the processor checkpoints per chunk.","A worker-level `limiter` keeps a whole fleet under a provider's quota with one setting, but it is per queue, not per tenant or per model; fairness across tenants needs separate queues, BullMQ Pro groups, or an in-processor token bucket.","At-least-once delivery is simple and robust and forces idempotent processors; exactly-once is not on offer from the queue, and chasing it with locks inside the processor usually costs more than a unique constraint.","BullMQ is far lighter than a workflow engine and needs only Redis, but it has no durable mid-job state; once runs need long waits, signals or replay, Temporal-style engines earn their operational cost."],"pitfalls":["A processor that blocks the event loop (large synchronous JSON parse, vector math): the symptom is `stalled` events and jobs processed twice because the lock renewal timer could not fire. Move CPU work to a sandboxed processor or a worker thread.","Non-idempotent side effects (send email, charge budget, append message) keyed on nothing: the symptom is duplicates after a deploy or a stall, with every attempt logging success.","An ioredis connection with `maxRetriesPerRequest` left at its default (20): the symptom is a Worker throwing at construction when handed that instance (BullMQ only warns and overrides it when given plain options), or blocking waits erroring out during a Redis reconnect.","Relying on `jobId` for dedup while also using `removeOnComplete`: the symptom is the \"duplicate\" document being embedded again a few minutes after the first job finished, because the completed job (and its id) had already been deleted.","`removeOnComplete` left off in a high-volume queue: the symptom is Redis memory growing until eviction or out-of-memory, weeks after launch.","Treating `queue.add` as part of a database transaction: the symptom is jobs for rows that rolled back, or committed rows with no job; use an outbox table and a relay worker.","Exiting with `process.exit()` instead of `await worker.close()`: the symptom is active jobs re-run after every deploy once the stall checker catches them."],"when":"Use BullMQ when work outlives a request, must survive restarts, needs retries with backoff, or must be\nthrottled against an external quota: agent runs, ingestion, embedding, evaluation batches, webhook\ndelivery, scheduled refreshes. Keep synchronous sub-second work in the request. If the team already has\nPostgres and low volume, `SKIP LOCKED` polling can be enough; if runs need durable multi-step state,\ntimers of hours or days, or human approval steps, reach for a durable-execution engine and keep BullMQ\nfor simple, idempotent units. Measure waiting-queue depth, job age at start, failure rate by attempt,\nstalled count and worker concurrency utilisation. Interviewers ask what happens when a worker dies\nmid-job and how you keep the job from doing its side effect twice.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Designs job payloads and ids, writes idempotent processors with per-chunk checkpoints, configures attempts, backoff, concurrency and limiter, and closes workers gracefully on SIGTERM.","architect":"Decides queue topology (per workload, per tenant tier), the Redis durability policy behind it, where the outbox sits, and when a workflow engine replaces the queue for long-running agent state.","fde":"Deploys workers inside the customer's environment, checks their Redis eviction and persistence settings, and tunes limiter values to the customer's negotiated provider quotas."},"check":{"q":"A worker embedding 2,000-chunk documents dies during a deploy. On restart, some documents are\nembedded twice and one job is marked failed after being \"stalled\" repeatedly. Explain what happened and\nwhat you change.","a":"When the process died, its jobs' locks expired, the stall checker moved them back to waiting and\nother workers re-ran them from the first chunk; because the upsert was not keyed on (document, chunk\nindex), the second pass wrote duplicate vectors. The repeatedly stalled job is a processor that blocks\nthe event loop, probably synchronous parsing or vector math, so lock renewal stops even though the\nprocess is alive; after `maxStalledCount` stalls BullMQ marks it failed. Changes: make the upsert\nidempotent with a unique key, check for existing chunks at the start of each iteration so a retry\nresumes instead of restarting, move CPU-heavy work to a sandboxed processor, and close workers with\n`await worker.close()` on SIGTERM so in-flight jobs finish before the pod is killed."},"quiz":[{"q":"What makes a job survive a worker process crash in BullMQ?","options":["The processor's promise is serialised to disk by Node","Job state lives in Redis and the lock expires, so the stall checker moves the active job back to waiting","Workers replicate in-flight jobs to each other over pub/sub","BullMQ writes a write-ahead log in the worker's filesystem"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Durability comes from Redis holding the job and from the lock-and-stall mechanism: a dead worker stops renewing its lock, and once it expires the job is returned to the queue for another worker, which is why handlers must be idempotent."},{"q":"Why does BullMQ require `maxRetriesPerRequest: null` on a worker's ioredis connection?","options":["To disable job retries, which BullMQ manages itself","Because Redis Cluster does not support per-request retries","So that commands, including the worker's long blocking waits, queue until Redis reconnects instead of failing after a fixed number of reconnection attempts","To allow the connection to be shared with pub/sub subscribers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"ioredis's `maxRetriesPerRequest` fails pending commands after N reconnection attempts (20 by default); a worker's loop is built around long blocking commands that must survive a reconnect rather than reject, so BullMQ insists on `null` (wait indefinitely) for worker connections."},{"q":"Which design keeps a 500-chunk embedding job from restarting at chunk 0 after a transient failure at chunk 499?","options":["Increase `attempts` so the job eventually gets through","Lower `concurrency` so fewer jobs fail at once","Make each chunk's write idempotent and check for existing chunks so a retry skips work already stored","Use `priority: 1` so the job is retried first"],"answer":2,"explanation":"BullMQ retries re-run the whole processor; only application-level checkpoints (idempotent upserts plus an existence check at the start of each chunk) turn a retry into a resume. More attempts just repeat the same 499 calls."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers","sources":["Nygard, 2007 — Release It!","Brooker, 2015 — Exponential Backoff And Jitter (AWS Architecture Blog)","Official docs: Anthropic TypeScript SDK","Official docs: OpenAI Node SDK"],"why":"A naive client does `await fetch(url)` and throws on error. In production that produces three distinct\nfailures. First, latency with no bound: a provider that accepts the TCP connection and then stalls\nleaves your handler waiting until the socket gives up, which for Node's built-in `fetch` (undici, with\n300-second header and body timeouts) is measured in minutes by default; a few hundred of those and every worker slot, pooled DB connection and queue lock is\nheld by a request going nowhere. Second, transient errors surface as user-facing failures: a 429 from a shared\nquota or a 529 `overloaded_error` from Anthropic often clears within seconds, so not retrying throws\naway a request that would have succeeded. Third, the opposite naive fix, \"retry everything, immediately,\nfive times\", is worse: during a provider incident a thousand clients retrying in lockstep multiply the\nload five-fold at exactly the wrong moment, synchronised by the same schedule, and non-idempotent calls\n(a tool that sends an email, a POST that creates a resource) execute more than once.\n\nRetries with backoff and jitter fix the second problem and defuse the third; timeouts fix the first;\nidempotency keys make retries safe; and the circuit breaker handles the case backoff cannot. When the\ndependency is down for minutes, the correct behaviour is not \"wait 30 s then fail\" per request, it is\n\"fail in a millisecond and try again later\", which protects your own latency budget, frees resources\nand gives the provider room to recover.","how":"### Timeouts first\nEvery attempt gets an `AbortSignal` with a per-attempt timeout (`AbortSignal.timeout(ms)` in current\nNode, or an `AbortController` you cancel), *because* a retry policy without a timeout retries nothing\nwhile the first attempt hangs. Time-to-first-token is a different number from total completion time:\nfor streaming calls put the timeout on the connection and first chunk, then an idle timeout between\nchunks, not one giant budget that must cover a 90-second answer. Carry a request-level deadline through\nthe call chain so attempt timeouts plus backoff never exceed what the caller can wait for.\n\n### Classify before retrying\nRetry only what can succeed on a second try: 408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504, Anthropic's 529 and network\nerrors. Do not retry 400, 401, 403, 404 or 422: the request is wrong and will be wrong again. Network\nerrors split by *when* they happened. A refused connection or a connect timeout (`ECONNREFUSED`,\n`UND_ERR_CONNECT_TIMEOUT`) means the request never reached the server, so it is always safe to retry. A\nreset or timeout after the request was sent (`ECONNRESET`, a read timeout, a socket closed mid-body) is\nambiguous: the server may have processed it and the reply was lost. Treat those as retryable only if the\noperation is idempotent, *because* the first attempt may have succeeded server-side after you stopped\nlistening, and an LLM call that completed is billed whether or not you received it.\n\n### Exponential backoff with full jitter\nAttempt *n* sleeps `random(0, min(cap, base × 2^n))`. The exponential term backs off fast enough to help\na recovering provider; the cap keeps the wait sane; the jitter spreads clients out *because* identical\nschedules re-synchronise the thundering herd on every attempt. Honour `Retry-After` (seconds or an HTTP\ndate; the Anthropic and OpenAI SDKs also read a millisecond variant) when present: the server knows its\nown recovery window better than your formula. Bound by both attempt count and total elapsed time.\n\n### Idempotency\nA retried POST that creates a run, sends a message or charges a budget must be safe. Pass an idempotency\nkey the server deduplicates on (many APIs accept an `Idempotency-Key` header), or make the operation\nnaturally idempotent (PUT by id, upsert). If neither is possible, do not retry automatically; surface the\nambiguity to the caller.\n\n### The vendor SDKs\nThe Anthropic TypeScript SDK retries connection errors, 408, 409, 429 and 5xx with backoff, honours\n`Retry-After`, and exposes `maxRetries` (default 2) and `timeout` (default 10 minutes, in milliseconds;\nfor a non-streaming call whose `max_tokens` implies a generation longer than that, the SDK refuses and\nasks you to stream unless you set `timeout` explicitly). A timed-out attempt is itself retried, so wall-clock time can reach roughly `timeout × (maxRetries + 1)`\nplus backoff, which is the number to put in your deadline arithmetic. The OpenAI Node SDK follows the same design with the\nsame defaults. Errors come back as typed classes (`Anthropic.RateLimitError` for 429,\n`Anthropic.InternalServerError` for 5xx including 529, `Anthropic.APIConnectionError`, all subclasses\nof `Anthropic.APIError` with `.status`, `.headers`, the parsed body on `.error` and `.type` such as\n`overloaded_error`), so classification is an `instanceof` chain plus a status check, not string\nmatching. The right move is\nto configure those options rather than wrap the SDK in a second retry loop, *because* nested retries\nmultiply: 3 × 3 = 9 attempts against a provider that is already overloaded.\n\n### Circuit breaker\nA breaker wraps a dependency and keeps a small amount of state:\n1. **Closed** (normal): calls pass through; failures are counted in a rolling window.\n2. **Open**: once failures exceed a threshold (say, over half of at least 20 calls in 10 s), the breaker\n   rejects calls immediately for a `resetTimeout` without touching the dependency.\n3. **Half-open**: after the timeout, one trial call is let through; success closes the breaker, failure\n   re-opens it.\n\nIt trips on rate over a minimum volume, not on a single failure, *because* one bad response is noise; it\nrejects fast *because* a queued request that will fail anyway costs latency and a connection slot; and\nit probes instead of flipping back blindly *because* you want evidence of recovery before releasing the\nfull load. A breaker needs a fallback: a smaller model, a cached answer, a degraded response, or a clear\nerror with a `Retry-After` for your own callers. In Node, `opossum` is the common breaker library;\n`cockatiel` composes retry, timeout, breaker and bulkhead policies; `p-retry` covers retry alone.\n\nBreaker state is per process. With 20 replicas, each one counts its own failures, trips on its own\nschedule and sends its own half-open probe, so the provider sees up to 20 probes per reset period. That\nis usually acceptable and keeps the breaker free of dependencies; sharing state through Redis makes the\nfleet trip together, but puts a network call in front of every request to protect against a network\nfailure.\n\n### Bulkheads\nLimit concurrent in-flight calls per dependency (`p-limit` or a semaphore) so one slow provider cannot\nconsume every worker slot. For LLM providers this doubles as a way to stay under concurrency-based\nquotas, and backed by Redis it works across replicas.\n\n### Retry budgets\nPer-request attempt limits do not bound fleet-wide amplification: if every request may make 3 attempts,\nan outage can triple traffic. A retry budget caps retries as a fraction of recent successful or total\nrequests (for example, at most one retry per ten requests over the last minute, an illustrative ratio\nto tune): during a blip almost every failure gets its retry, during an outage the budget empties and\nfurther failures surface immediately. It is the per-client analogue of a breaker, and some service\nmeshes and gRPC clients implement it as retry throttling.\n\n### Composing the layers\nOrder matters, from the outside in: request deadline, then bulkhead, then breaker, then retry with\nbackoff, then the per-attempt timeout around the actual call. The deadline is outermost *because* it is\nthe caller's promise and every inner wait must fit inside it. The breaker sits outside the retry loop\n*because* one exhausted retry sequence is one logical failure; inside, it would count each attempt and\ntrip three times faster than intended, and an open breaker should stop the retries from starting at\nall. The timeout is innermost *because* it bounds a single attempt, so a hung attempt becomes a\nclassifiable error the retry layer can act on.","example":"A minimal `fetchWithRetry` with per-attempt timeout (combined with the caller's own signal),\nerror classification that retries ambiguous failures (timeouts, resets) only for idempotent requests, full-jitter backoff and\n`Retry-After`, plus a small consecutive-failure circuit breaker that lets exactly one probe through when\nhalf-open. Production code uses the SDK's built-in retries and `opossum` or `cockatiel`, but the\nmechanics are these. After the last retryable status the response is returned, not thrown, so the\nbreaker's wrapped function must turn 5xx and 429 into failures itself, as the usage line shows.\n\n```ts\nconst RETRYABLE = new Set([408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504, 529]);\nconst NEVER_SENT = [\"ECONNREFUSED\", \"UND_ERR_CONNECT_TIMEOUT\"];           // safe for any method\nconst MAYBE_SENT = [\"ECONNRESET\", \"ETIMEDOUT\", \"UND_ERR_SOCKET\"];         // idempotent requests only\nconst sleep = (ms: number) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));\nconst jitter = (n: number, base: number, cap: number) => Math.random() * Math.min(cap, base * 2 ** n);\n\nexport async function fetchWithRetry(url: string, init: RequestInit,\n                                     o = { attempts: 4, baseMs: 500, capMs: 8_000, attemptTimeoutMs: 20_000 }) {\n  const idempotent = [\"GET\", \"HEAD\", \"PUT\", \"DELETE\"].includes(init.method ?? \"GET\")\n    || new Headers(init.headers).has(\"idempotency-key\");\n  for (let n = 0; ; n++) {\n    const timeout = AbortSignal.timeout(o.attemptTimeoutMs);\n    try {\n      const res = await fetch(url, { ...init, signal: init.signal ? AbortSignal.any([init.signal, timeout]) : timeout });\n      if (!RETRYABLE.has(res.status) || n + 1 >= o.attempts) return res;     // success, non-retryable, or out of attempts\n      await res.body?.cancel();                                               // free the socket before retrying\n      const retryAfter = Number(res.headers.get(\"retry-after\"));             // seconds form; an HTTP date falls back to jitter\n      await sleep(retryAfter > 0 ? retryAfter * 1000 : jitter(n, o.baseMs, o.capMs));\n    } catch (err: any) {\n      const code = err?.cause?.code;                                          // undici wraps socket errors in err.cause\n      const transient = NEVER_SENT.includes(code) ||\n        (idempotent && (err?.name === \"TimeoutError\" || MAYBE_SENT.includes(code)));\n      if (!transient || n + 1 >= o.attempts) throw err;                       // caller aborts (AbortError) are never retried\n      await sleep(jitter(n, o.baseMs, o.capMs));\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nexport class CircuitBreaker {\n  private failures = 0; private openedAt = 0; private state: \"closed\" | \"open\" | \"half\" = \"closed\";\n  constructor(private threshold = 5, private resetMs = 30_000) {}\n  async call<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>, fallback: () => T): Promise<T> {\n    if (this.state !== \"closed\") {\n      if (this.state === \"half\" || Date.now() - this.openedAt < this.resetMs) return fallback();  // fail fast; one probe at a time\n      this.state = \"half\";                                                      // this caller is the probe\n    }\n    try {\n      const out = await fn();\n      this.failures = 0; this.state = \"closed\"; return out;\n    } catch (e) {\n      if (++this.failures >= this.threshold || this.state === \"half\") { this.state = \"open\"; this.openedAt = Date.now(); }\n      throw e;\n    }\n  }\n}\n// await breaker.call(async () => { const r = await fetchWithRetry(url, init);\n//   if (r.status >= 500 || r.status === 429) throw new Error(`upstream ${r.status}`); return r; }, () => degraded());\n```","tradeoffs":["Retries raise success rate during blips but multiply load during outages; with jitter and a cap on total time the amplification is bounded, without them a fleet-wide retry storm can keep a provider down.","Aggressive per-attempt timeouts keep latency predictable but cut off slow-yet-successful long completions; for large outputs use a time-to-first-token timeout plus an idle timeout rather than one short total budget.","A circuit breaker protects your latency and the provider, but a mis-tuned threshold trips on normal error noise and takes a healthy dependency offline; thresholds should be rate-based over a minimum call volume.","Falling back to a cheaper model or a cached answer keeps the product up at a quality cost users may notice; whether that beats a clean error depends on the task (a chat assistant can degrade, a compliance extraction probably cannot).","A retry budget bounds fleet-wide amplification during outages, but it also denies retries to some requests during a genuine blip that a per-request policy would have saved; it flips from worth it to overhead when traffic is low enough that amplification cannot hurt the provider.","The SDK's built-in retry is simpler and correct for its own error classes, but it knows nothing about your deadline or breaker; the integration point is its `maxRetries` and `timeout` options, not a wrapper loop around it."],"pitfalls":["Nested retry loops (SDK retries inside your retries inside the queue's retries): the symptom is a single user action producing dozens of provider calls during an incident, visible as a cost spike in the same hour as the outage.","Retrying non-idempotent POSTs on timeout or `ECONNRESET`: the symptom is duplicate runs, messages or charges that each carry a different request id but trace back to one user click.","No jitter: the symptom is provider error rates oscillating in neat waves at your backoff intervals, as every client retries at the same instant.","Breaker without a minimum volume: the symptom is the breaker opening at 03:00 because two of the night's three requests failed.","Ignoring `Retry-After`: the symptom is 429s that keep coming despite backoff, because the server told you its window and you retried earlier than it.","Treating a stream that failed after the first tokens as retryable and replaying it into the same response: the symptom is clients receiving an answer that restarts in the middle."],"when":"Every outbound call to an LLM, embedding, vector, search or SaaS API gets a timeout and a classified\nretry policy; this is the baseline, not an optimisation. Add a circuit breaker per dependency when the\nservice has a meaningful fallback or when the dependency's failure would otherwise exhaust worker\ncapacity; add a bulkhead when one slow dependency can starve the others. Do not retry inside a request\npath when a queue above it already retries; decide which layer owns retries and make the others fail\nfast. Measure attempts per request, retry success rate, breaker state transitions, p99 latency with and\nwithout retries, and duplicate side effects. In interviews, derive the full-jitter formula, explain why\nbreakers trip on rate, and say which layer should own retries.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures SDK `maxRetries` and `timeout`, wraps tool calls with `cockatiel` or `opossum`, passes `AbortSignal`s and idempotency keys, and writes tests that simulate 429s, 529s and timeouts.","architect":"Assigns retry ownership per layer (client, service, queue), defines breaker thresholds and fallbacks per provider, and sets the latency budget each dependency is allowed to consume.","fde":"Tunes timeouts and limits to a customer's negotiated quotas and their egress proxy's behaviour, and explains why their incident dashboard shows retry waves."},"check":{"q":"During a provider incident your service's p99 latency went from 3 s to 90 s and your bill\ntripled for the hour, even though the provider was mostly returning errors. Explain the mechanics and\nthe fix.","a":"Each attempt hung for tens of seconds on a provider that accepted connections but failed slowly\n(the SDK's 10-minute default timeout never fired, so nothing cut the wait short), then the SDK retried\ntwice, then the handler's own loop retried three times, and the queue above re-enqueued failures: a\nsingle user action became a dozen or more slow attempts (3 SDK attempts × 4 handler attempts before the\nqueue even acts), which explains the latency. The cost comes from the attempts that did complete\nserver-side, and are billed, after your client had already timed out or given up, plus work the queue\nre-ran after it had in fact succeeded. The fix is layered: a\nper-attempt timeout sized for the call, a single owner of retries (the SDK with `maxRetries` set and\nnothing above it), full-jitter backoff honouring `Retry-After`, a total deadline per request, and a\ncircuit breaker per provider that opens on a high failure rate and fails fast with a fallback or a clear\nerror. Idempotency keys make any remaining retries safe to repeat."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why add random jitter to exponential backoff rather than retrying at fixed exponential intervals?","options":["Jitter makes individual retries succeed more often","Without jitter, clients that failed together retry together, so each wave of retries hits the provider at the same instant","Jitter is required by the HTTP specification for 429 responses","It reduces the total number of attempts per request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Backoff alone keeps the schedule synchronised across clients; randomising each sleep spreads the retries over the window so the provider sees a smooth load instead of spikes that re-trigger the overload."},{"q":"A circuit breaker is in the open state. What happens to a new call?","options":["It is queued until the dependency recovers","It is retried with backoff until the reset timeout elapses","It is rejected immediately (or served a fallback) without contacting the dependency","It is sent, and the breaker closes if it succeeds"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Open means \"known bad\": the breaker fails fast to protect latency and the dependency, and only after the reset timeout does it move to half-open and allow a single probe call."},{"q":"Which response should a well-behaved client NOT retry automatically?","options":["503 Service Unavailable with a `Retry-After` header","A connection reset before any response bytes arrived","400 Bad Request caused by an over-long prompt","429 Too Many Requests from a shared rate limit"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A 400 means the request itself is invalid and will fail identically on every attempt; retrying only adds load and latency, whereas 503, 429 and a reset before any response bytes are transient signals that a later attempt can succeed."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation","sources":["Official docs: OpenTelemetry JavaScript","OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions — Generative AI","Official docs: Anthropic TypeScript SDK (OpenTelemetry tracing)"],"why":"An agent request in Node fans out: an HTTP handler calls a planner model, which calls three tools, two\nof which hit Postgres and one of which enqueues a BullMQ job that calls an embedding API on another\nreplica. When that request takes 40 s or costs 60,000 tokens, logs from six processes tell you *that*\nit happened but not *why*, because nothing ties the lines together and nothing records durations as a\ntree. Hand-rolled solutions, a `requestId` threaded through every function and `console.time` pairs,\nbreak at the first `await` you forgot, do not cross the queue, and produce data no tool can draw as a\nwaterfall.\n\nNode makes the problem sharper than threaded runtimes do: there is no thread to hang context on, so\n\"which request is this log line from\" must travel along the async continuation chain. OTel's Node SDK\nsolves that with `AsyncLocalStorage`, propagates context across processes with standard headers, and\nemits the result in a format every backend (Jaeger, Tempo, Honeycomb, Datadog, Langfuse and others via\nOTLP) accepts. The vendor-neutral part matters *because* the backend you pick in year one is rarely the\none you have in year three, and because enterprise customers want your spans in *their* collector. The\nobservability module covers the concepts; this lesson is the Node mechanics.","how":"### API, SDK and the split between them\nYour code calls the API (`@opentelemetry/api`): `trace.getTracer(name).startActiveSpan(...)`,\n`metrics.getMeter(...)`. The SDK (`@opentelemetry/sdk-node`, or `NodeTracerProvider` directly) is\nconfigured once and decides what happens: sampling, batching, exporting. Libraries depend only on the\nAPI *because* then a library can be instrumented without forcing an SDK or an exporter on its users;\nwith no SDK registered the API calls are no-ops.\n\n### Startup order and auto-instrumentation\nAuto-instrumentation works by patching modules (`http`, `undici`/`fetch`, `pg`, `ioredis`, `express`,\n`fastify`, `pino` and others) when they are loaded. It must therefore be installed *before* those\nmodules are imported, which is why the SDK lives in its own `instrumentation.ts` loaded with\n`node --require` (CommonJS) or `node --import` (ESM), or via `NODE_OPTIONS`. ESM needs one more piece:\n`import` statements bypass the CommonJS `require` hook, so the file must also register OTel's ESM\nloader hook (`module.register(\"@opentelemetry/instrumentation/hook.mjs\", ...)`), which the zero-code\nentry point `@opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node/register` does for you. Importing it from\n`app.ts` after your framework is a classic silent failure: no error, no spans.\n\n### Spans, kinds and the tree\nA span is a named, timed operation with a trace id (shared by the whole request), its own span id, a\nparent span id, attributes, events (timestamped points such as \"first token\") and a status. The tree\nis reconstructed by the backend from parent ids alone, which is why a missing parent link produces an\norphan root rather than an error. Each span has a kind: `SERVER` for handling an inbound request,\n`CLIENT` for an outbound call, `PRODUCER` and `CONSUMER` for the two ends of a queue, `INTERNAL` for\nin-process work such as an agent step. Kinds matter *because* backends derive service maps and\nlatency breakdowns from them: a `CLIENT` span with no matching `SERVER` child is time spent waiting on\nsomething you do not instrument, such as a model provider.\n\n### Context propagation\nWithin a process the SDK installs an `AsyncLocalStorage`-based context manager so the active span\nfollows `await`, promises, timers and callbacks. `AsyncLocalStorage` works by capturing the current\nstore whenever Node creates an async resource (a promise, a timer, a socket callback) and restoring it\nwhen that resource's continuation runs, so \"the active span\" is whatever was active when the\ncontinuation was *scheduled*. That rule explains where context gets lost: a library that collects\ncallbacks in its own queue and invokes them later from a different continuation (a hand-rolled\nconnection pool, a batching client) runs them in the wrong context, and an `EventEmitter` listener runs\nin the context of whoever called `emit()`, not whoever registered it. The fix is to bind explicitly with\n`context.bind(context.active(), fn)` (or `AsyncResource.bind`) at the point the callback is handed\nover. Across HTTP it injects and extracts `traceparent` and\n`tracestate` (W3C Trace Context); the `http` and `fetch` instrumentations do this for every outbound\nand inbound request. Across a queue nothing is automatic unless the library supports it (BullMQ 5 has an opt-in `telemetry`\noption, provided by the `bullmq-otel` package, that does this): otherwise serialise the\ncontext into job data on the producer (`propagation.inject(context.active(), carrier)`) and restore it\nin the worker (`propagation.extract(...)`) so the job's spans link to the originating request.\n\n### Spans for model calls\nThe Anthropic TypeScript SDK emits its own span per request, `anthropic.messages.create` (kind\n`CLIENT`), through whatever tracer provider the application registered, carrying\n`gen_ai.provider.name`, `gen_ai.usage.input_tokens` (cache reads and writes included),\n`gen_ai.usage.output_tokens`, the cache read and write counts, and `anthropic.request.id`; its tool\nrunner adds a parent span plus one span per tool it runs. The feature is in beta, so pin the SDK\nversion. Two rules follow. Wrap the call in a span of your own (`agent.plan`, `tool.search_docs`) with\napp-level attributes (tenant, run id, prompt version) and the SDK span nests under it; the SDK then\nsends its own span's `traceparent` to the API, so server-side spans (if API tracing is configured) join\nthe same trace. And if a third-party instrumentation already patches the SDK,\nturn one of the two off (`openTelemetry: false` or `ANTHROPIC_OPEN_TELEMETRY=false`) or every request\nappears twice. For providers whose SDK emits nothing, set the GenAI semantic-convention attributes\nyourself (`gen_ai.provider.name`, the request model, `gen_ai.usage.*`); the conventions are still\nmarked experimental, so expect renames.\n\nPrompt and completion text stay out of spans by default: the SDK's content mode is `metadata_only`, and\n`content` mode exists *because* reading conversations from traces is useful but makes your tracing\nbackend a store of customer data under its own retention rules, with a per-span size limit (0.75 MB by\ndefault) beyond which content is truncated. Treat that as an explicit opt-in, or send content to an\nLLM-specific tool (Langfuse, Phoenix) built for it.\n\n### Streaming\nA streaming call's span ends when the stream is fully read or closed, so close a stream you abandon or\nits span never ends. Spans you start while reading chunks are siblings of the SDK span, not children;\nstart one span of your own around both the call and the read loop, and add an event at the first token\nso time-to-first-token is queryable.\n\n### Metrics and logs\nUse a histogram for model latency and a counter for tokens by model and tenant; these aggregate cheaply\nwhere traces are sampled. Pino or Winston with the OTel log instrumentation gets `trace_id` and\n`span_id` on every line, which is what lets you jump from a log to the trace.\n\n### Export, sampling, cost\nSpans go through a `BatchSpanProcessor` to an OTLP exporter (HTTP or gRPC), usually to an OpenTelemetry\nCollector sidecar or gateway that fans out to backends, redacts, and does tail sampling. The batch\nprocessor exists *because* exporting each span synchronously would put a network call on every\noperation: ended spans go into an in-memory queue (2,048 spans by default) and are exported in batches\nof up to 512 every 5 seconds or when a batch fills. When the exporter cannot keep up, the queue fills\nand new spans are dropped, not blocked, which protects request latency at the cost of silently missing\ndata; tune `OTEL_BSP_MAX_QUEUE_SIZE` and watch the dropped count. Use the `SimpleSpanProcessor`\n(export on end) only in tests. Head sampling\nin the SDK (`parentbased_traceidratio` at, say, 10%) cuts cost; the `parentbased` part means a service\nfollows the sampled flag in the incoming `traceparent` and only applies its own ratio at the root,\n*because* independent coin flips per service would produce traces with random holes. Head sampling\ndecides before anything has gone wrong, so it throws away 90% of the rare slow or failed traces along with the boring ones; tail\nsampling in the collector keeps errors and slow traces *because* it decides after seeing the whole\ntrace. Configuration is environment-driven: `OTEL_SERVICE_NAME`, `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT`,\n`OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER`, `OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES`. On shutdown call `sdk.shutdown()` so the batch\nprocessor flushes; otherwise the last spans of every deploy vanish.","example":"`instrumentation.ts` configures the SDK and is loaded before the app with\n`node --import ./instrumentation.js app.js` (for a CommonJS build, use `--require` and drop the two\n`register` lines, which only an ES module can run). The second block shows an\napplication span around a model call; the Anthropic SDK's own `anthropic.messages.create` span, with\ntoken usage, nests under it automatically because the SDK uses the registered provider.\n\n```ts\n// instrumentation.ts — loaded first, never imported by application code except for shutdown\nimport { register } from \"node:module\";\nregister(\"@opentelemetry/instrumentation/hook.mjs\", import.meta.url);  // ESM only: lets patches reach `import`ed modules\nimport { NodeSDK } from \"@opentelemetry/sdk-node\";\nimport { getNodeAutoInstrumentations } from \"@opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node\";\nimport { OTLPTraceExporter } from \"@opentelemetry/exporter-trace-otlp-http\";\n\nexport const sdk = new NodeSDK({\n  traceExporter: new OTLPTraceExporter(),            // endpoint from OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT\n  instrumentations: [getNodeAutoInstrumentations({ \"@opentelemetry/instrumentation-fs\": { enabled: false } })],\n});\nsdk.start();                                          // registers the provider and the AsyncLocalStorage context manager\n\n// llm.ts — your span carries app semantics; the SDK span carries gen_ai.* usage\nimport { trace, SpanStatusCode } from \"@opentelemetry/api\";\nimport Anthropic from \"@anthropic-ai/sdk\";\nconst tracer = trace.getTracer(\"agent-api\");\nconst client = new Anthropic();                       // SDK tracing on by default, metadata only: no prompt text exported\n\nexport async function plan(tenant: string, runId: string, prompt: string) {\n  return tracer.startActiveSpan(\"agent.plan\", async (span) => {\n    span.setAttributes({ \"app.tenant\": tenant, \"app.run_id\": runId, \"app.prompt_version\": \"planner-v7\" });\n    try {\n      const res = await client.messages.create(\n        { model: process.env.MODEL ?? \"claude-opus-5-5\", max_tokens: 16000, messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: prompt }] },\n        { openTelemetry: { conversationId: runId } },   // written to gen_ai.conversation.id on the SDK span\n      );\n      span.setAttribute(\"app.stop_reason\", res.stop_reason ?? \"unknown\");\n      return res.content.flatMap((b) => (b.type === \"text\" ? [b.text] : [])).join(\"\");\n    } catch (e: any) {\n      span.recordException(e); span.setStatus({ code: SpanStatusCode.ERROR, message: e.message });\n      throw e;\n    } finally {\n      span.end();                                     // always end, or the span never exports\n    }\n  });\n}\n```","tradeoffs":["Auto-instrumentation gives HTTP, DB and Redis spans with zero code changes, but it patches modules and must load first; it also instruments noisy things (`fs`, DNS) that you will want to disable to control volume.","Head sampling at the SDK is cheap and predictable, but drops rare slow or failed traces; tail sampling in a collector keeps them at the cost of running and sizing the collector.","Rich span attributes (tenant, model, tokens, prompt version) make traces queryable for cost and quality questions, but attributes are billed per byte by most vendors, and prompt content in spans is a data-protection liability that needs an explicit decision.","Linking queue jobs to their originating request through injected context gives end-to-end traces, but requires every producer and consumer to carry the carrier; one unlinked hop breaks the tree into orphans.","LLM-specific observability tools (Langfuse, Phoenix, LangSmith) understand prompts, evals and sessions in ways generic tracing does not, while OTel is the common wire; sending OTel spans to such a tool via OTLP avoids instrumenting twice."],"pitfalls":["SDK initialised after the framework or `pg` is imported: the symptom is a trace with only your manual spans, no HTTP or DB children, and no error anywhere.","ESM app started with `--require`: the symptom is auto-instrumentation silently doing nothing because ESM modules bypass the CommonJS hook; use `--import` with the register entry.","Spans never ended on an error path (missing `finally`), or a streaming response abandoned without closing it: the symptom is traces that look incomplete or never export, and memory growing with open spans.","No `sdk.shutdown()` on SIGTERM: the symptom is the last few seconds of spans missing before every deploy, which is exactly when errors happen.","Both the vendor SDK's built-in tracing and a third-party instrumentation of that SDK active: the symptom is every model call appearing twice in the trace with identical timing.","Context lost across a callback queue or an `EventEmitter`: the symptom is DB or tool spans that attach to the wrong request's trace, or start new root traces, only under concurrency, because the callback ran in whichever context was active when it was invoked.","Trace context not propagated into BullMQ jobs: the symptom is worker spans that form their own root traces, unlinked from the API request that caused them."],"when":"Instrument every service that calls a model, from the first deployment; retrofitting traces during an\nincident is the worst time. Use auto-instrumentation for transport and storage, the vendor SDK's spans\nfor model calls, manual spans for anything semantically meaningful (agent step, tool call, retrieval),\nmetrics for cost and latency aggregates, and logs with trace ids for detail. Reach for an LLM-specific\ntool when you need prompt versioning, evals and human review, and feed it through OTLP. Measure spans\nper request, export errors, dropped spans from the batch processor, and whether every error trace is\nkept. Interviewers ask how context crosses `await` in Node, why auto-instrumentation must load first,\nand what you would put on a model-call span.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Owns `instrumentation.ts`, wraps agent steps and tool calls in spans with tenant and run attributes, propagates context into jobs, and ensures spans end and flush on every path.","architect":"Chooses the collector topology, sampling policy, attribute and content-mode rules, and whether an LLM observability product sits beside or behind the OTel pipeline.","fde":"Points the exporter at the customer's collector, negotiates which attributes may leave their environment, and uses traces to show where latency and tokens go in their workflow."},"check":{"q":"Traces from your Node agent API show the HTTP server span and your manual spans, but no\nPostgres or Redis spans, and worker-side spans appear as separate traces. What is wrong and how do you\nfix both?","a":"Missing `pg` and `ioredis` spans almost always mean the SDK was initialised after those modules\nwere loaded, so the patches never applied; the fix is a separate instrumentation file loaded via\n`--require` or, for ESM, `--import` with the auto-instrumentation register hook, before any\napplication import. The worker traces are orphans because queue hops do not propagate context on their\nown: on the producer, call `propagation.inject(context.active(), carrier)` and store the carrier in the\njob data; in the worker, `propagation.extract` it and start the processing span inside that context so\nit becomes a child (or a linked span) of the API request. Verify with one request and confirm a single\ntrace spans API, database, Redis and worker."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must the OpenTelemetry SDK be loaded before importing your web framework and database client?","options":["Because the SDK reserves port 4318 before the server starts","Auto-instrumentation patches modules when they are first loaded, so modules imported earlier are never wrapped","Because spans need a root span created at process start","Because the exporter needs the framework's config to resolve the endpoint"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Instrumentations hook the module loader; anything required before the hook is installed is the original, unpatched module, which is why the SDK lives in a file passed via `--require` or `--import`."},{"q":"How does the active span follow an `await` in Node when there is no thread to attach it to?","options":["Spans are stored in a global variable that the SDK resets per request","The event loop tags each callback with the span id","The SDK uses `AsyncLocalStorage` so context flows along the async continuation chain","Each promise carries the span in its prototype"],"answer":2,"explanation":"OTel's Node context manager stores the active context in `AsyncLocalStorage`, which Node propagates through promises, timers and callbacks, so code after an `await` still sees the right parent span."},{"q":"Which is the best default for recording model calls in spans?","options":["Store full prompt and completion text as attributes on every span","Record model, token usage, finish reason and tenant as attributes, and keep content out unless explicitly opted in","Record only the HTTP status, since the auto-instrumented span already exists","Skip spans for model calls and rely on provider dashboards"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Token and model attributes make cost and latency queryable at low volume; prompt text is large, sensitive and billed per byte, so it belongs behind an opt-in content mode or in a purpose-built LLM observability store."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/graceful-shutdown","sources":["Official docs: Node.js (process, http)","Official docs: Kubernetes — Pod Lifecycle","Official docs: BullMQ"],"why":"Kubernetes replaces a pod by sending SIGTERM and, after `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` (30 s by\ndefault), SIGKILL. `docker stop` does the same with a 10 s default. In a Node process with no handler,\nSIGTERM either terminates it immediately or, when Node is PID 1 in the container, is ignored outright\n(the kernel drops signals that PID 1 has no handler for), so the pod sits until SIGKILL. Either way\nnothing drains: an SSE stream halfway through a 2,000-token answer closes without a\nfinal event, a BullMQ worker mid-way through a 500-chunk embedding loses its place and the job stalls\nand re-runs, a Postgres transaction is rolled back by the server when the socket drops, and the last\nbatch of OpenTelemetry spans, which are precisely the ones covering the deploy, is never exported.\n\nThe naive fix, `process.on('SIGTERM', () => server.close(() => process.exit(0)))`, is better and still\nwrong in three ways. First, Kubernetes removes the pod from the Service's endpoints asynchronously with\nsending SIGTERM, so for a short window new requests still arrive at a server that has stopped accepting;\nyou see connection errors at the load balancer on every deploy. Second, `server.close()` waits for every\nopen connection, and on older Node versions that includes idle keep-alive connections from the load\nbalancer, so the process can hang until SIGKILL. Third, closing the HTTP server says nothing about the\nworker, the pool, the Redis client or the telemetry exporter, each of which has its own flush and close\nsemantics and must be closed in the right order. Graceful shutdown is the explicit choreography of all\nof that against a deadline.","how":"### What the orchestrator does\n1. The pod is marked terminating and is taken out of the Service's ready endpoints straight away,\n   whatever its readiness probe says; that removal then propagates to kube-proxy rules and ingress\n   controllers with a delay of typically a few seconds, during which they still route to the pod.\n2. The container's `preStop` hook runs, if any; a common one is `sleep 5` to let endpoint removal\n   propagate before the signal arrives (the exec form needs a `sleep` binary in the image; recent\n   Kubernetes versions also offer a built-in `sleep` preStop action). The hook's time counts against\n   the grace period.\n3. SIGTERM is sent to PID 1 of the container.\n4. After the grace period, SIGKILL.\n\nTwo consequences: the process must actually receive the signal (use the exec form\n`CMD [\"node\", \"dist/server.js\"]`; the shell form `CMD npm start` leaves a shell as PID 1 that does not\nforward signals, and `docker run --init` or `tini` is the safe default), and the application's own\ndeadline must be shorter than the grace period minus the `preStop` sleep, *because* otherwise SIGKILL\nwins and the work is lost anyway.\n\n### The sequence inside Node\n1. **Flip readiness, then keep serving briefly.** Make `/ready` return 503 immediately so any balancer\n   that health-checks the pod directly stops routing; keep `/live` healthy, *because* a failing liveness\n   probe during shutdown would make the orchestrator restart the container mid-drain. In Kubernetes the\n   readiness flip is not what removes the pod from rotation (termination already did); what closes the\n   502 window is continuing to accept requests for a few seconds while the endpoint removal propagates.\n   Do that with a `preStop` sleep, or, if you cannot change the manifest, an in-app delay before\n   `server.close()`, but not both, since each costs grace period.\n2. **Stop intake.** `server.close()` stops accepting connections. Since Node 19, `close()` also closes\n   idle keep-alive connections; on older versions call `server.closeIdleConnections()` yourself. Keep\n   `keepAliveTimeout` short so balancers reconnect elsewhere. `close()` still waits for *active*\n   connections, and an SSE stream is never idle, so a server with open streams will not finish closing\n   until every stream ends; track open streams so the drain step can end them deliberately, and use\n   `server.closeAllConnections()` as the last resort when the deadline is near. For BullMQ, `await worker.close()` stops\n   taking new jobs and waits for active ones; `worker.pause()` is the softer form. Cancel scheduled\n   timers and polling loops.\n3. **Drain in flight.** Requests complete naturally. Streaming responses need a product decision:\n   finish them if they will end within the deadline, otherwise send a final event that tells the client\n   to resume or retry, and abort the upstream model call with an `AbortController` so you stop paying\n   for tokens nobody will receive.\n4. **Close resources in reverse dependency order.** Queue and Redis clients (`queue.close()`,\n   `redis.quit()`, which waits for pending replies, unlike `disconnect()`), then `pool.end()`, which\n   must come after every worker and request that might still query. Telemetry goes last\n   (`sdk.shutdown()` flushes the batch span processor): the exporter depends on none of the other\n   resources, and flushing last also exports the spans and errors of the close steps themselves. Give\n   the flush its own short timeout.\n5. **Exit explicitly.** Once a SIGTERM listener is registered, Node no longer exits on the signal by\n   default, so call `process.exit(0)` at the end (or set `process.exitCode` and let the loop drain).\n   Wrap the sequence in a deadline timer (`setTimeout(..., 20_000).unref()` for a 30 s grace period with a 5 s `preStop`) that exits non-zero if\n   draining hangs, *because* a hung shutdown wastes the whole grace period and still ends in SIGKILL.\n6. **Second signal means force.** A second SIGTERM or SIGINT during draining should exit immediately;\n   operators expect it.\n\n### Crashes\n`uncaughtException` and `unhandledRejection` leave the process in an unknown state. Log, then either\nflush telemetry and exit non-zero within a second or two, or run the normal drain under a short\ndeadline (as the example does); either way stop accepting work and exit non-zero, never keep serving. The orchestrator\nstarts a fresh instance, and the queue's stall detection re-runs the job, which is why jobs must be\nidempotent in the first place.\n\n### What \"done\" looks like\nDeploys produce zero 5xx at the load balancer, zero stalled jobs, no truncated streams in client logs,\nand the last trace before shutdown is present in the backend.","example":"A shutdown orchestrator for an API process that also runs a BullMQ worker. Readiness fails first, HTTP\nintake and the worker close in parallel, then Redis and Postgres close, and telemetry is flushed last\nunder its own 3-second cap. The 20-second deadline leaves margin under a 30-second grace period minus a\n5-second `preStop` sleep; the timer is `unref()`'d so it cannot keep an otherwise-finished process\nalive, and a second signal forces exit.\n\n```ts\nimport http from \"node:http\";\nimport { Pool } from \"pg\";\nimport { Worker } from \"bullmq\";\nimport { sdk } from \"./instrumentation\";          // the OpenTelemetry NodeSDK instance\nimport { redis } from \"./redis\";                  // an ioredis client\n\nlet ready = true;\nconst pool = new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL });\nconst server = http.createServer((req, res) => {\n  if (req.url === \"/ready\") { res.writeHead(ready ? 200 : 503); return res.end(); }\n  if (req.url === \"/live\")  { res.writeHead(200); return res.end(); }\n  handle(req, res);                                 // normal routing\n});\nserver.keepAliveTimeout = 5_000;\nconst workerConn = redis.duplicate({ maxRetriesPerRequest: null });   // BullMQ workers require null\nconst worker = new Worker(\"embed\", processEmbed, { connection: workerConn, concurrency: 4 });\nserver.listen(8080);\n\nlet shuttingDown = false;\nasync function shutdown(signal: string, code = 0) {\n  if (shuttingDown) { console.warn(\"second signal, forcing exit\"); process.exit(1); }\n  shuttingDown = true; ready = false;               // 1. fail readiness first\n  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, Number(process.env.DRAIN_DELAY_MS ?? 0)));  // keep serving while endpoints update; 0 if preStop sleeps\n  const deadline = setTimeout(() => { console.error(\"drain timeout\"); process.exit(1); }, 20_000).unref();\n  console.log(`${signal}: draining`);\n  try {\n    await Promise.all([\n      new Promise<void>((resolve) => server.close(() => resolve())),  // 2. stop intake (closes idle keep-alive on Node 19+)\n      worker.close(),                                                   //    stop taking jobs, wait for active ones\n    ]);\n    await Promise.all([redis.quit(), workerConn.quit()]);              // 3. waits for pending replies\n    await pool.end();                                                   //    only after nothing can still query\n    await Promise.race([sdk.shutdown(), new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 3_000))]);  // 4. flush spans last, capped\n    clearTimeout(deadline);\n    process.exit(code);\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.error(\"shutdown error\", err); process.exit(1);\n  }\n}\nprocess.on(\"SIGTERM\", () => void shutdown(\"SIGTERM\"));\nprocess.on(\"SIGINT\",  () => void shutdown(\"SIGINT\"));\nprocess.on(\"unhandledRejection\", (e) => { console.error(e); void shutdown(\"unhandledRejection\", 1); });\n```","tradeoffs":["Draining in-flight work avoids lost requests and duplicate jobs, but every second of drain delays the rollout; with long LLM streams the deadline has to be a product decision (finish, or cut with a resume token), not just a timeout value.","Keeping the listener open for a few seconds after SIGTERM (a `preStop` sleep or an in-app delay) removes the propagation-window 502s, but those seconds come out of the grace period and slow every rollout; size the delay from how long your ingress or proxy actually takes to drop an endpoint, and drop it where the balancer deregisters targets before the signal arrives.","A hard internal deadline guarantees the process exits before SIGKILL and reports a non-zero code, but it cuts whatever is still running; keep it comfortably below the grace period and raise the grace period for worker pods rather than removing the deadline.","Closing BullMQ workers gracefully prevents stalls on every deploy, but with `concurrency: 10` and ten-minute jobs no sensible grace period suffices; the fix is per-chunk checkpoints so a re-run resumes, not a longer wait.","Flushing telemetry on shutdown preserves the most interesting spans, but a slow or unreachable collector can eat the whole deadline; give the flush its own short timeout."],"pitfalls":["Shell-form `CMD npm start` or a wrapper script as PID 1: the symptom is containers that always take the full grace period and die by SIGKILL, with no shutdown log line ever appearing.","Registering a SIGTERM handler that forgets to call `process.exit`: the symptom is the process lingering after draining because an open handle (a timer, a Redis socket) keeps the event loop alive.","`server.close()` on Node 18 or older without `closeIdleConnections()`: the symptom is shutdown hanging until SIGKILL while the balancer holds idle keep-alive sockets.","Liveness probe wired to the same flag as readiness: the symptom is the container being restarted by the orchestrator in the middle of its own drain.","Closing the Postgres pool before the worker finishes: the symptom is \"Cannot use a pool after calling end on the pool\" errors in the last jobs of every deploy.","Deadline timer not `unref()`'d: the symptom is a process that finished draining but waits the full timeout before exiting."],"when":"Every long-running Node process needs this: API servers, BullMQ workers, MCP servers, schedulers. Do it\non day one of containerising the service, because the symptoms (5xx spikes, duplicate jobs, truncated\nstreams, missing traces) are easy to blame on everything else. Set the internal deadline from the\norchestrator's grace period and the longest acceptable in-flight unit; raise\n`terminationGracePeriodSeconds` for worker deployments with long jobs, and shorten jobs with checkpoints\nwhere that is not enough. Measure 5xx and connection errors during rollouts, stalled job counts, and\nwhether shutdown logs show the sequence completing. In interviews, walk through the SIGTERM-to-SIGKILL\ntimeline and the ordering of readiness, intake, drain, flush, close, exit.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the shutdown orchestrator, wires readiness to it, closes the worker, telemetry, Redis and pool in order, and tests it by sending SIGTERM to a loaded local process.","architect":"Sets grace periods, `preStop` policy and the rule for in-flight streams across services, and makes idempotent, checkpointed jobs a requirement so shutdown can afford to be strict.","fde":"Verifies the customer's cluster settings (grace period, probe intervals, PID 1 handling in their base image) and demonstrates a deploy with zero dropped requests in their environment."},"check":{"q":"After every deploy your Node API shows a burst of 502s at the load balancer and BullMQ reports a\nhandful of stalled jobs, even though you added `process.on('SIGTERM', () => server.close(() =>\nprocess.exit(0)))`. Explain both symptoms and the full fix.","a":"The 502s come from the window between SIGTERM and the balancer learning the pod is gone: the\nserver stopped accepting instantly while proxies were still routing to it. Kubernetes has already\nremoved the pod from the endpoints, so the fix is to keep serving for a few seconds while that\npropagates (a short `preStop` sleep, or an in-app delay before `server.close()`); fail readiness too\nfor any balancer that probes the pod directly, and keep the liveness probe passing.\nThe stalled jobs come from the worker never being closed: `server.close` only drains HTTP, so\n`process.exit` killed active jobs whose locks then expired and were re-run by the stall checker. The\nfull sequence is: readiness to 503, a short delay, `server.close()` (closing idle keep-alive connections) in parallel\nwith `await worker.close()`, `redis.quit()`, `pool.end()`, flush OpenTelemetry, then `process.exit(0)`,\nall under a deadline below the grace period, with a non-zero exit if the deadline fires. Verify the\nsignal reaches Node at all: exec-form `CMD` so Node (or a forwarding init such as `tini`) is PID 1,\nnot a shell."},"quiz":[{"q":"A Kubernetes pod calls `server.close()` the instant it receives SIGTERM and the load balancer logs a burst of 502s on every deploy. What removes the burst?","options":["Making `server.close()` asynchronous so it does not block the event loop","Continuing to serve for a few seconds, via a `preStop` sleep or an in-app delay, while endpoint removal propagates to the proxies","Failing the readiness probe first, because Kubernetes only removes a pod from endpoints after readiness fails","Calling `process.exit(0)` immediately so the pod disappears from endpoints faster"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A terminating pod is removed from endpoints regardless of readiness, but proxies and ingress learn that asynchronously, so traffic keeps arriving for a few seconds; staying up through that window is the fix. Failing readiness helps only balancers that probe the pod directly."},{"q":"Once you register a `process.on('SIGTERM', ...)` handler in Node, what changes?","options":["Node forwards the signal to child processes automatically","The grace period is extended by the orchestrator","The default exit on SIGTERM is disabled, so the process keeps running until you exit explicitly or the event loop empties","Subsequent SIGTERMs are ignored"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Installing a signal listener replaces the default behaviour; if the handler drains but never calls `process.exit` and some handle keeps the loop alive, the process lingers until SIGKILL."},{"q":"After readiness has been flipped to 503, which shutdown order is right?","options":["End the Postgres pool so no new writes can start, then close the worker, then flush telemetry, then exit","Close the HTTP server and the worker, then Redis and the Postgres pool, then flush telemetry, then exit","Flush telemetry, then exit and let the orchestrator close the remaining sockets","Close the worker, then exit, because the stall checker will finish any remaining jobs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The pool must outlive every request and job that may still query, so it closes only after the server and worker have drained; ending it first produces \"pool after end\" errors in the last jobs. Telemetry goes last, under a short cap, so the close steps' own spans are exported before exit."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/vitest-jest-and-integration-testing","sources":["Official docs: Vitest","Official docs: Jest","Official docs: Mock Service Worker (msw)","Official docs: Testcontainers for Node"],"why":"An AI service is ordinary backend code wrapped around a call that behaves unlike anything else in the\nprocess. The call takes seconds, costs money per token, needs a secret, and returns different text for\nthe same input. Every property breaks a conventional test suite: a suite that hits the real model is\ntoo slow to run on every push, too expensive to run in parallel, leaks a production key into CI logs,\nand fails randomly whenever the provider is degraded or the model phrases an answer differently. Teams\nthat start this way end up with `it.skip` all over the repo and no safety net at all.\n\nThe opposite naive answer, \"mock everything\", fails more quietly. A `vi.mock(\"@anthropic-ai/sdk\")`\nthat returns a canned object tests only your own branches; it says nothing about whether the request\nyou build is one the API accepts. The bug class that actually bites, a renamed field, a tool schema the\nprovider rejects, a stream event type you forgot to handle, lives in the serialization and parsing\nlayer that a module mock skips entirely. The same is true for the database: an in-memory stand-in does\nnot enforce unique constraints, JSON column semantics or transaction isolation, so the test passes and\nproduction throws.\n\nThe practice therefore has to separate *what you can assert* from *what you can only measure*. Logic\naround the model (prompt assembly, retries, parsing, tool dispatch, persistence) is deterministic once\nthe model is faked, so assert it. The model's output quality is statistical, so measure it in an eval\nwith a threshold and run it on a schedule, not as a merge gate. Getting that boundary right is the\nwhole skill; the runner is secondary.","how":"### The runners\nVitest and Jest share the `describe` / `it` / `expect` vocabulary, the `beforeAll` / `afterEach` hooks,\nmodule mocking, fake timers, snapshots and coverage, which is deliberate: Vitest was built to be\nlargely Jest-compatible. They differ in how code reaches the test. Jest predates native ES modules and\nTypeScript in Node; it runs each file through a transform you configure (`ts-jest`, `babel-jest` or an\nswc plugin) and its ESM support has long sat behind an experimental flag, which is why Jest projects\naccumulate `moduleNameMapper` and `transformIgnorePatterns` entries. Vitest reuses the Vite pipeline:\nVite's transformer (esbuild, or Oxc in recent Vite releases) strips types without type-checking, so\nrun `tsc --noEmit` separately; ESM is the default, `vite.config.ts` aliases apply to tests too,\nand watch mode re-runs only the files whose import graph changed. For a TypeScript-first AI service\nstarted today, Vitest is the default; Jest remains the right answer when the repo already runs on it,\nbecause migrating a large suite buys little.\n\nBoth run test files in parallel workers with an isolated module registry per file (Vitest's default\n`isolate: true`), *because* module state (a cached client, a memoised config) would otherwise leak\nbetween files. Jest's workers are child processes; Vitest lets you choose `pool: \"forks\"` (child\nprocesses, its default since v2) or `\"threads\"` (worker threads, lighter but unable to use\n`process.chdir` and fragile with some native addons). Parallelism is per file, not per test: tests\ninside one file run sequentially unless marked concurrent, and `fileParallelism: false` (Vitest) or\n`--runInBand` (Jest) serialises files when a shared resource forces it. Separately, `vi.mock` / `jest.mock` calls are hoisted above imports by the transform:\nESM imports resolve before any statement runs, so a mock registered in normal source order would\narrive too late. (Jest's hoisting is a Babel/ts-jest transform for CommonJS output; under native ESM\nJest you need `jest.unstable_mockModule` plus a dynamic `import()` instead.)\n\n### Three kinds of double for the model\n1. **An injected fake.** Define the narrowest interface your code needs (`complete(prompt): Promise<string>`,\n   or a stream of events) and pass an implementation in, rather than importing the SDK at module scope.\n   Tests then hand in a function that returns scripted answers, including malformed ones, in a known\n   order. This tests your parsing, repair loops, budgets and persistence, and it runs in microseconds.\n2. **HTTP interception.** Construct the real SDK and intercept at the network layer with `msw`\n   (`setupServer` from `msw/node`), `nock`, or undici's `MockAgent`. Now the request body you assert on\n   is the exact JSON the provider would receive, and the response you return is parsed by the real SDK\n   code. This is the layer that catches a wrong field name or a stream-event handler that never fires.\n   Set `maxRetries: 0` on the client so a deliberate 500 does not trigger the SDK's default two\n   retries (three attempts) with backoff. Streams are intercepted the same way: the handler returns\n   `new HttpResponse(body, { headers: { \"content-type\": \"text/event-stream\" } })` where `body` is a\n   `ReadableStream` emitting recorded `event: …\\ndata: {…}\\n\\n` frames, so the SDK's real SSE\n   parser runs. Splitting a frame across two chunks, or ending the stream before `message_stop`, is\n   how you test the reassembly and early-termination paths that hand-written fakes never exercise.\n3. **Recorded fixtures.** Capture a handful of real responses once (including tool-call turns and a\n   full stream transcript), store them as JSON, and replay them through the interceptor. They are the\n   only cheap way to test against the real response shape of a provider, and the only way to notice\n   that an SDK upgrade changed a type. Refresh them deliberately, not on every run.\n\nEach layer exists because the one below it cannot see the failure it catches. The fake cannot see\nserialization bugs; the interceptor cannot see output-quality drift; evals cannot see a bug in your\nretry logic because they are too noisy to attribute a failure to it.\n\n### Integration tests with real dependencies\nPostgres, Redis and a queue should be real in integration tests, *because* the behaviours you depend\non (constraint violations, `SELECT … FOR UPDATE`, JSONB queries, Redis expiry, BullMQ retries) are the\nthings fakes get wrong. Two patterns work: a `globalSetup` that starts containers with Testcontainers\nand exports connection strings, or a service started by CI (a GitHub Actions `services:` block, a\ncompose file locally). Isolation then has to be designed, since the runner is parallel: give each\nworker its own database or schema (derive the name from `VITEST_POOL_ID` / `JEST_WORKER_ID`), or run\nevery test inside a transaction that is rolled back in `afterEach`, or truncate between tests and\naccept running those files serially. The rollback trick has a precondition people miss: the code\nunder test must run on the *same connection* as the test's transaction. If it takes its own client\nfrom a pool, it neither sees the test's uncommitted fixtures nor gets rolled back, so inject the\ntransaction-bound client (the dependency-injection lesson again), and if the code opens its own\ntransactions, map them to savepoints. Shared mutable state across workers is the most common source\nof flaky integration suites.\n\n### Async, streams, time and cancellation\nModel code is asynchronous and often streamed, so the fakes must be too. An `async function*` is a\nperfect fake stream, and a fake that waits on a `signal` lets you assert that aborting the request\nstops iteration. Retries and backoff should run under fake timers (`vi.useFakeTimers()` plus\n`vi.advanceTimersByTimeAsync`) so a 30-second backoff test finishes in milliseconds; forgetting to\nawait the async advance is the usual reason a retry test hangs. `expect.poll` and `vi.waitFor` cover\neventually-consistent assertions against a queue.\n\n### Snapshots: prompts yes, completions no\nInline snapshots of the *rendered prompt* (system text plus serialized tool definitions) are cheap\nregression tests: a prompt edit shows up as a diff in review rather than as a silent behaviour change.\nSnapshotting model output is the opposite: it either fails on every run or is updated blindly with\n`-u`, which trains people to ignore it.\n\n### Where the real model lives\nReal-model tests belong in a separate suite, selected by a tag or directory, run on a schedule or on\ndemand with a budget, and scored against a threshold (pass rate ≥ 0.9 on a golden set, judge score\nabove a floor). They publish a metric; they do not block a merge on one bad sample. The evals module\ncovers how to build that set and the harness around it.","example":"A `summarize` service that depends on a narrow `ModelClient` interface,\n`{ complete(prompt: string): Promise<string> }`. It calls `complete`, tries to parse\n`{\"bullets\": string[]}`, and on failure calls once more with a repair prompt that quotes the bad\noutput. `anthropicAdapter(client)` implements `complete` with `client.messages.create` and joins the\ntext blocks. The first test drives the repair loop with a scripted fake; the second constructs the\nreal Anthropic SDK and intercepts the HTTP call with `msw`, so the request body and the response\nparsing are the real ones. Note that the handler *captures* the body and the assertion runs in the\ntest: an `expect` thrown inside an msw handler becomes a 500, which surfaces as the SDK's\n`InternalServerError` rather than as the assertion message you wanted to read.\n\n```ts\nimport { describe, it, expect, vi, beforeAll, afterAll, afterEach } from \"vitest\";\nimport { http, HttpResponse } from \"msw\";\nimport { setupServer } from \"msw/node\";\nimport Anthropic from \"@anthropic-ai/sdk\";\nimport { summarize, anthropicAdapter } from \"../src/summarize\";\n\ndescribe(\"summarize (unit, injected fake)\", () => {\n  it(\"feeds a malformed answer back once, then returns parsed bullets\", async () => {\n    const complete = vi.fn()\n      .mockResolvedValueOnce(\"not json\")\n      .mockResolvedValueOnce('{\"bullets\":[\"a\",\"b\"]}');\n    const out = await summarize(\"long text\", { complete });\n    expect(out.bullets).toEqual([\"a\", \"b\"]);\n    expect(complete).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(2);\n    expect(complete.mock.calls[1][0]).toContain(\"not json\");   // repair prompt quotes the bad output\n  });\n});\n\nlet sent: { max_tokens: number; messages: unknown[] } | undefined;   // the exact JSON on the wire\nconst server = setupServer(\n  http.post(\"https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages\", async ({ request }) => {\n    sent = (await request.json()) as typeof sent;\n    return HttpResponse.json({\n      id: \"msg_test\", type: \"message\", role: \"assistant\", model: \"test\",\n      content: [{ type: \"text\", text: '{\"bullets\":[\"x\"]}' }],\n      stop_reason: \"end_turn\", usage: { input_tokens: 12, output_tokens: 6 },\n    });\n  }),\n);\nbeforeAll(() => server.listen({ onUnhandledRequest: \"error\" }));  // any other network call fails loudly\nafterEach(() => { server.resetHandlers(); sent = undefined; });\nafterAll(() => server.close());\n\ndescribe(\"summarize (integration, real SDK over intercepted HTTP)\", () => {\n  it(\"builds a request the API accepts and parses the response\", async () => {\n    const client = new Anthropic({ apiKey: \"test-key\", maxRetries: 0 });\n    const out = await summarize(\"long text\", anthropicAdapter(client));\n    expect(out.bullets).toEqual([\"x\"]);\n    expect(sent?.max_tokens).toBeGreaterThan(0);    // required field present in the real request\n    expect(sent?.messages).toHaveLength(1);\n  });\n});\n```","tradeoffs":["An injected fake makes tests fast and deterministic but can drift from the real client's behaviour; the interception layer is what keeps it honest, so you need both, not one.","Real containers in integration tests catch database semantics fakes miss, at the cost of slower startup and CI complexity; an in-memory repository is acceptable only while the code relies on no constraint, isolation or JSON semantics, and stops being acceptable the day it does.","Recorded fixtures pin the provider's response shape, which is the point, but they go stale when the provider adds fields; refresh them on SDK upgrades rather than letting them rot or auto-updating.","Vitest's Vite pipeline gives fast TS/ESM with little config, while Jest's maturity and ecosystem win in large existing suites; switching an established Jest repo rarely pays for itself.","Keeping real-model tests out of the merge gate protects velocity, but only if the scheduled eval actually runs and someone owns its threshold; otherwise quality regressions go unnoticed."],"pitfalls":["Calling the real model in the PR suite; the symptom is intermittent red builds, a growing list of `it.skip`, and a provider key visible in CI logs.","Mocking the SDK module instead of the network; the symptom is a green suite and a 400 from the provider in production because a field was renamed or a tool schema was invalid.","Sharing one database between parallel workers; the symptom is tests that pass alone and fail in the full run, with unique-constraint or \"row not found\" errors that depend on ordering.","Testing backoff with real timers; the symptom is a retry test that takes a minute or hits the default test timeout, which then gets raised instead of fixed.","Forgetting `maxRetries: 0` on the client under test; the symptom is a test of a 500 response that is slowed by real backoff sleeps and sees three requests where it expected one.","Snapshotting model text; the symptom is a snapshot file updated with `-u` on every change, so the test no longer tests anything.","Using rollback-per-test isolation while the code under test takes its own pooled connection; the symptom is \"row not found\" for fixtures the test just inserted, and rows that survive between tests.","Leaving `onUnhandledRequest` at its default (`\"warn\"`); the symptom is a test that hit the real network because a URL did not match the handler, with only a warning buried in the output."],"when":"Reach for the injected fake whenever logic surrounds the model: prompt assembly, parsing, repair loops,\nbudgets, tool dispatch, persistence. Add the interception layer for each provider adapter and for any\nstream-handling code, since that is where wire-format bugs live. Run real dependencies in integration\ntests for anything touching SQL, Redis or a queue, and design worker isolation before the suite grows.\nKeep the real model in a separate scored suite with a budget. Measure the suite's wall-clock time, its\nflake rate, and how many bugs reach production in the serialization layer; those three numbers tell you\nwhich layer is missing. Interviewers ask how you test non-deterministic code and what a fake cannot\ncatch; the layered answer, with the merge-gate versus eval distinction, is what they are listening for.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the `ModelClient` interface, the fakes and the msw handlers, keeps the unit suite under a few seconds, and owns the Testcontainers setup for Postgres and Redis.","architect":"Decides the boundary between asserted tests and scored evals, which dependencies must be real in CI, and the budget and cadence of the real-model suite.","fde":"Builds a replayable fixture set from the customer's real traffic so their team can change prompts and upgrade SDKs with a safety net, without spending their model budget on CI."},"check":{"q":"Your team's suite calls the real model in twenty tests. It is slow, flaky and costs money, but\nthe lead argues that mocks would make the tests meaningless. How do you restructure it, and what does\neach layer guarantee?","a":"Split by what each layer can observe. Inject the model behind a narrow interface and rewrite the\ntwenty tests as unit tests with scripted fakes; they now assert parsing, retries, tool dispatch and\npersistence deterministically. Add one interception test per provider adapter using the real SDK over\nmsw, asserting the request body and feeding back recorded responses; this catches the wire-format bugs\nthe lead is worried about. Keep real dependencies (Postgres, Redis) in integration tests with\nper-worker isolation. Move the real-model cases into a scored eval with a golden set and a pass-rate\nthreshold, run on a schedule with a budget, so quality drift is measured rather than asserted. The\nmerge gate becomes fast and deterministic without pretending the model is deterministic."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does an HTTP-interception test with the real SDK catch that an injected fake model cannot?","options":["Non-deterministic differences in the model's wording","Errors in the request the SDK actually sends and in parsing the real response shape","Database constraint violations in the persistence layer","Slow backoff logic that should run under fake timers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The fake replaces everything below your interface, so a renamed field or an invalid tool schema never reaches a test. Running the real SDK against intercepted HTTP exercises serialization and parsing while remaining deterministic. Model wording belongs to evals, not either test layer."},{"q":"Why are `vi.mock` and `jest.mock` hoisted above the import statements in a test file?","options":["To make the mock apply to every file in the worker pool","Because mocks must be registered before the test runner starts its timers","Because ES module imports resolve before any statement runs, so a mock in source order would register too late","To allow the mocked module to be type-checked by TypeScript"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Imports are evaluated before the module body executes. If the mock registration ran in normal order, the module under test would already hold the real dependency. Hoisting the mock call fixes the ordering; it does not change scope across files or affect type checking."},{"q":"Where should tests that call the real model live, and how should they report?","options":["In the PR suite, retried three times to absorb non-determinism","In the PR suite, with snapshots of the model's text updated when they change","In a separate scheduled, budgeted suite that scores results against a threshold rather than asserting each case","Nowhere; faked tests plus interception tests are sufficient"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Model output is statistical, so a single failing case is not a regression and a single passing one is not proof. A scored suite with a golden set and a pass-rate floor measures quality drift without making every merge depend on provider latency, cost and randomness."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/docker-ci-cd-deployment","sources":["Official docs: Docker — Dockerfile reference and multi-stage builds","Official docs: Node.js — Docker and signal handling","Official docs: GitHub Actions"],"why":"A TypeScript service does not run as written. It has to be compiled (or at least type-stripped),\nits dependency tree is large and mostly only needed at build time, and `node_modules` can contain\nnative addons compiled for one libc. Shipping the source checkout and running `npm install` on the\nserver reproduces none of this reliably: the lockfile may be ignored, `devDependencies` land in\nproduction, the compile step depends on whatever `tsc` version the host has, and the image weighs\nhundreds of megabytes more than it needs to. Docker exists so the artifact that passed the tests is\nthe artifact that runs; the Node-specific work is arranging the build so that is actually true.\n\nAI services add three pressures ordinary web services lack. First, requests are long: a streamed\ncompletion holds a connection for seconds to minutes, so a deploy that kills the process on `SIGTERM`\nwithout draining cuts users off mid-answer and wastes tokens already billed. Second, the process holds\nthe most sensitive secret in the company, the provider API key, and a key baked into an image layer is\nvisible to anyone who can pull it. Third, \"the code\" now includes prompts, tool schemas and model\nidentifiers whose change can regress quality without any test turning red; the pipeline has to version\nthem with the build and make the quality check a visible, budgeted step.\n\nThe naive pipeline, \"build on push, deploy on green\", is not wrong; it is incomplete. Without\nreproducible installs it is not building what you tested; without a drain step it is not deploying\nsafely; without an eval beside the gate it is deploying prompt changes blind.","how":"### The image: stages because the runtime needs almost nothing\nA multi-stage Dockerfile separates *installing*, *building* and *running*. The deps stage copies only\n`package.json` and the lockfile and runs `npm ci` (`pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`, `yarn install\n--immutable`): `ci` refuses to proceed if the lockfile and manifest disagree, which is exactly the\nguarantee a build wants, and copying the manifests before the source means this expensive layer is\ncached until a dependency changes rather than on every commit. The build stage adds the source and\nruns `tsc` (or a bundler such as esbuild), then prunes dev dependencies. The runtime stage starts from\na fresh base image and copies in only `dist/`, the pruned `node_modules` and `package.json`. The result\nis smaller, has a smaller attack surface, and cannot accidentally depend on a dev tool.\n\nChoose the base deliberately. `node:<major>-slim` is Debian-based with glibc and is the safe default;\n`node:<major>-alpine` is smaller but uses musl, which breaks some native addons and some prebuilt\nbinaries (tokenizer and ONNX packages are common victims). Pin the major version to an LTS line and\nset `NODE_ENV=production`, which some libraries read to disable debug paths. A `.dockerignore` listing\n`node_modules`, `.env`, `dist` and `.git` keeps the build context small and keeps local secrets out of\nthe image. Run as the `node` user the official image provides rather than root.\n\n### Signals: exec form, PID 1 and draining\n`CMD [\"node\", \"dist/server.js\"]` (exec form) makes Node PID 1. The shell form `CMD node dist/server.js`\nruns `/bin/sh -c \"node dist/server.js\"`, so the shell is PID 1 and Node its child, and a shell does not\nforward `SIGTERM` to its children; the orchestrator waits out its grace period and then `SIGKILL`s the\nprocess mid-stream. (Some shells exec a lone `-c` command directly, which would accidentally make\nNode PID 1; do not rely on it, since it breaks the moment someone adds `&&`.) Being PID 1 has two\nconsequences of its own. The kernel applies no default action to signals sent to PID 1, so Node only\nreacts to `SIGTERM` if your code installs a handler; without one the signal is ignored and you again\nwait for `SIGKILL`. And Node does not reap zombie children; if the service spawns subprocesses (an MCP\nserver over stdio, a converter), run with `docker run --init`, `tini` or `dumb-init` as the entrypoint. The drain itself, stop accepting, finish or\nabort in-flight streams, close the pool, is the graceful-shutdown lesson; the deployment's job is to\ndeliver the signal and to set the grace period longer than the longest completion you allow.\n\n### Memory and the container boundary\nNode's default V8 heap limit is a heuristic derived from the memory it detects. Older releases read\nthe host's total memory and ignored the container's cgroup limit; newer ones read the cgroup limit,\nbut how well depends on the Node version and cgroup v1 versus v2. Either way the heap limit covers only\nthe JavaScript heap: `Buffer`s, native addons, thread stacks and the code cache live outside it, so the\nprocess's resident memory can cross the container limit while V8 still believes it has room, and the\nkernel's OOM killer ends it. Set `--max-old-space-size` explicitly, somewhat below the container limit\n(the example leaves about a quarter of a 1 GiB limit for non-heap memory), and confirm with\n`v8.getHeapStatistics()` at startup; streaming large contexts and holding many concurrent responses\nare where Node services discover this.\n\n### Health, readiness and config\nExpose a liveness endpoint that only proves the event loop is responsive and a readiness endpoint that\nchecks dependencies (database, cache, and whether the process is draining). Keep the two apart\n*because* a liveness probe that checks the database turns a database blip into a restart of every\nreplica at once. On shutdown, readiness flips to false the moment `SIGTERM` arrives, which is the\nsignal a plain load balancer uses to stop routing. Kubernetes adds a race: a terminating pod is\nremoved from the Service's endpoints, but that removal reaches kube-proxy and ingress controllers\nasynchronously, so for a few seconds after `SIGTERM` new connections can still arrive. If the\nprocess closes its listener immediately, those requests fail. The usual fix is a short `preStop`\nsleep (or a delay in the handler) before `server.close()`, and the grace period must cover that\ndelay plus the longest stream, since `preStop` time counts against it. Configuration comes from the environment, parsed once at startup with a schema so a\nmissing `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` or a non-numeric `PORT` fails before the first request. Secrets are\nmounted or injected by the platform at runtime (Kubernetes Secrets, a secret manager, CI-provided\nenvironment), never passed as `ARG`/`ENV` at build time: build args are recorded in image history.\n\n### The pipeline\nA pull request runs, in parallel where possible: `tsc --noEmit`, lint, unit tests, and integration\ntests against service containers started by CI. `main` additionally builds the image once with\nBuildKit, tags it with the git SHA (and a semver tag at release), pushes it to a registry, and deploys\nthat digest, so staging and production run byte-identical images and a rollback is a tag swap. Note\nthe gap this leaves: the PR tests ran against the source on the CI runner, not against the image.\nA file missing from a `COPY`, a native addon built for the wrong libc, or a `dist/` path typo only\nappears in the image, so a smoke test against the deployed digest in staging (start, hit readiness,\nmake one request through a stubbed or cheap model) is what makes \"the artifact we tested\" true. Prompt\nfiles, tool schemas and the model identifier are part of the repository and therefore of the image;\nchanging them is a deploy like any other. Beside the gate, an eval job runs the golden set against\nthe real model with a budget and publishes its score; a drop blocks promotion to production without\nmaking every pull request pay for model calls. Rollouts of prompt or model changes are safest as a\ncanary: route a slice of traffic, compare quality and cost metrics, then widen.","example":"A multi-stage Dockerfile for a TypeScript service, then a trimmed GitHub Actions workflow that gates on\ntypecheck and tests against a real Postgres, builds once, and pushes an image tagged with the commit.\nSecrets reach the runtime from the platform, not from either file.\n\n```dockerfile\n# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1\nFROM node:22-slim AS deps\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY package.json package-lock.json ./\nRUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.npm npm ci       # cached until the lockfile changes\n\nFROM deps AS build\nCOPY tsconfig.json ./\nCOPY src ./src\nRUN npm run build && npm prune --omit=dev             # dist/ plus production deps only\n\nFROM node:22-slim AS runtime\nENV NODE_ENV=production\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY --from=build /app/node_modules ./node_modules\nCOPY --from=build /app/dist ./dist\nCOPY package.json ./\nUSER node\nEXPOSE 3000\n# exec form so SIGTERM reaches node; heap set for a 1 GiB container memory limit\nCMD [\"node\", \"--max-old-space-size=768\", \"dist/server.js\"]\n```\n\n```yaml\nname: ci\non: { pull_request: {}, push: { branches: [main] } }\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    services:\n      postgres:\n        image: postgres:16\n        env: { POSTGRES_PASSWORD: test }\n        ports: [\"5432:5432\"]\n        options: --health-cmd pg_isready --health-interval 5s --health-retries 10\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n        with: { node-version: 22, cache: npm }\n      - run: npm ci\n      - run: npm run typecheck && npm run lint && npm test\n        env: { DATABASE_URL: postgres://postgres:test@localhost:5432/postgres }\n  image:\n    needs: test\n    if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    permissions: { contents: read, packages: write }\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: docker/login-action@v3\n        with:\n          registry: ghcr.io\n          username: ${{ github.actor }}\n          password: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}\n      - uses: docker/build-push-action@v6\n        with:\n          push: true\n          tags: ghcr.io/${{ github.repository }}:${{ github.sha }}\n```","tradeoffs":["Multi-stage builds shrink the runtime image and its attack surface but add build complexity; for a throwaway prototype a single-stage image is fine until it ships to a shared environment.","Alpine images are smaller than slim ones, yet musl breaks some native tokenizer and ML addons; the saving flips to a loss the first time a prebuilt binary fails to load.","Bundling with esbuild into one file speeds cold start and removes `node_modules` from the image, but it hides dependency versions from scanners and breaks packages that rely on `__dirname` or dynamic requires.","Keeping evals out of the PR gate keeps merges fast and cheap, but only if the scheduled eval blocks promotion; an eval that merely reports is a dashboard nobody reads.","A long termination grace period lets streams finish cleanly, while a very long one slows every rollout and masks handlers that never stop; cap it at the maximum completion time you actually allow.","Pinning the base image by digest is reproducible but freezes security patches; a pinned major tag plus a scheduled rebuild is the usual compromise."],"pitfalls":["Shell-form `CMD` or an entrypoint script that does not `exec`; the symptom is pods that always take the full grace period to stop and users whose streams cut off at every deploy.","Copying the whole source before `npm ci`; the symptom is a dependency install on every commit and a ten-minute build for a one-line change.","Passing the provider key as a build `ARG` or `ENV`; the symptom is the key readable in the image history (`docker history`) and in every registry that image was pushed to.","Leaving the heap limit to the default heuristic; the symptom is OOM kills by the kernel under load (exit code 137) with no JavaScript heap error in the logs.","Running `npm install` instead of `npm ci`; the symptom is a dependency version in production that no developer machine or CI run ever tested.","A readiness probe that stays green while draining, or a listener closed the instant `SIGTERM` arrives with no `preStop` delay; the symptom is a burst of 502s or connection resets at every rollout even though the drain code looks correct.","Deploying prompt or model-id changes with no eval in the pipeline; the symptom is a quality regression reported by users days after a deploy that \"only touched a text file\"."],"when":"Every Node AI service that runs anywhere but a laptop needs this; the question is how much. A single\nmulti-stage Dockerfile, `npm ci`, exec-form `CMD`, runtime secrets and a PR pipeline with typecheck and\ntests are the floor. Add integration tests with service containers once a database or queue is in the\npath, an explicit heap limit and readiness-aware drain once you stream, and a budgeted eval job as soon\nas prompts change regularly. Measure image size, build time for a one-line change (with warm caches it\nshould cost little more than the compile step, since the install layer is reused), deploy duration, and the count of requests failed during a\nrollout, which should be zero. Interviewers ask why `SIGTERM` is not reaching the process, what\n`npm ci` guarantees, and where the API key should live; concrete answers to those three separate a\nsenior engineer from someone who has only run `docker build`.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the Dockerfile and workflow, keeps the layer order cache-friendly, wires the readiness flip into shutdown and sets heap and grace-period values from measured completion times.","architect":"Owns the promotion policy: which checks gate a merge, which gate production, how evals and canaries fit the rollout, and how secrets and model credentials are isolated per environment.","fde":"Adapts the pipeline to the customer's registry, secret manager and cluster, and proves to their platform team that the image is non-root, lockfile-pinned and free of embedded keys."},"check":{"q":"After each deploy, a streaming chat service shows a spike of truncated responses and the old\npods linger for exactly thirty seconds before dying. What is happening, and what changes fix it?","a":"Thirty seconds is Kubernetes' default `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`, and lingering for all of\nit means the process never acted on `SIGTERM`. Three causes are common: the `CMD` is in shell form, so\n`sh` is PID 1 and the signal never reaches Node; Node is PID 1 but installs no `SIGTERM` handler, and\nthe kernel ignores default-action signals for PID 1; or the handler exists but nothing stops the HTTP\nserver and in-flight streams. In each case the orchestrator finally sends `SIGKILL` mid-response. Fix it by switching to exec-form\n`CMD` (or `exec` in the entrypoint script), handling `SIGTERM` by flipping readiness to false, waiting a few seconds\n(a `preStop` sleep) for endpoint removal to propagate, then calling `server.close()`, aborting or finishing active streams with an `AbortController`, and closing pools;\nthen set the grace period above the longest allowed completion. Verify with a deploy under synthetic\nstreaming load and confirm zero truncated responses."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why copy `package.json` and the lockfile and run `npm ci` before copying the source tree?","options":["Because `npm ci` fails if source files are present in the build context","So the dependency layer is cached and only rebuilt when the lockfile changes, not on every commit","To keep `devDependencies` out of the final image","Because TypeScript must be compiled before dependencies can resolve"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Docker caches layers by their inputs. If the source is copied first, every commit invalidates the install layer. Copying manifests first means the slow install reruns only when dependencies change. Pruning dev dependencies is a separate step and unrelated to layer order."},{"q":"A container's `CMD` is written as `CMD node dist/server.js`. What is the practical consequence on `kubectl rollout`?","options":["Node starts faster because the shell preloads the module cache","Environment variables are not expanded, so the process reads no configuration","`/bin/sh` becomes PID 1 and does not forward `SIGTERM`, so the process is killed after the grace period instead of draining","The container cannot expose ports because the shell owns the network namespace"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Shell form wraps the command in `sh -c`. The shell receives the signal and does not pass it on, Node never sees it, and the orchestrator escalates to `SIGKILL`. Exec form (`CMD [\"node\", ...]`) makes Node PID 1 so the graceful-shutdown handler you install runs. Environment expansion and ports are unaffected."},{"q":"Where should the provider API key enter a deployed Node AI service?","options":["As a Docker build argument so the image is self-contained","Committed in an `.env` file that the Dockerfile copies into the image","Injected at runtime by the platform (secret manager or orchestrator secret) into the process environment","Hard-coded in the compiled bundle, since `dist/` is not human-readable"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Anything present at build time lands in an image layer and is readable from image history or any registry copy. Runtime injection keeps the secret out of the artifact, lets it rotate without a rebuild, and allows different keys per environment."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/vercel-ai-sdk","sources":["Official docs: Vercel AI SDK","Official docs: Zod"],"why":"Two kinds of plumbing dominate the first week of any TypeScript AI product. The first is provider\nshape: OpenAI returns `choices[0].message.content` and tool calls with JSON-string arguments, Anthropic\nreturns an array of content blocks with parsed `input`, Google differs again, and each streams a\ndifferent event vocabulary. A team that wants to compare models, keep a fallback provider, or let a\ncustomer bring their own key ends up writing an adapter per provider and a normalised stream type of\nits own. The second is the browser: a chat UI needs tokens as they arrive, tool-call progress, partial\nstructured objects, stop buttons and message history, and the naive `fetch` plus `TextDecoder` loop\ngrows into a protocol with no name that only one engineer understands.\n\nCalling the native SDKs directly is the right choice when you use one provider and need its full\nsurface, but it does not solve either problem; it is the thing the adapter would wrap. Picking a\nheavyweight agent framework solves them at the cost of owning its abstractions for everything else.\nThe AI SDK is deliberately narrower: it standardises the model call and the UI stream and stops\nthere. Its design bet is that the unified call can cover the large common core (messages, tools,\nstructured output, streaming, usage) and that the provider-specific long tail can be reached through\ntyped provider options rather than by abandoning the layer. Whether that bet holds for your use case is\nexactly the decision this lesson is about.","how":"### The provider abstraction\nEvery provider package (`@ai-sdk/openai`, `@ai-sdk/anthropic`, `@ai-sdk/google`, plus community ones)\nimplements the SDK's language-model specification: a small interface with a `doGenerate` and a\n`doStream` method that take the unified prompt (system, messages with typed parts, tool definitions,\nsettings) and return either a result or a stream of unified parts: text deltas, reasoning deltas,\ntool-call deltas, tool results, a finish part with `finishReason` and `usage`. The core functions are\nwritten once against that interface, which is *why* `anthropic(\"...\")` and `openai(\"...\")` are\ninterchangeable at the call site: the mapping from the unified shape to each provider's wire format and\nback lives inside the provider package, not in your code. Provider-only features (Anthropic's\n`cache_control` breakpoints or thinking settings, OpenAI's reasoning effort, a Google safety setting)\nare passed through `providerOptions` keyed by provider, so you can reach them without leaving the\nunified call; they simply do nothing on a provider that lacks them.\n\n### Core functions\n- `generateText` and `streamText` take `model`, `system`, `messages` (or `prompt`), optional `tools`,\n  and settings such as `temperature`, `maxOutputTokens` and `abortSignal`. The streaming variant returns\n  an object whose `textStream` and `fullStream` are async iterables and whose `text`, `usage` and\n  `steps` are promises that resolve when the stream finishes, so one call serves both the response\n  body and your logging.\n- `tool({ description, inputSchema, execute })` defines a tool from a Zod (or JSON) schema; the schema\n  becomes the provider's tool definition and the model's arguments are validated against it before\n  `execute` runs, which is the same contract-as-value idea as the schema-validation lesson. When\n  `execute` is present the SDK runs the tool and records its result, and if you set `stopWhen` (for\n  example `stepCountIs(n)`) it appends the result and calls the model again, looping until the condition\n  holds or the model stops calling tools. The default for `generateText` / `streamText` is a single\n  step (`stepCountIs(1)`), so without `stopWhen` the tool runs but the model never sees its result and\n  never writes the final answer. Omit `execute` to receive the tool call and run it yourself, for\n  approvals or client-side tools.\n- `generateObject` and `streamObject` take a Zod schema and return a typed, validated object (or a\n  stream of partial objects). Under the hood the provider package picks its best mechanism, a JSON\n  schema response format, a forced tool call, or an instruction, and the SDK validates the result\n  against the schema, so a non-conforming answer surfaces as an error (`NoObjectGeneratedError`) rather\n  than a mis-typed value.\n- `embed` and `embedMany` do the same unification for embedding models.\n\n### One request through the tool loop\nWith `tools` that have `execute` and `stopWhen: stepCountIs(4)`, a single `streamText` call runs:\n1. **Step 1.** The SDK maps `system`, `messages` and the tool schemas to the provider's request and\n   streams the reply. The model emits a tool call; the SDK accumulates its argument fragments and\n   validates the parsed input against the tool's Zod schema.\n2. **Execute.** `execute` runs with the typed input. The SDK appends two messages to the step's\n   history: the assistant message holding the tool call and a tool message holding the result.\n   (In v5 a failed execution is recorded as a tool-error part rather than thrown, so the model can\n   read it; check how your version treats errors.)\n3. **Check the stop condition.** The loop continues only if the last step ended in tool calls and\n   `stopWhen` is not yet satisfied. Otherwise the finish part is emitted.\n4. **Step 2.** A *new* provider request is sent containing the original prompt plus the appended\n   messages. The model now writes the answer.\n\nTwo consequences follow from step 4. Each step is a separate billed request that resends the whole\nhistory, so input tokens grow with every round, which is *why* `totalUsage` (summed over steps)\ncan be several times the first request's input and why a step limit is a cost control, not only a\nloop guard. And the result exposes `steps`, each with its own text, tool calls, results and usage,\nwhich is what you log to see where a slow or expensive answer spent its rounds.\n\n### The UI half and its protocol\n`useChat` (from `@ai-sdk/react` in v5; Vue and Svelte packages exist too) on the client keeps the\nmessage list, sends it to your route on submit, and appends parts as\nthey stream: text, tool invocations with their state, and custom data. The server route returns the\nresult's response helper, which encodes the unified stream parts into the SDK's UI message stream\n(in v5, JSON parts sent as server-sent events and marked by a protocol-version header; v4 used a\nline-prefixed \"data stream\" format), consumed by the hook; a plain text alternative exists when you\nonly need tokens. This is the piece that lets a tool call started on the server render\nas a progress card in the browser without you writing the protocol. The client and server packages\nmust agree on the protocol version, which is why the two sides are upgraded together.\n\n### Middleware and telemetry\n`wrapLanguageModel` wraps any model with middleware that can transform the prompt, intercept the\nresult or short-circuit the call: logging, caching, guardrails and default settings live here instead\nof at every call site. OpenTelemetry support, switched on per call with the `experimental_telemetry`\noption, emits spans for each call and step with token usage, which plugs into the same tracing\npipeline as the rest of the service.\n\n### Runtime fit\nThe core runs anywhere `fetch` exists: Node, Bun, Deno, edge runtimes, and inside Express, Fastify,\nHono or Next.js route handlers. Nothing requires Vercel's platform; the UI hooks require a framework\nonly on the client side. Note that names have shifted across major versions: in v5 tool `parameters`\nbecame `inputSchema`, `maxTokens` became `maxOutputTokens`, `maxSteps` became `stopWhen`, and\n`convertToCoreMessages` became `convertToModelMessages`. v6 then deprecated `generateObject` /\n`streamObject` in favour of `generateText` / `streamText` with an `output` setting and added an agent\nabstraction, and its migration guide lists further signature changes. Check the version you install\nagainst its docs rather than copying old snippets.","example":"A route handler that streams a tool-using answer to a `useChat` client, and a separate function that\nclassifies text into a typed object. The model line is the only provider-specific code; written\nagainst AI SDK 5 naming.\n\n```ts\nimport { streamText, generateObject, tool, stepCountIs, convertToModelMessages } from \"ai\";\nimport { anthropic } from \"@ai-sdk/anthropic\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { db } from \"./db\";\n\nconst model = anthropic(process.env.MODEL_ID ?? \"claude-opus-5-5\");   // swap for openai(\"...\") to compare\n\nexport async function chatHandler(req: Request): Promise<Response> {\n  const { messages } = await req.json();                             // UI messages from useChat\n  const result = streamText({\n    model,\n    system: \"You are a support assistant. Look orders up with the tool; never guess order data.\",\n    messages: convertToModelMessages(messages),\n    tools: {\n      lookupOrder: tool({\n        description: \"Fetch an order by its id\",\n        inputSchema: z.object({ orderId: z.string().regex(/^ord_\\w+$/) }),\n        execute: async ({ orderId }) => (await db.orders.find(orderId)) ?? { error: \"not found\" },\n      }),\n    },\n    stopWhen: stepCountIs(4),          // at most 4 model/tool rounds per request\n    abortSignal: req.signal,           // client disconnect stops generation and further billing\n    onFinish: ({ totalUsage, steps }) =>   // `usage` here is the last step only; totalUsage sums all\n      console.log({ totalUsage, steps: steps.length, toolCalls: steps.flatMap((s) => s.toolCalls).length }),\n  });\n  return result.toUIMessageStreamResponse();   // tokens, tool state and data to the browser\n}\n\nconst Intent = z.object({\n  intent: z.enum([\"refund\", \"shipping\", \"other\"]),\n  confidence: z.number().min(0).max(1),\n});\n\nexport async function classify(text: string) {\n  const { object } = await generateObject({ model, schema: Intent, prompt: `Classify:\\n${text}` });\n  return object;                       // typed { intent, confidence }, validated against Intent\n}\n```\n\nWalk the chat route for \"Where is ord_42?\". Step 1 sends the system prompt, one user message and\nthe `lookupOrder` schema; the model replies with a tool call `{ orderId: \"ord_42\" }`, the regex\npasses, and `execute` reads the database. Step 2 resends everything plus the call and its result,\nand the model writes the answer. `steps.length` is 2, the browser saw a tool card move from\n\"input available\" to \"output available\" before any answer text, and `totalUsage.inputTokens` counts\nthe system prompt twice, because it was sent twice. Had the model asked for `\"42\"`, the Zod regex\nwould have rejected the input before `execute` ran. Swapping `anthropic(...)` for `openai(...)`\nchanges the bytes on the wire (a `tool_calls` entry with string arguments instead of a `tool_use`\nblock) and nothing in this file.","tradeoffs":["One call shape across providers makes comparisons and fallbacks cheap, but the newest provider-specific feature usually arrives behind `providerOptions` or not at all for a while; if your product is built on such a feature, the native SDK is the better primary interface.","The built-in tool loop and `generateObject` remove boilerplate, yet they decide the mechanism (forced tool versus JSON mode) for you; when you need deterministic control over every request body, for caching or auditing, drop to the native SDK for that path.","The UI protocol gives streaming tool and data parts for free, at the cost of coupling client and server package versions and of a wire format you do not own; a plain text stream is simpler when the client is not yours.","Middleware centralises logging, caching and guardrails, but a stack of middlewares is a second place behaviour hides; it pays while every call site needs the same policy, and stops paying once middlewares need per-route exceptions, at which point explicit calls are clearer.","Major versions have renamed core options, which is cheap to follow in a small codebase and painful in a large one; pin the version and schedule upgrades rather than floating."],"pitfalls":["Assuming `generateObject` removes the need to handle failure; the symptom is an unhandled schema validation error in production when the model returns a value outside the enum.","Setting a generous `stopWhen` limit, or using an agent abstraction with a high default, without a budget; the symptom is a request that makes a dozen tool calls, times out at the proxy and bills for every round. The opposite mistake, omitting `stopWhen`, shows up as a tool that runs but a reply that ends with no answer text.","Forgetting to pass `abortSignal` from the request; the symptom is server logs showing completions finishing minutes after users closed the tab.","Upgrading `ai` on the server without the client package; the symptom is a UI that renders nothing or shows raw protocol lines because the stream format changed.","Reaching for `providerOptions` without checking the provider; the symptom is a cache or thinking setting silently ignored after switching models, with cost and latency changing unexpectedly.","Treating the model string as configuration-free; the symptom is a provider swap that passes tests with the fake and fails on tool schemas the new provider rejects."],"when":"Use it when you want to compare or switch providers, when a browser UI needs streamed text, tool state\nor partial objects, or when a small team wants typed tools and structured output without owning an\nagent framework. Prefer the native provider SDK when you commit to one provider and lean on its\nspecific features (explicit prompt caching, batch APIs, server-side tools), when you need byte-level\ncontrol over requests, or when you are writing a library others will consume. Measure the same things\nas with a native client: tokens and cost per request, p95 time to first token, tool-call counts per\nconversation, and schema validation failure rate. In interviews the useful answer explains what the\nabstraction normalises, what it cannot, and when you would step outside it.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes route handlers with `streamText`, defines tools with Zod, sets step limits and abort signals, and uses `providerOptions` for caching or reasoning settings per provider.","architect":"Decides whether the unified layer or a native SDK is the primary interface, how provider switching is exercised in CI, and where middleware for logging and guardrails sits.","fde":"Uses the provider swap to run a customer's prompts across models with one codebase and shows them the cost, latency and quality differences before they commit to a vendor."},"check":{"q":"A team built their product on the AI SDK and now wants Anthropic prompt caching on a 40k-token\nsystem prompt and a batch job that classifies a million records overnight. What do you advise?","a":"Split the two. Prompt caching is reachable without leaving the SDK: pass the cache breakpoint\nthrough `providerOptions` for the Anthropic provider on the system message or tool definitions, then\nverify by reading cache-read token counts in `usage` or provider metadata; if the breakpoint is not\nexposed in their version, that single path can use the native SDK while the rest stays unified. The\nbatch job is a different story: a million offline classifications belong on the provider's batch API,\nwhich trades latency for a lower price and is not something the unified call models. Write that job\ndirectly against the native SDK, reuse the same Zod schema for validation, and keep the interactive\npaths on the AI SDK. The rule is: unified layer for the common interactive surface, native SDK for\nprovider-specific economics."},"quiz":[{"q":"What makes `anthropic(\"...\")` and `openai(\"...\")` interchangeable in a `streamText` call?","options":["The SDK translates prompts into a provider-neutral language the models all understand","Each provider package implements the same language-model specification, mapping the unified prompt and stream parts to its own wire format","Both providers expose an identical REST API, so no mapping is needed","The SDK proxies every request through Vercel's servers, which normalise the response"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The core functions are written against one interface; the provider packages do the translation in both directions. The provider APIs differ substantially, and nothing is routed through Vercel; the package runs wherever `fetch` runs."},{"q":"When should a team step outside the AI SDK and call a provider's native SDK directly?","options":["Whenever they stream responses to a browser","Whenever they use tools, since the SDK cannot run a tool loop","When they depend on provider-specific features or endpoints the unified layer does not model, such as a batch API or byte-level request control","Never; the SDK exposes every provider feature through its core functions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Streaming and tool loops are the SDK's core strengths. Its limits are the features it chose not to unify; `providerOptions` covers many, but batch processing, exact request bodies and the newest provider capabilities are reasons to use the native client for that path."},{"q":"What does `generateObject` with a Zod schema do that `JSON.parse` on the model's text does not?","options":["It guarantees the model cannot produce an invalid answer, so no error handling is needed","It caches the result so repeated prompts are free","It sends the schema to the provider using its structured-output mechanism and validates the result, so the returned value is typed and conforming or an error is raised","It streams the object to the client automatically"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`JSON.parse` gives you `unknown` from whatever the model wrote. `generateObject` constrains the generation where the provider supports it and validates against the schema, so a non-conforming value becomes an explicit error you must handle rather than a silently wrong object. It does not guarantee success, cache results or stream to the client."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/openai-anthropic-node-sdks","sources":["Official docs: OpenAI Node SDK (openai)","Official docs: Anthropic TypeScript SDK (@anthropic-ai/sdk)"],"why":"Calling a model is one HTTPS POST, and the temptation is to write it with `fetch`. That works for a\ndemo and fails in production in predictable ways. The provider returns 429 or 529 under load and the\nhand-rolled call either gives up or retries immediately and makes the overload worse. A streamed\nresponse arrives as server-sent events with several event types that must be parsed, re-assembled\ninto content blocks and matched to a stop reason; a hand-written parser handles the text case and\nsilently drops the tool-call case. A tool call arrives with a JSON schema contract that TypeScript\ncannot see, so arguments are cast rather than checked. Timeouts, request IDs for support tickets,\nrate-limit headers, pagination of file and batch endpoints and the exact error taxonomy are all left\nto each caller to rediscover.\n\nThe SDKs exist to make those behaviours uniform and typed. Both are generated from the provider's API\nspecification, which is *why* every request and response has a TypeScript type that matches the\nwire exactly and why the type changes when the API does. Both wrap the transport with the same\nproduction defaults: retries on connection errors and on 408, 409, 429 and 5xx responses with\nexponential backoff that honours `retry-after`, a default timeout of 10 minutes, and per-request\noverrides. The naive alternative, a thin wrapper of your own, ends up re-implementing a worse version\nof exactly this. The reason to understand the SDKs rather than just call them is that their defaults\ninteract with yours: an outer retry loop around the SDK's inner retries multiplies attempts, a proxy\ntimeout shorter than the SDK's makes the SDK's retries invisible, and a stream you forget to abort on\nclient disconnect keeps billing.","how":"### Shared client mechanics\n`new OpenAI()` reads `OPENAI_API_KEY`; `new Anthropic()` reads `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`. Both accept\n`apiKey`, `baseURL`, `timeout` (milliseconds, unlike the Python SDKs' seconds), `maxRetries` (default\n2, so up to three attempts), `defaultHeaders` and a custom `fetch`, and both run on supported Node LTS\nreleases (check the `engines` field of the version you install), Bun, Deno and edge runtimes because\nthey use the global `fetch`. Every method takes an optional second argument of request\noptions: `{ timeout, maxRetries, signal, headers }`. The `signal` is an ordinary `AbortSignal`, so the\ncancellation lesson applies unchanged: aborting rejects the promise with `APIUserAbortError` and\ncloses the socket, which stops further generation (tokens already produced are still billed). Retries\nare counted per request, and a timeout is retried, so the worst-case wall clock is roughly\n`timeout × (maxRetries + 1)` plus the backoff sleeps; size proxy and handler timeouts from that\nnumber, not from `timeout` alone. One asymmetry: for non-streaming requests with a large\n`max_tokens`, the Anthropic SDK raises its default timeout on its own, and very large output caps\neffectively require streaming, *because* a single HTTP response that takes many minutes to arrive\nis exactly what proxies and load balancers kill.\n\nBoth expose typed errors under the default export: `APIError` with a numeric `status`, and subclasses\nsuch as `BadRequestError` (400), `AuthenticationError` (401), `RateLimitError` (429),\n`InternalServerError` (5xx), `APIConnectionError` and `APIConnectionTimeoutError`. Check with\n`instanceof` from most specific to least, never by matching message text. Each response carries the\nprovider's request ID (`_request_id` on objects returned by non-streaming calls, `request_id` on\nAnthropic's `MessageStream` helper) for support tickets, and `.withResponse()`\nreturns `{ data, response }` so you can read rate-limit headers from the raw `Response`.\n\n### Requests and responses\nOpenAI has two chat surfaces. Chat Completions (`client.chat.completions.create`) takes `messages`\nwith `system`, `user`, `assistant` and `tool` roles and returns `choices[0].message` with `content`\nand optional `tool_calls`; the newer Responses API (`client.responses.create`) takes `input` and\nreturns a list of typed `output` items with `output_text` as a convenience, and can keep\nconversation state server-side: pass `previous_response_id` instead of resending history. Responses\nare stored by default unless you set `store: false`, which is a data-retention decision, not just a\nconvenience. Anthropic has one: `client.messages.create` takes a top-level `system`\nstring or blocks, a `messages` array of `user` / `assistant` turns, a required `max_tokens`, and returns\n`content` as an array of blocks (`text`, `tool_use`, `thinking`) with a `stop_reason`\n(`end_turn`, `tool_use`, `max_tokens`, `stop_sequence`, `pause_turn`, `refusal`, and new values as the\nAPI grows, so keep a default branch). The block array is the structural difference to\ninternalise: Anthropic responses are always lists, so code narrows on `block.type`, whereas Chat\nCompletions responses are a single message with optional fields. Usage is `prompt_tokens` /\n`completion_tokens` on Chat Completions (`input_tokens` / `output_tokens` on the Responses API) and `input_tokens` / `output_tokens` (plus cache read and creation\ncounts) on Anthropic; normalise early if you log cost.\n\n### Streaming\nBoth stream as async iterables. OpenAI returns a stream when `stream: true` is set: Chat Completions\nyields chunks whose `choices[0].delta.content` you concatenate, with usage in a final chunk when\n`stream_options: { include_usage: true }` is set; tool calls arrive as `delta.tool_calls[]` fragments\nkeyed by `index`, whose `function.arguments` strings you must concatenate per index before parsing\n(or use the `client.chat.completions.stream()` helper, whose `finalChatCompletion()` does it for you); the Responses API yields named events such as\n`response.output_text.delta`. Anthropic's `client.messages.stream()` returns a helper that is both an\niterable of raw events (`content_block_start`, `content_block_delta` with `text_delta` or\n`input_json_delta`, `message_delta`, `message_stop`) and an emitter with `.on(\"text\", …)`, and whose\n`.finalMessage()` resolves the complete, typed `Message` once the stream ends, so one call feeds the\nuser and your logs. The helper exists *because* tool-call arguments arrive as JSON fragments across many\nevents; accumulating them correctly is exactly the code you do not want to write per service.\n\n### Tools and structured output: the two wire shapes\nOpenAI Chat Completions defines a tool as `{ type: \"function\", function: { name, description,\nparameters, strict } }`; the model answers with `tool_calls[]`, each carrying an `id` and\n`function.arguments` as a JSON *string* you must `JSON.parse`, and the result goes back as a message\nwith `role: \"tool\"` and `tool_call_id`. Anthropic defines `{ name, description, input_schema }`; the\nmodel answers with `tool_use` content blocks whose `input` is already an object, and results go back\ninside a `user` message as `tool_result` blocks keyed by `tool_use_id`, all results for one turn in one\nmessage. Both SDKs ship Zod helpers that derive the schema and validate the arguments: OpenAI's\n`zodFunction` and `zodResponseFormat` from `openai/helpers/zod` with a `parse` method that returns\n`message.parsed`, and Anthropic's `betaZodTool` with `client.beta.messages.toolRunner`, which also runs\nthe call-tool-append-repeat loop for you. Structured output is `response_format` with a JSON schema\nand `strict: true` on OpenAI (`text.format` on the Responses API), and an `output_config.format` JSON\nschema on Anthropic in current SDK versions; in both cases keep your own `safeParse` on the result, as\nthe schema-validation lesson argues.\n\n### Caching, batching, platforms\nAnthropic prompt caching is opt-in: place `cache_control: { type: \"ephemeral\" }` on the last stable\nblock (system prompt, tool list), or in recent API versions set a top-level `cache_control` that marks\nthe last cacheable block for you, and verify with `usage.cache_read_input_tokens`. Any byte change\nbefore the breakpoint invalidates it, and a prefix below the model's minimum cacheable length is not\ncached at all. OpenAI caches long, repeated prefixes automatically and reports it in\n`usage.prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens`, so the lever there is prompt ordering rather than\nannotations. Both offer a batch endpoint for offline work at a discount and a files API; Anthropic has\n`messages.countTokens`, while OpenAI estimates usually come from a tokenizer library such as\n`tiktoken` (check whether your API version also offers a counting endpoint). For cloud platforms, OpenAI ships an\n`AzureOpenAI` client, and Anthropic ships `@anthropic-ai/bedrock-sdk` and `@anthropic-ai/vertex-sdk`\nwith the same `messages` surface behind platform credentials.","example":"One narrow `ModelClient` interface with two adapters. Each streams tokens to stdout, returns the full\ntext with normalised usage, relies on the SDK's retries rather than its own, and accepts an\n`AbortSignal` so a disconnected client stops generation. `isRetryable` is what an outer circuit breaker\nwould consult, using the typed errors instead of message text. A second block then shows the one\nthing the adapters hide: the tool-call round trip on the wire in each shape.\n\n```ts\nimport Anthropic from \"@anthropic-ai/sdk\";\nimport OpenAI from \"openai\";\n\nexport interface Completion { text: string; inputTokens: number; outputTokens: number; requestId?: string }\nexport interface ModelClient { complete(system: string, user: string, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<Completion> }\n\nexport function anthropicClient(model = \"claude-opus-5-5\"): ModelClient {\n  const client = new Anthropic({ maxRetries: 3, timeout: 60_000 });      // ms; retries 408/409/429/5xx\n  return {\n    async complete(system, user, signal) {\n      const stream = client.messages.stream(\n        { model, max_tokens: 2048, system, messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: user }] },\n        { signal },\n      );\n      stream.on(\"text\", (delta) => process.stdout.write(delta));\n      const msg = await stream.finalMessage();                             // typed Message, after the stream\n      const text = msg.content.flatMap((b) => (b.type === \"text\" ? [b.text] : [])).join(\"\");\n      return { text, inputTokens: msg.usage.input_tokens, outputTokens: msg.usage.output_tokens,\n               requestId: stream.request_id ?? undefined };                // from the response header\n    },\n  };\n}\n\nexport function openaiClient(model = process.env.OPENAI_MODEL ?? \"set-a-current-model-id\"): ModelClient {\n  const client = new OpenAI({ maxRetries: 3, timeout: 60_000 });\n  return {\n    async complete(system, user, signal) {\n      const stream = await client.chat.completions.create(\n        { model, stream: true, stream_options: { include_usage: true },\n          messages: [{ role: \"system\", content: system }, { role: \"user\", content: user }] },\n        { signal },\n      );\n      let text = \"\", usage: OpenAI.CompletionUsage | undefined;\n      for await (const chunk of stream) {\n        const delta = chunk.choices[0]?.delta?.content ?? \"\";\n        process.stdout.write(delta); text += delta;\n        if (chunk.usage) usage = chunk.usage;                               // arrives in the final chunk\n      }\n      return { text, inputTokens: usage?.prompt_tokens ?? 0, outputTokens: usage?.completion_tokens ?? 0 };\n    },\n  };\n}\n\nexport function isRetryable(err: unknown): boolean {      // abort and 4xx errors are not retryable\n  if (err instanceof Anthropic.APIConnectionError || err instanceof OpenAI.APIConnectionError) return true;\n  const e = err instanceof Anthropic.APIError || err instanceof OpenAI.APIError ? err : undefined;\n  return e !== undefined && (e.status === 429 || (e.status ?? 0) >= 500);\n}\n```\n\n```jsonc\n// OpenAI Chat Completions: the model's turn, then yours. arguments is a STRING.\n{ \"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": null, \"tool_calls\": [{ \"id\": \"call_1\", \"type\": \"function\",\n    \"function\": { \"name\": \"lookup_order\", \"arguments\": \"{\\\"orderId\\\":\\\"ord_42\\\"}\" } }] }\n{ \"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": \"call_1\", \"content\": \"{\\\"status\\\":\\\"shipped\\\"}\" }\n\n// Anthropic Messages: content is a list of blocks; input is an OBJECT; the result rides in a user turn.\n{ \"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": [\n    { \"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Let me check that order.\" },\n    { \"type\": \"tool_use\", \"id\": \"toolu_1\", \"name\": \"lookup_order\", \"input\": { \"orderId\": \"ord_42\" } }] }\n{ \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [\n    { \"type\": \"tool_result\", \"tool_use_id\": \"toolu_1\", \"content\": \"{\\\"status\\\":\\\"shipped\\\"}\" }] }\n```\n\nEverything a tool-calling adapter does follows from these four objects: parse or do not parse the\narguments, echo the assistant turn back unchanged, and key the result by the call's id. If the model\nmade two calls, OpenAI expects two `tool` messages and Anthropic expects both `tool_result` blocks\nin the same single user message.","tradeoffs":["Built-in retries make transient failures invisible, which is what you want until you wrap the call in your own retry or a queue with redelivery; then attempts multiply and you should set `maxRetries: 0` in one of the layers.","Generated types track the API exactly, so upgrades surface breaking changes at compile time; the flip side is that a minor SDK bump can rename a type and force a same-day fix across the codebase.","The streaming helpers accumulate tool arguments and expose the final message for free, but they buffer the whole response in memory; for very long outputs you may prefer the raw event iterable.","Explicit cache breakpoints (Anthropic) give control and a verifiable hit counter at the cost of discipline about prompt ordering; automatic caching (OpenAI) needs no annotations but gives less control over what is cached.","Using a provider's Zod helper couples your tool definitions to that SDK; a shared schema module converted per provider keeps the option of a second provider open."],"pitfalls":["Matching on `error.message` strings instead of `instanceof` and `status`; the symptom is retry logic that stops working after an SDK or API wording change.","Passing the timeout in seconds as in Python; the symptom is every request timing out after a few milliseconds and being retried twice (three attempts) before failing.","Forgetting the `signal` on streaming calls; the symptom is completions still running, and billing, long after the HTTP client disconnected.","Treating `function.arguments` as an object on OpenAI, or `input` as a string on Anthropic; the symptom is a `TypeError` or `[object Object]` inside a tool.","Putting text before the `tool_result` blocks, sending them in the wrong role, or letting another turn come between the `tool_use` and its result; the symptom is a 400 saying `tool_use` ids were found without `tool_result` blocks immediately after. Splitting parallel results across messages is a quieter mistake that discourages the model from parallel tool calls.","Reading `response.content[0].text` on Anthropic without narrowing; the symptom is a compile error at best, and at worst a runtime `undefined` when the first block is `tool_use` or `thinking`.","Omitting `max_tokens` on Anthropic or setting it too low; the symptom is a 400 on the former and truncated JSON with `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` on the latter.","Sending a non-streaming request with a very large `max_tokens` through a proxy with a short idle timeout; the symptom is 504s or `APIConnectionTimeoutError` on long answers only, followed by retries that may pay for the same long generation again. Stream long generations.","Verifying caching by vibes; the symptom is a system prompt with a timestamp in it, a cache hit rate of zero and a bill that never drops."],"when":"Use the native SDK whenever you commit to a provider and want its full surface: streaming helpers,\ntool runners, batch and files endpoints, explicit caching, platform clients for Azure, Bedrock or\nVertex. Wrap it behind a narrow interface of your own so tests can inject a fake and a second provider\ncan be added later, and keep the SDK's retries while deciding explicitly where your own retries, queue\nredelivery and circuit breaker sit. Reach for a unification layer such as the Vercel AI SDK when\nmulti-provider or browser streaming is the primary requirement. Measure tokens and cost per request\nfrom `usage`, p95 time to first token, retry counts, and cache hit rate. Interviews probe the tool-call\nround trip in both shapes, what the SDK retries and what it does not, and how a cancelled request\nactually stops.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures clients with explicit timeouts and retries, writes adapters that normalise usage and errors, narrows content blocks correctly, and propagates `AbortSignal` into every call.","architect":"Decides the provider boundary (native SDK behind an interface versus a unification layer), where retries and breakers live across layers, and how cost telemetry is normalised.","fde":"Reads a customer's integration for the classic faults (string-matched errors, missing signals, doubled retries, unverified caching) and converts it to typed, cancellable, observable calls."},"check":{"q":"A service wraps `client.messages.create` in its own retry loop of three attempts with backoff,\nand the SDK keeps its default retries. Under a provider outage, how many requests can one user action\ngenerate, how long can it take, and what would you change?","a":"The SDK retries twice by default, so each outer attempt can send three requests; three outer\nattempts give up to nine requests, each waiting up to the SDK timeout plus backoff, so one click can\nhold a connection for many minutes and amplify load on an already failing provider. The fix is to pick\none layer. Either set `maxRetries: 0` and own retries, jitter and a circuit breaker in your client\nwrapper, or keep the SDK's retries and remove the outer loop, treating a thrown `RateLimitError` or\n`InternalServerError` as a signal to shed load or fail fast. Whichever you choose, set the handler and\nproxy timeouts from `timeout × (retries + 1)`, pass an `AbortSignal` so a disconnected user stops the\nchain, and record the request ID from the response for the inevitable support ticket."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the key structural difference between a tool call in OpenAI Chat Completions and in Anthropic Messages?","options":["OpenAI requires a JSON schema while Anthropic infers parameters from the description","OpenAI returns `tool_calls` with `arguments` as a JSON string and takes results as `role: \"tool\"` messages; Anthropic returns `tool_use` content blocks with a parsed `input` and takes results as `tool_result` blocks in a user message","Anthropic executes tools server-side, so no result needs to be returned","OpenAI allows only one tool call per response while Anthropic allows many"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Both require a JSON schema and both can return several calls in one turn. The difference is the envelope: a string to parse and a `tool` role on one side, a parsed object inside a content block and a `tool_result` block on the other. Neither executes your custom tool for you."},{"q":"The TypeScript SDKs take `timeout: 30`. What happens, and why?","options":["Requests time out after 30 seconds, matching the Python SDK","Requests time out after 30 milliseconds and are retried `maxRetries` times, because the TypeScript SDKs take milliseconds","The value is ignored because timeouts are set per request only","The SDK throws at construction because the minimum timeout is one second"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Unit mismatch is a common port-from-Python bug. The TypeScript clients take milliseconds, and because timeouts count as retryable, the 30 ms failure repeats up to the retry limit before the typed `APIConnectionTimeoutError` surfaces."},{"q":"Why should error handling use `instanceof OpenAI.RateLimitError` rather than inspecting `error.message`?","options":["Because `error.message` is always empty in the SDKs","Because `instanceof` is faster than string comparison","Because the typed classes map to HTTP status and error type stably, while message text can change with any SDK or API release","Because `error.message` contains the API key and must not be read"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The SDKs expose a class per status (and a `status` field) precisely so callers can branch on a stable contract. Message wording is informational and may change; code that depends on it breaks silently when it does."}]},{"key":"node-ts-for-ai/mcp-typescript-sdk","sources":["Official docs: Model Context Protocol specification","Official docs: MCP TypeScript SDK (@modelcontextprotocol/sdk)","Official docs: Zod"],"why":"MCP is a wire protocol: JSON-RPC 2.0 messages with an `initialize` handshake, capability negotiation,\na fixed set of request methods and notifications, and two transport bindings with their own framing\nand session rules. Implementing that by hand for one server is a week of work and a long tail of\ninteroperability bugs: a missing `initialized` notification, a tool whose JSON Schema the host rejects,\nan HTTP endpoint that does not honour the session header, a stdout log line that corrupts the stream.\nEvery server author would repeat the work and every host would discover a different subset of\nmistakes. The SDK exists so that a server author writes handlers and schemas, and a host author writes\n`connect`, `listTools` and `callTool`, while the protocol machinery, versioning and transport details\nare shared code maintained alongside the specification.\n\nThe naive alternatives both lose. Writing a bespoke HTTP API for your tools and asking each agent to\nintegrate it reintroduces the per-framework connector problem MCP was created to remove, and it offers\nnothing for resources, prompts or server-initiated requests. Using a framework's proprietary tool\nformat works inside that framework only. The SDK's design choice, schemas as Zod values that become\nJSON Schema on the wire and TypeScript types in the handler, is the same single-source-of-truth idea\nas the schema-validation lesson, applied at a protocol boundary: the model sees the schema the\nhandler enforces, and the host can validate what it sends.","how":"### What happens on connect\nEvery session, on either transport, starts with the same three messages, and the SDK sends and\nchecks all of them:\n1. The client sends `initialize` with the newest protocol version it supports, its own\n   capabilities (for example `sampling`, `roots`, `elicitation`) and its name and version.\n2. The server answers with the version it will speak (the client's if it supports it, otherwise\n   one it does; a client that cannot speak that version disconnects) and its capabilities (`tools`,\n   `resources`, `prompts`, `logging`, each possibly with `listChanged`).\n3. The client sends the `notifications/initialized` notification. Only then do ordinary requests\n   such as `tools/list` flow.\n\nNegotiation exists *because* hosts and servers upgrade independently: each side may only use\nfeatures the other advertised, so an old host and a new server still interoperate on the common\nsubset. After the handshake, every request carries a JSON-RPC `id` and gets exactly one response;\nnotifications (progress, log messages, `notifications/cancelled`, list-changed) carry none. The\nclient side of the SDK also enforces a per-request timeout (60 seconds by default in 1.x) and\nrejects with a request-timeout `McpError` when it expires, which matters for slow tools.\n\n### Server side: two levels\n`McpServer` (from `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js`) is the high-level API. You construct it\nwith a name and version, then register capabilities: `registerTool(name, { description, inputSchema,\noutputSchema? }, handler)`, `registerResource(name, uriOrTemplate, metadata, reader)` and\n`registerPrompt(name, { argsSchema }, builder)`. `inputSchema` is a Zod shape (recent 1.x releases\nalso accept a full Zod object schema); the SDK converts it to JSON Schema for `tools/list` and\nvalidates incoming `tools/call` arguments against it before your handler runs, so the handler\nreceives a typed object. A handler returns `{ content: [...] }` where content items are `text`,\n`image`, `audio`, resource links or embedded resources, optionally `structuredContent` (required, and\nvalidated, when an `outputSchema` is declared), and optionally `isError: true`.\n\nThat flag reflects the spec's split between two kinds of failure. A *tool execution* error (order not\nfound, a business rule violated, an upstream API down) is a normal result with `isError: true`, so\nthe model reads the message and can adapt. A *protocol* error (unknown method, malformed request) is a\nJSON-RPC error aimed at the host, which typically treats it as a broken call rather than something to\nshow the model. In `McpServer` a handler that throws is caught and converted into an `isError` result\nwhose text is the raw exception message, so throwing \"works\", but you lose control of what the model\nreads and may leak stack-level detail into its context. Return `isError` results deliberately, with a\nmessage written for the model. With the low-level `Server`, an exception in your request handler does\nbecome a JSON-RPC error, which is exactly the conflation to avoid: the host then treats a routine \"not\nfound\" as an outage and the model never learns why the call failed.\n\n`Server` (the low level) exposes `setRequestHandler(RequestSchema, handler)` for every method in the\nspec and is what `McpServer` is built on; drop to it for custom capabilities, fully dynamic tool lists\ncomputed per request, or when you need to intercept raw requests. (`McpServer` already sends\n`tools/list_changed` when you enable, disable or remove a registered tool.) Both levels implement\nthe handshake, capability advertisement, progress and logging notifications and cancellation for you.\n\n### Transports\nA transport moves JSON-RPC messages; the server object is transport-agnostic. `StdioServerTransport`\nreads requests from `stdin` and writes responses to `stdout`, which is why a stdio server must never\n`console.log`: anything on stdout that is not a JSON-RPC message corrupts the stream. Log to `stderr`\nor via the protocol's logging notification. Stdio is the right choice for local tools launched by an\nIDE or desktop host, *because* the host owns the process, its environment and its lifetime, and no\nnetwork, authentication or multi-tenancy question arises.\n\n`StreamableHTTPServerTransport` serves remote clients from a single endpoint, conventionally `/mcp`:\nthe client POSTs JSON-RPC requests, the server replies either with a JSON body or with a server-sent\nevent stream for responses that take time or carry notifications, and an optional GET opens a\nlong-lived stream for server-initiated messages. Sessions are identified by an `Mcp-Session-Id`\nheader that the server issues during `initialize`; the SDK supports stateful mode (a\n`sessionIdGenerator`, one transport instance per session, kept in a map) and stateless mode (no\nsession id, every request self-contained). Stateless is what you want behind a load balancer with\nseveral replicas, since a stateful session pins a client to the replica holding its transport; the\ncost is that server-initiated requests and resumable streams are unavailable. The older HTTP+SSE\ntransport is still shipped for compatibility but is superseded. Over HTTP the specification also\nrequires servers to validate the `Origin` header, *because* a server listening on localhost is\notherwise reachable from any web page the user opens (DNS rebinding); bind local servers to\n`127.0.0.1` and turn on the SDK transport's host and origin checks. Remote servers authenticate with\nOAuth 2.1 per the specification's authorization section; the SDK ships helpers for both sides, and the\ndedicated security lesson covers the trust decisions.\n\n### Client side\n`Client` (from `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/index.js`) is constructed with a name and version\nand connected to a transport: `StdioClientTransport({ command, args, env })` spawns the server as a\nchild process, `StreamableHTTPClientTransport(url)` talks to a remote one. `connect` performs the\nhandshake and records the server's capabilities. From there `listTools()`, `callTool({ name,\narguments })`, `listResources()`, `readResource({ uri })`, `listPrompts()` and `getPrompt()` map\none-to-one onto protocol methods and return typed results. The client is also where a host handles\nserver-initiated requests: sampling (the server asks the host's model for a completion), roots (the\nhost tells the server which directories it may touch) and elicitation (the server asks the user for\ninput), each opt-in through capability declarations.\n\n### Bridging to a model\nNothing in the SDK calls a language model. The host does the mapping: each tool from `listTools()`\nbecomes a provider tool definition (`inputSchema` is already JSON Schema, so it drops into Anthropic's\n`input_schema` or OpenAI's `parameters`); a `tool_use` or `tool_calls` entry in the model's reply\nbecomes `callTool`; the returned `content` becomes the tool result sent back. Name collisions across\nservers are the host's problem, usually solved with a server prefix. Anthropic's API additionally\noffers a server-side MCP connector that performs this bridging for remote servers inside the request,\nwhich removes the loop from your code but also removes per-call inspection and approval (you keep\nonly the static tool allow-list the connector configuration offers), so it suits trusted servers and\nsimple hosts. Keep the exposed tool set small: a host that forwards forty\ntools from three servers pays in tokens and in tool-selection accuracy on every turn.","example":"A stdio server exposing one tool with a Zod-typed input, and a host that discovers the tool, hands it\nto Claude, and routes the resulting `tool_use` back through `callTool`. The server logs to stderr\nbecause stdout is the wire. Written against the 1.x `registerTool` API; older minors used `tool()`,\nand the maintainers have been preparing a v2 line that splits client and server into separate packages, so import paths depend on\nwhich line you install. The host completes the round trip: MCP `text` items map directly onto\nAnthropic text blocks, while images would need re-encoding into Anthropic's `source` shape.\n\n```ts\n// server.ts — one tool over stdio. Nothing but JSON-RPC may touch stdout.\nimport { McpServer } from \"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js\";\nimport { StdioServerTransport } from \"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/stdio.js\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { db } from \"./db\";\n\nconst server = new McpServer({ name: \"orders\", version: \"1.0.0\" });\nserver.registerTool(\n  \"lookup_order\",\n  { description: \"Fetch an order by id\", inputSchema: { orderId: z.string().regex(/^ord_\\w+$/) } },\n  async ({ orderId }) => {                                    // typed from the Zod shape, already validated\n    const order = await db.orders.find(orderId);\n    if (!order) return { isError: true, content: [{ type: \"text\", text: `no order ${orderId}` }] };\n    return { content: [{ type: \"text\", text: JSON.stringify(order) }] };\n  },\n);\nconsole.error(\"orders server starting\");                      // stderr, never console.log\nawait server.connect(new StdioServerTransport());\n\n// host.ts — discover tools, give them to the model, route tool_use back to the server.\nimport { Client } from \"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/index.js\";\nimport { StdioClientTransport } from \"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/stdio.js\";\nimport Anthropic from \"@anthropic-ai/sdk\";\n\nconst mcp = new Client({ name: \"host\", version: \"1.0.0\" });\nawait mcp.connect(new StdioClientTransport({ command: \"node\", args: [\"dist/server.js\"] }));\nconst { tools } = await mcp.listTools();\nconst claudeTools: Anthropic.Tool[] = tools.map((t) => ({\n  name: t.name, description: t.description ?? \"\", input_schema: t.inputSchema as Anthropic.Tool.InputSchema,\n}));\nconst anthropic = new Anthropic();\nconst ask = (messages: Anthropic.MessageParam[]) =>\n  anthropic.messages.create({ model: \"claude-opus-5-5\", max_tokens: 1024, tools: claudeTools, messages });\nconst messages: Anthropic.MessageParam[] = [{ role: \"user\", content: \"What is the status of ord_42?\" }];\nconst reply = await ask(messages);\nconst results: Anthropic.ToolResultBlockParam[] = [];\nfor (const block of reply.content) {\n  if (block.type !== \"tool_use\") continue;\n  const r = await mcp.callTool({ name: block.name, arguments: block.input as Record<string, unknown> });\n  const text = (r.content as { type: string; text?: string }[])        // keep text items only here\n    .flatMap((c) => (c.type === \"text\" ? [{ type: \"text\" as const, text: c.text ?? \"\" }] : []));\n  results.push({ type: \"tool_result\", tool_use_id: block.id, is_error: r.isError === true, content: text });\n}\nmessages.push({ role: \"assistant\", content: reply.content }, { role: \"user\", content: results });\nconst final = await ask(messages);                 // the model now answers from the tool result\n```","tradeoffs":["The SDK handles handshake, schemas and transports so a server is a few dozen lines, but you inherit its release cadence; the high-level API has been renamed between minors (`tool()` to `registerTool`) and error-handling behaviour has shifted too, so pin versions and read release notes on upgrade.","Stdio is simple and secure by construction, yet it ties the server's lifetime to one host process; the moment two hosts or a remote user need the same server, Streamable HTTP and auth are required.","Stateful HTTP sessions enable server-initiated requests and stream resumption, while stateless mode scales horizontally with no affinity; choose stateless unless you actually use sampling or elicitation.","Returning `isError` results keeps the model in the loop on failures, but also lets a server leak internal detail into model context, and an uncaught throw in `McpServer` does so automatically with the raw exception message; format tool errors for the model, not from stack traces.","A server-side MCP connector in the model API removes the bridging loop, at the cost of losing the host's ability to filter, approve or rate-limit calls; use it for trusted servers only.","Exposing many tools through MCP is cheap to build and expensive per turn in tokens and selection accuracy; curate per host rather than forwarding everything."],"pitfalls":["A `console.log` in a stdio server; the symptom is the host reporting a JSON parse error or hanging at `initialize` while the server appears to run fine.","Throwing from a tool handler for expected failures; with `McpServer` the symptom is the model reading raw exception text (a driver error, an internal hostname) instead of a useful message, and with the low-level `Server` it is the agent loop aborting on a protocol error instead of the model reading \"not found\" and trying something else.","Deploying a stateful Streamable HTTP server behind a round-robin balancer; the symptom is intermittent \"session not found\" errors after the first request.","Registering tools with vague descriptions or untyped `z.any()` inputs; the symptom is the model calling the wrong tool or passing arguments the handler cannot use.","Forgetting to prefix tool names when a host connects to several servers; the symptom is one server's `search` silently shadowing another's.","Building a public HTTP server with no authentication because the local stdio version needed none; the symptom is anyone with the URL calling your tools.","Calling a tool that runs longer than the client's default request timeout; the symptom is a request-timeout `McpError` at about a minute while the server keeps working. Pass a longer `timeout` in the request options, or have the server send progress notifications and set `resetTimeoutOnProgress`.","Leaving the child process running when the host exits; the symptom is orphaned server processes accumulating on developer machines."],"when":"Write an MCP server when a system should be usable by more than one host or agent framework, when a\ncustomer or vendor already speaks MCP, or when you want tools, resources and prompts described once in\na typed way. Use stdio for local developer tooling and anything launched by an IDE; use Streamable\nHTTP, stateless where possible, with OAuth, for shared or remote servers. Write a client when your own\nagent must consume third-party servers. Do not reach for MCP for a single in-process tool used by one\nservice; a plain function with a Zod schema is less code. Measure the number of tools exposed per\nhost, tool-call error rate split by `isError` versus protocol error, and p95 call latency. Interviews\nask what the SDK does and does not do (it never calls a model), the tool-error-versus-protocol-error\ndistinction, and why stdout is sacred.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Registers tools with Zod shapes and useful descriptions, returns `isError` results for expected failures, picks the transport, and writes the host loop that bridges tools to the model.","architect":"Decides which systems get MCP servers, stateful versus stateless HTTP, how servers are authenticated and versioned, and which hosts may see which tools.","fde":"Wraps a customer's internal API as an MCP server so their existing hosts can use it, and audits the servers they already run for stdout logging, missing auth and oversized tool sets."},"check":{"q":"A team ported their stdio MCP server to Streamable HTTP, deployed three replicas behind a load\nbalancer, and now see intermittent failures after the first request. What is wrong and what are their\noptions?","a":"The server runs in stateful mode: `initialize` creates a session on one replica and returns an\n`Mcp-Session-Id`, and the next request carrying that id lands on a different replica that has no such\ntransport, so it fails. Options: switch to stateless mode (no session id generator), which makes each\nrequest self-contained and scales freely at the cost of server-initiated requests and resumable\nstreams; keep stateful mode and enable session affinity at the balancer, accepting uneven load and\nsession loss on replica restarts; or externalise session state so any replica can serve it, which the\nSDK does not do for you and is rarely worth it. Most tool servers use neither sampling nor\nelicitation, so stateless is the usual answer, together with OAuth now that the server is reachable."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must an MCP server using the stdio transport never write logs with `console.log`?","options":["Because the SDK disables `console` for performance reasons","Because stdout is the JSON-RPC channel to the host, so any non-protocol output corrupts the message stream","Because logs would be visible to the model and leak information","Because stdio servers run without a TTY and `console.log` throws"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The transport frames messages on stdout; a stray line breaks parsing on the host side, often showing up as a hang during `initialize`. Logs go to stderr or to the protocol's logging notifications. The model never sees server logs unless a tool returns them."},{"q":"A tool handler cannot find the requested record. What should it do?","options":["Throw an error and let the SDK report the failure to the host","Return an empty `content` array and let the model infer the failure","Return a normal result with `isError: true` and a message, so the model sees why the call failed and can adapt","Call `process.exit(1)` to signal the failure unambiguously"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`isError` marks a tool-level failure the model is expected to handle, with a message you control. Throwing is the tempting option but is wrong either way: `McpServer` catches it and forwards the raw exception text as an `isError` result, while a low-level `Server` turns it into a JSON-RPC error that typically aborts the agent step. An empty result hides the cause. Exiting kills the server for every other call."},{"q":"What does the MCP TypeScript SDK not do for a host application?","options":["Perform the `initialize` handshake and capability negotiation","Convert a server's Zod schemas into JSON Schema for `tools/list`","Call a language model and decide which tool to invoke","Spawn a stdio server as a child process and talk to it"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The SDK implements the protocol on both sides and manages transports. Bridging to a model, mapping tool definitions into the provider's format, sending the model's tool call to `callTool` and returning the result, is the host's code, or a provider-side connector if one is used."}]}]});
